\id XXA purgatorio
\usfm 3.1
\ide UTF-8
\rem anagnosis work=purgatorio edition=longfellow.en lang=en
\rem source Project Gutenberg 1004, https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1004
\rem rights Public domain (Longfellow, 1867)
\rem note Who speaks each speech (zsay) is an editorial annotation, not Longfellow's.
\h Purgatorio
\toc1 Purgatorio
\mt1 Purgatorio
\c 1
\cp 1
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* To run o’er better waters hoists its sail
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* The little vessel of my genius now,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* That leaves behind itself a sea so cruel;
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* And of that second kingdom will I sing
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* Wherein the human spirit doth purge itself,
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* And to ascend to heaven becometh worthy.
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* But let dead Poesy here rise again,
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* O holy Muses, since that I am yours,
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* And here Calliope somewhat ascend,
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* My song accompanying with that sound,
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* Of which the miserable magpies felt
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* The blow so great, that they despaired of pardon.
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* Sweet colour of the oriental sapphire,
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* That was upgathered in the cloudless aspect
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* Of the pure air, as far as the first circle,
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* Unto mine eyes did recommence delight
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* Soon as I issued forth from the dead air,
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* Which had with sadness filled mine eyes and breast.
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* The beauteous planet, that to love incites,
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* Was making all the orient to laugh,
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* Veiling the Fishes that were in her escort.
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* To the right hand I turned, and fixed my mind
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* Upon the other pole, and saw four stars
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* Ne’er seen before save by the primal people.
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* Rejoicing in their flamelets seemed the heaven.
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* O thou septentrional and widowed site,
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* Because thou art deprived of seeing these!
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* When from regarding them I had withdrawn,
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* Turning a little to the other pole,
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* There where the Wain had disappeared already,
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* I saw beside me an old man alone,
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* Worthy of so much reverence in his look,
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* That more owes not to father any son.
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* A long beard and with white hair intermingled
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* He wore, in semblance like unto the tresses,
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* Of which a double list fell on his breast.
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* The rays of the four consecrated stars
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* Did so adorn his countenance with light,
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* That him I saw as were the sun before him.
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cato"\*“Who are you? ye who, counter the blind river,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cato"\*Have fled away from the eternal prison?”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* Moving those venerable plumes, he said:
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cato"\*“Who guided you? or who has been your lamp\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cato"\*In issuing forth out of the night profound,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cato"\*That ever black makes the infernal valley?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cato"\*The laws of the abyss, are they thus broken?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cato"\*Or is there changed in heaven some council new,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cato"\*That being damned ye come unto my crags?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* Then did my Leader lay his grasp upon me,
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* And with his words, and with his hands and signs,
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* Reverent he made in me my knees and brow;
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* Then answered him: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“I came not of myself;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*A Lady from Heaven descended, at whose prayers\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*I aided this one with my company.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But since it is thy will more be unfolded\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Of our condition, how it truly is,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Mine cannot be that this should be denied thee.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*This one has never his last evening seen,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But by his folly was so near to it\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That very little time was there to turn.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*As I have said, I unto him was sent\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*To rescue him, and other way was none\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Than this to which I have myself betaken.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*I’ve shown him all the people of perdition,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And now those spirits I intend to show\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Who purge themselves beneath thy guardianship.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*How I have brought him would be long to tell thee.\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Virtue descendeth from on high that aids me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*To lead him to behold thee and to hear thee.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Now may it please thee to vouchsafe his coming;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*He seeketh Liberty, which is so dear,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*As knoweth he who life for her refuses.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thou know’st it; since, for her, to thee not bitter\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Was death in Utica, where thou didst leave\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The vesture, that will shine so, the great day.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*By us the eternal edicts are not broken;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Since this one lives, and Minos binds not me;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But of that circle I, where are the chaste\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Eyes of thy Marcia, who in looks still prays thee,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*O holy breast, to hold her as thine own;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*For her love, then, incline thyself to us.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Permit us through thy sevenfold realm to go;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*I will take back this grace from thee to her,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*If to be mentioned there below thou deignest.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cato"\*“Marcia so pleasing was unto mine eyes\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cato"\*While I was on the other side,”\zsay-e\* then said he,
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cato"\*“That every grace she wished of me I granted;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cato"\*Now that she dwells beyond the evil river,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cato"\*She can no longer move me, by that law\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cato"\*Which, when I issued forth from there, was made.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cato"\*But if a Lady of Heaven do move and rule thee,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cato"\*As thou dost say, no flattery is needful;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cato"\*Let it suffice thee that for her thou ask me.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cato"\*Go, then, and see thou gird this one about\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cato"\*With a smooth rush, and that thou wash his face,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cato"\*So that thou cleanse away all stain therefrom,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cato"\*For ’twere not fitting that the eye o’ercast\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cato"\*By any mist should go before the first\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cato"\*Angel, who is of those of Paradise.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cato"\*This little island round about its base\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cato"\*Below there, yonder, where the billow beats it,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cato"\*Doth rushes bear upon its washy ooze;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cato"\*No other plant that putteth forth the leaf,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cato"\*Or that doth indurate, can there have life,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cato"\*Because it yieldeth not unto the shocks.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cato"\*Thereafter be not this way your return;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cato"\*The sun, which now is rising, will direct you\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cato"\*To take the mount by easier ascent.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* With this he vanished; and I raised me up
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* Without a word, and wholly drew myself
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* Unto my Guide, and turned mine eyes to him.
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* And he began: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Son, follow thou my steps;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Let us turn back, for on this side declines\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The plain unto its lower boundaries.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* The dawn was vanquishing the matin hour
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* Which fled before it, so that from afar
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* I recognised the trembling of the sea.
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* Along the solitary plain we went
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* As one who unto the lost road returns,
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* And till he finds it seems to go in vain.
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* As soon as we were come to where the dew
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* Fights with the sun, and, being in a part
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* Where shadow falls, little evaporates,
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* Both of his hands upon the grass outspread
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* In gentle manner did my Master place;
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* Whence I, who of his action was aware,
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* Extended unto him my tearful cheeks;
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* There did he make in me uncovered wholly
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* That hue which Hell had covered up in me.
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* Then came we down upon the desert shore
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* Which never yet saw navigate its waters
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* Any that afterward had known return.
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* There he begirt me as the other pleased;
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* O marvellous! for even as he culled
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* The humble plant, such it sprang up again
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* Suddenly there where he uprooted it.
\c 2
\cp 2
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* Already had the sun the horizon reached
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Whose circle of meridian covers o’er
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* Jerusalem with its most lofty point,
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* And night that opposite to him revolves
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* Was issuing forth from Ganges with the Scales
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* That fall from out her hand when she exceedeth;
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* So that the white and the vermilion cheeks
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Of beautiful Aurora, where I was,
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* By too great age were changing into orange.
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* We still were on the border of the sea,
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* Like people who are thinking of their road,
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* Who go in heart and with the body stay;
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* And lo! as when, upon the approach of morning,
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* Through the gross vapours Mars grows fiery red
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* Down in the West upon the ocean floor,
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* Appeared to me—may I again behold it!—
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* A light along the sea so swiftly coming,
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* Its motion by no flight of wing is equalled;
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* From which when I a little had withdrawn
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* Mine eyes, that I might question my Conductor,
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* Again I saw it brighter grown and larger.
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* Then on each side of it appeared to me
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* I knew not what of white, and underneath it
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* Little by little there came forth another.
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* My Master yet had uttered not a word
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* While the first whiteness into wings unfolded;
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* But when he clearly recognised the pilot,
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* He cried: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Make haste, make haste to bow the knee!\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Behold the Angel of God! fold thou thy hands!\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Henceforward shalt thou see such officers!\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*See how he scorneth human arguments,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*So that nor oar he wants, nor other sail\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Than his own wings, between so distant shores.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*See how he holds them pointed up to heaven,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Fanning the air with the eternal pinions,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That do not moult themselves like mortal hair!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* Then as still nearer and more near us came
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* The Bird Divine, more radiant he appeared,
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* So that near by the eye could not endure him,
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* But down I cast it; and he came to shore
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* With a small vessel, very swift and light,
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* So that the water swallowed naught thereof.
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* Upon the stern stood the Celestial Pilot;
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* Beatitude seemed written in his face,
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* And more than a hundred spirits sat within.
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*“In exitu Israel de Aegypto!”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* They chanted all together in one voice,
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* With whatso in that psalm is after written.
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* Then made he sign of holy rood upon them,
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* Whereat all cast themselves upon the shore,
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* And he departed swiftly as he came.
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* The throng which still remained there unfamiliar
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* Seemed with the place, all round about them gazing,
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* As one who in new matters makes essay.
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* On every side was darting forth the day.
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* The sun, who had with his resplendent shafts
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* From the mid-heaven chased forth the Capricorn,
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* When the new people lifted up their faces
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* Towards us, saying to us: \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*“If ye know,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*Show us the way to go unto the mountain.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* And answer made Virgilius: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Ye believe\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Perchance that we have knowledge of this place,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But we are strangers even as yourselves.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Just now we came, a little while before you,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Another way, which was so rough and steep,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That mounting will henceforth seem sport to us.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* The souls who had, from seeing me draw breath,
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* Become aware that I was still alive,
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* Pallid in their astonishment became;
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* And as to messenger who bears the olive
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* The people throng to listen to the news,
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* And no one shows himself afraid of crowding,
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* So at the sight of me stood motionless
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* Those fortunate spirits, all of them, as if
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* Oblivious to go and make them fair.
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* One from among them saw I coming forward,
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* As to embrace me, with such great affection,
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* That it incited me to do the like.
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* O empty shadows, save in aspect only!
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* Three times behind it did I clasp my hands,
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* As oft returned with them to my own breast!
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* I think with wonder I depicted me;
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* Whereat the shadow smiled and backward drew;
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* And I, pursuing it, pressed farther forward.
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* Gently it said that I should stay my steps;
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* Then knew I who it was, and I entreated
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* That it would stop awhile to speak with me.
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* It made reply to me: \zsay-s |who="Casella"\*“Even as I loved thee\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Casella"\*In mortal body, so I love thee free;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Casella"\*Therefore I stop; but wherefore goest thou?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“My own Casella! to return once more\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*There where I am, I make this journey,”\zsay-e\* said I;
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“But how from thee has so much time be taken?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* And he to me: \zsay-s |who="Casella"\*“No outrage has been done me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Casella"\*If he who takes both when and whom he pleases\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Casella"\*Has many times denied to me this passage,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Casella"\*For of a righteous will his own is made.\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Casella"\*He, sooth to say, for three months past has taken\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Casella"\*Whoever wished to enter with all peace;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Casella"\*Whence I, who now had turned unto that shore\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Casella"\*Where salt the waters of the Tiber grow,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Casella"\*Benignantly by him have been received.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Casella"\*Unto that outlet now his wing is pointed,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Casella"\*Because for evermore assemble there\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Casella"\*Those who tow’rds Acheron do not descend.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* And I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“If some new law take not from thee\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Memory or practice of the song of love,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Which used to quiet in me all my longings,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Thee may it please to comfort therewithal\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Somewhat this soul of mine, that with its body\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Hitherward coming is so much distressed.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Casella"\*“Love, that within my mind discourses with me,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* Forthwith began he so melodiously,
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* The melody within me still is sounding.
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* My Master, and myself, and all that people
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* Which with him were, appeared as satisfied
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* As if naught else might touch the mind of any.
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* We all of us were moveless and attentive
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* Unto his notes; and lo! the grave old man,
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* Exclaiming: \zsay-s |who="Cato"\*“What is this, ye laggard spirits?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cato"\*What negligence, what standing still is this?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cato"\*Run to the mountain to strip off the slough,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cato"\*That lets not God be manifest to you.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* Even as when, collecting grain or tares,
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* The doves, together at their pasture met,
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* Quiet, nor showing their accustomed pride,
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* If aught appear of which they are afraid,
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* Upon a sudden leave their food alone,
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* Because they are assailed by greater care;
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* So that fresh company did I behold
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* The song relinquish, and go tow’rds the hill,
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* As one who goes, and knows not whitherward;
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* Nor was our own departure less in haste.
\c 3
\cp 3
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* Inasmuch as the instantaneous flight
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Had scattered them asunder o’er the plain,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* Turned to the mountain whither reason spurs us,
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* I pressed me close unto my faithful comrade,
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* And how without him had I kept my course?
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* Who would have led me up along the mountain?
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* He seemed to me within himself remorseful;
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* O noble conscience, and without a stain,
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* How sharp a sting is trivial fault to thee!
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* After his feet had laid aside the haste
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* Which mars the dignity of every act,
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* My mind, that hitherto had been restrained,
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* Let loose its faculties as if delighted,
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* And I my sight directed to the hill
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* That highest tow’rds the heaven uplifts itself.
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* The sun, that in our rear was flaming red,
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* Was broken in front of me into the figure
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* Which had in me the stoppage of its rays;
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* Unto one side I turned me, with the fear
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* Of being left alone, when I beheld
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* Only in front of me the ground obscured.
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Why dost thou still mistrust?”\zsay-e\* my Comforter
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* Began to say to me turned wholly round;
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Dost thou not think me with thee, and that I guide thee?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*’Tis evening there already where is buried\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The body within which I cast a shadow;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*’Tis from Brundusium ta’en, and Naples has it.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Now if in front of me no shadow fall,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Marvel not at it more than at the heavens,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Because one ray impedeth not another\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*To suffer torments, both of cold and heat,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Bodies like this that Power provides, which wills\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That how it works be not unveiled to us.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Insane is he who hopeth that our reason\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Can traverse the illimitable way,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Which the one Substance in three Persons follows!\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Mortals, remain contented at the ‘Quia;’\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*For if ye had been able to see all,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*No need there were for Mary to give birth;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And ye have seen desiring without fruit,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Those whose desire would have been quieted,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Which evermore is given them for a grief.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*I speak of Aristotle and of Plato,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And many others;”\zsay-e\*—and here bowed his head,
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* And more he said not, and remained disturbed.
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* We came meanwhile unto the mountain’s foot;
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* There so precipitate we found the rock,
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* That nimble legs would there have been in vain.
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* ’Twixt Lerici and Turbia, the most desert,
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* The most secluded pathway is a stair
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* Easy and open, if compared with that.
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Who knoweth now upon which hand the hill\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Slopes down,”\zsay-e\* my Master said, his footsteps staying,
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“So that who goeth without wings may mount?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* And while he held his eyes upon the ground
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* Examining the nature of the path,
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* And I was looking up around the rock,
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* On the left hand appeared to me a throng
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* Of souls, that moved their feet in our direction,
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* And did not seem to move, they came so slowly.
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Lift up thine eyes,”\zsay-e\* I to the Master said;
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Behold, on this side, who will give us counsel,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*If thou of thine own self can have it not.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* Then he looked at me, and with frank expression
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* Replied: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Let us go there, for they come slowly,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And thou be steadfast in thy hope, sweet son.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* Still was that people as far off from us,
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* After a thousand steps of ours I say,
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* As a good thrower with his hand would reach,
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* When they all crowded unto the hard masses
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* Of the high bank, and motionless stood and close,
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* As he stands still to look who goes in doubt.
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“O happy dead!  O spirits elect already!”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* Virgilius made beginning, \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“by that peace\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Which I believe is waiting for you all,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Tell us upon what side the mountain slopes,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*So that the going up be possible,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*For to lose time irks him most who most knows.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* As sheep come issuing forth from out the fold
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* By ones and twos and threes, and the others stand
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* Timidly, holding down their eyes and nostrils,
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* And what the foremost does the others do,
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* Huddling themselves against her, if she stop,
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* Simple and quiet and the wherefore know not;
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* So moving to approach us thereupon
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* I saw the leader of that fortunate flock,
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* Modest in face and dignified in gait.
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* As soon as those in the advance saw broken
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* The light upon the ground at my right side,
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* So that from me the shadow reached the rock,
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* They stopped, and backward drew themselves somewhat;
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* And all the others, who came after them,
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* Not knowing why nor wherefore, did the same.
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Without your asking, I confess to you\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*This is a human body which you see,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Whereby the sunshine on the ground is cleft.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Marvel ye not thereat, but be persuaded\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That not without a power which comes from Heaven\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Doth he endeavour to surmount this wall.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* The Master thus; and said those worthy people:
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*“Return ye then, and enter in before us,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* Making a signal with the back o’ the hand
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* And one of them began: \zsay-s |who="Manfred"\*“Whoe’er thou art,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Manfred"\*Thus going turn thine eyes, consider well\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Manfred"\*If e’er thou saw me in the other world.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* I turned me tow’rds him, and looked at him closely;
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* Blond was he, beautiful, and of noble aspect,
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* But one of his eyebrows had a blow divided.
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* When with humility I had disclaimed
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* E’er having seen him, \zsay-s |who="Manfred"\*“Now behold!”\zsay-e\* he said,
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* And showed me high upon his breast a wound.
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* Then said he with a smile: \zsay-s |who="Manfred"\*“I am Manfredi,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Manfred"\*The grandson of the Empress Costanza;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Manfred"\*Therefore, when thou returnest, I beseech thee\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Manfred"\*Go to my daughter beautiful, the mother\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Manfred"\*Of Sicily’s honour and of Aragon’s,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Manfred"\*And the truth tell her, if aught else be told.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Manfred"\*After I had my body lacerated\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Manfred"\*By these two mortal stabs, I gave myself\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Manfred"\*Weeping to Him, who willingly doth pardon.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Manfred"\*Horrible my iniquities had been;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Manfred"\*But Infinite Goodness hath such ample arms,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Manfred"\*That it receives whatever turns to it.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Manfred"\*Had but Cosenza’s pastor, who in chase\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Manfred"\*Of me was sent by Clement at that time,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Manfred"\*In God read understandingly this page,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Manfred"\*The bones of my dead body still would be\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Manfred"\*At the bridge-head, near unto Benevento,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Manfred"\*Under the safeguard of the heavy cairn.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Manfred"\*Now the rain bathes and moveth them the wind,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Manfred"\*Beyond the realm, almost beside the Verde,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Manfred"\*Where he transported them with tapers quenched.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Manfred"\*By malison of theirs is not so lost\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Manfred"\*Eternal Love, that it cannot return,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Manfred"\*So long as hope has anything of green.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Manfred"\*True is it, who in contumacy dies\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Manfred"\*Of Holy Church, though penitent at last,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Manfred"\*Must wait upon the outside this bank\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* \zsay-s |who="Manfred"\*Thirty times told the time that he has been\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* \zsay-s |who="Manfred"\*In his presumption, unless such decree\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* \zsay-s |who="Manfred"\*Shorter by means of righteous prayers become.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* \zsay-s |who="Manfred"\*See now if thou hast power to make me happy,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* \zsay-s |who="Manfred"\*By making known unto my good Costanza\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* \zsay-s |who="Manfred"\*How thou hast seen me, and this ban beside,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* \zsay-s |who="Manfred"\*For those on earth can much advance us here.”\zsay-e\*
\c 4
\cp 4
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* Whenever by delight or else by pain,
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* That seizes any faculty of ours,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* Wholly to that the soul collects itself,
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* It seemeth that no other power it heeds;
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* And this against that error is which thinks
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* One soul above another kindles in us.
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* And hence, whenever aught is heard or seen
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Which keeps the soul intently bent upon it,
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* Time passes on, and we perceive it not,
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* Because one faculty is that which listens,
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* And other that which the soul keeps entire;
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* This is as if in bonds, and that is free.
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* Of this I had experience positive
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* In hearing and in gazing at that spirit;
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* For fifty full degrees uprisen was
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* The sun, and I had not perceived it, when
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* We came to where those souls with one accord
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* Cried out unto us: \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*“Here is what you ask.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* A greater opening ofttimes hedges up
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* With but a little forkful of his thorns
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* The villager, what time the grape imbrowns,
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* Than was the passage-way through which ascended
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* Only my Leader and myself behind him,
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* After that company departed from us.
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* One climbs Sanleo and descends in Noli,
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* And mounts the summit of Bismantova,
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* With feet alone; but here one needs must fly;
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* With the swift pinions and the plumes I say
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* Of great desire, conducted after him
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* Who gave me hope, and made a light for me.
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* We mounted upward through the rifted rock,
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* And on each side the border pressed upon us,
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* And feet and hands the ground beneath required.
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* When we were come upon the upper rim
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* Of the high bank, out on the open slope,
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“My Master,”\zsay-e\* said I, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“what way shall we take?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* And he to me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“No step of thine descend;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Still up the mount behind me win thy way,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Till some sage escort shall appear to us.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* The summit was so high it vanquished sight,
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* And the hillside precipitous far more
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* Than line from middle quadrant to the centre.
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* Spent with fatigue was I, when I began:
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“O my sweet Father! turn thee and behold\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*How I remain alone, unless thou stay!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“O son,”\zsay-e\* he said, \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“up yonder drag thyself,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* Pointing me to a terrace somewhat higher,
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* Which on that side encircles all the hill.
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* These words of his so spurred me on, that I
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* Strained every nerve, behind him scrambling up,
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* Until the circle was beneath my feet.
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* Thereon ourselves we seated both of us
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* Turned to the East, from which we had ascended,
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* For all men are delighted to look back.
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* To the low shores mine eyes I first directed,
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* Then to the sun uplifted them, and wondered
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* That on the left hand we were smitten by it.
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* The Poet well perceived that I was wholly
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* Bewildered at the chariot of the light,
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* Where ’twixt us and the Aquilon it entered.
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* Whereon he said to me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“If Castor and Pollux\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Were in the company of yonder mirror,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That up and down conducteth with its light,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thou wouldst behold the zodiac’s jagged wheel\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Revolving still more near unto the Bears,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Unless it swerved aside from its old track.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*How that may be wouldst thou have power to think,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Collected in thyself, imagine Zion\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Together with this mount on earth to stand,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*So that they both one sole horizon have,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And hemispheres diverse; whereby the road\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Which Phaeton, alas! knew not to drive,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thou’lt see how of necessity must pass\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*This on one side, when that upon the other,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*If thine intelligence right clearly heed.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Truly, my Master,”\zsay-e\* said I, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“never yet\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Saw I so clearly as I now discern,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*There where my wit appeared incompetent,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That the mid-circle of supernal motion,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Which in some art is the Equator called,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And aye remains between the Sun and Winter,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*For reason which thou sayest, departeth hence\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Tow’rds the Septentrion, what time the Hebrews\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Beheld it tow’rds the region of the heat.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*But, if it pleaseth thee, I fain would learn\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*How far we have to go; for the hill rises\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Higher than eyes of mine have power to rise.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* And he to me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“This mount is such, that ever\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*At the beginning down below ’tis tiresome,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And aye the more one climbs, the less it hurts.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Therefore, when it shall seem so pleasant to thee,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That going up shall be to thee as easy\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*As going down the current in a boat,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Then at this pathway’s ending thou wilt be;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*There to repose thy panting breath expect;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*No more I answer; and this I know for true.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* And as he finished uttering these words,
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* A voice close by us sounded: \zsay-s |who="Belacqua"\*“Peradventure\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Belacqua"\*Thou wilt have need of sitting down ere that.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* At sound thereof each one of us turned round,
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* And saw upon the left hand a great rock,
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* Which neither I nor he before had noticed.
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* Thither we drew; and there were persons there
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* Who in the shadow stood behind the rock,
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* As one through indolence is wont to stand.
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* And one of them, who seemed to me fatigued,
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* Was sitting down, and both his knees embraced,
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* Holding his face low down between them bowed.
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“O my sweet Lord,”\zsay-e\* I said, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“do turn thine eye\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*On him who shows himself more negligent\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Than even Sloth herself his sister were.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* Then he turned round to us, and he gave heed,
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* Just lifting up his eyes above his thigh,
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* And said: \zsay-s |who="Belacqua"\*“Now go thou up, for thou art valiant.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* Then knew I who he was; and the distress,
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* That still a little did my breathing quicken,
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* My going to him hindered not; and after
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* I came to him he hardly raised his head,
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* Saying: \zsay-s |who="Belacqua"\*“Hast thou seen clearly how the sun\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Belacqua"\*O’er thy left shoulder drives his chariot?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* His sluggish attitude and his curt words
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* A little unto laughter moved my lips;
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* Then I began: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Belacqua, I grieve not\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*For thee henceforth; but tell me, wherefore seated\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*In this place art thou?  Waitest thou an escort?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Or has thy usual habit seized upon thee?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* And he: \zsay-s |who="Belacqua"\*“O brother, what’s the use of climbing?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Belacqua"\*Since to my torment would not let me go\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Belacqua"\*The Angel of God, who sitteth at the gate.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Belacqua"\*First heaven must needs so long revolve me round\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Belacqua"\*Outside thereof, as in my life it did,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Belacqua"\*Since the good sighs I to the end postponed,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Belacqua"\*Unless, e’er that, some prayer may bring me aid\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Belacqua"\*Which rises from a heart that lives in grace;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Belacqua"\*What profit others that in heaven are heard not?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* Meanwhile the Poet was before me mounting,
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* And saying: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Come now; see the sun has touched\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Meridian, and from the shore the night\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Covers already with her foot Morocco.”\zsay-e\*
\c 5
\cp 5
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* I had already from those shades departed,
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* And followed in the footsteps of my Guide,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* When from behind, pointing his finger at me,
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* One shouted: \zsay-s |who="A soul"\*“See, it seems as if shone not\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* \zsay-s |who="A soul"\*The sunshine on the left of him below,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* \zsay-s |who="A soul"\*And like one living seems he to conduct him.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* Mine eyes I turned at utterance of these words,
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* And saw them watching with astonishment
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* But me, but me, and the light which was broken!
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Why doth thy mind so occupy itself,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* The Master said, \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“that thou thy pace dost slacken?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*What matters it to thee what here is whispered?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Come after me, and let the people talk;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Stand like a steadfast tower, that never wags\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Its top for all the blowing of the winds;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*For evermore the man in whom is springing\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thought upon thought, removes from him the mark,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Because the force of one the other weakens.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* What could I say in answer but \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“I come”\zsay-e\*?
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* I said it somewhat with that colour tinged
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* Which makes a man of pardon sometimes worthy.
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* Meanwhile along the mountain-side across
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* Came people in advance of us a little,
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* Singing the Miserere verse by verse.
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* When they became aware I gave no place
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* For passage of the sunshine through my body,
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* They changed their song into a long, hoarse \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*“Oh!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* And two of them, in form of messengers,
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* Ran forth to meet us, and demanded of us,
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Two souls"\*“Of your condition make us cognisant.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* And said my Master: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Ye can go your way\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And carry back again to those who sent you,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That this one’s body is of very flesh.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*If they stood still because they saw his shadow,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*As I suppose, enough is answered them;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Him let them honour, it may profit them.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* Vapours enkindled saw I ne’er so swiftly
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* At early nightfall cleave the air serene,
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* Nor, at the set of sun, the clouds of August,
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* But upward they returned in briefer time,
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* And, on arriving, with the others wheeled
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* Tow’rds us, like troops that run without a rein.
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“This folk that presses unto us is great,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And cometh to implore thee,”\zsay-e\* said the Poet;
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“So still go onward, and in going listen.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*“O soul that goest to beatitude\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*With the same members wherewith thou wast born,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* Shouting they came, \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*“a little stay thy steps,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*Look, if thou e’er hast any of us seen,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*So that o’er yonder thou bear news of him;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*Ah, why dost thou go on?  Ah, why not stay?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*Long since we all were slain by violence,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*And sinners even to the latest hour;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*Then did a light from heaven admonish us,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*So that, both penitent and pardoning, forth\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*From life we issued reconciled to God,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*Who with desire to see Him stirs our hearts.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* And I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Although I gaze into your faces,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*No one I recognize; but if may please you\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Aught I have power to do, ye well-born spirits,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Speak ye, and I will do it, by that peace\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Which, following the feet of such a Guide,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*From world to world makes itself sought by me.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* And one began: \zsay-s |who="Jacopo del Cassero"\*“Each one has confidence\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo del Cassero"\*In thy good offices without an oath,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo del Cassero"\*Unless the I cannot cut off the I will;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo del Cassero"\*Whence I, who speak alone before the others,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo del Cassero"\*Pray thee, if ever thou dost see the land\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo del Cassero"\*That ’twixt Romagna lies and that of Charles,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo del Cassero"\*Thou be so courteous to me of thy prayers\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo del Cassero"\*In Fano, that they pray for me devoutly,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo del Cassero"\*That I may purge away my grave offences.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo del Cassero"\*From thence was I; but the deep wounds, through which\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo del Cassero"\*Issued the blood wherein I had my seat,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo del Cassero"\*Were dealt me in bosom of the Antenori,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo del Cassero"\*There where I thought to be the most secure;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo del Cassero"\*’Twas he of Este had it done, who held me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo del Cassero"\*In hatred far beyond what justice willed.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo del Cassero"\*But if towards the Mira I had fled,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo del Cassero"\*When I was overtaken at Oriaco,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo del Cassero"\*I still should be o’er yonder where men breathe.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo del Cassero"\*I ran to the lagoon, and reeds and mire\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo del Cassero"\*Did so entangle me I fell, and saw there\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo del Cassero"\*A lake made from my veins upon the ground.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* Then said another: \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*“Ah, be that desire\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*Fulfilled that draws thee to the lofty mountain,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*As thou with pious pity aidest mine.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*I was of Montefeltro, and am Buonconte;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*Giovanna, nor none other cares for me;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*Hence among these I go with downcast front.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* And I to him: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“What violence or what chance\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Led thee astray so far from Campaldino,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That never has thy sepulture been known?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*“Oh,”\zsay-e\* he replied, \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*“at Casentino’s foot\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*A river crosses named Archiano, born\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*Above the Hermitage in Apennine.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*There where the name thereof becometh void\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*Did I arrive, pierced through and through the throat,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*Fleeing on foot, and bloodying the plain;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*There my sight lost I, and my utterance\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*Ceased in the name of Mary, and thereat\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*I fell, and tenantless my flesh remained.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*Truth will I speak, repeat it to the living;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*God’s Angel took me up, and he of hell\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*Shouted: ‘O thou from heaven, why dost thou rob me?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*Thou bearest away the eternal part of him,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*For one poor little tear, that takes him from me;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*But with the rest I’ll deal in other fashion!’\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*Well knowest thou how in the air is gathered\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*That humid vapour which to water turns,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*Soon as it rises where the cold doth grasp it.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*He joined that evil will, which aye seeks evil,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*To intellect, and moved the mist and wind\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*By means of power, which his own nature gave;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*Thereafter, when the day was spent, the valley\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*From Pratomagno to the great yoke covered\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*With fog, and made the heaven above intent,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*So that the pregnant air to water changed;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*Down fell the rain, and to the gullies came\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*Whate’er of it earth tolerated not;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*And as it mingled with the mighty torrents,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*Towards the royal river with such speed\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*It headlong rushed, that nothing held it back.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*My frozen body near unto its outlet\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*The robust Archian found, and into Arno\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*Thrust it, and loosened from my breast the cross\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*I made of me, when agony o’ercame me;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*It rolled me on the banks and on the bottom,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buonconte"\*Then with its booty covered and begirt me.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pia"\*“Ah, when thou hast returned unto the world,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pia"\*And rested thee from thy long journeying,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* After the second followed the third spirit,
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pia"\*“Do thou remember me who am the Pia;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pia"\*Siena made me, unmade me Maremma;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pia"\*He knoweth it, who had encircled first,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pia"\*Espousing me, my finger with his gem.”\zsay-e\*
\c 6
\cp 6
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* Whene’er is broken up the game of Zara,
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* He who has lost remains behind despondent,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* The throws repeating, and in sadness learns;
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* The people with the other all depart;
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* One goes in front, and one behind doth pluck him,
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* And at his side one brings himself to mind;
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* He pauses not, and this and that one hears;
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* They crowd no more to whom his hand he stretches,
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* And from the throng he thus defends himself.
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* Even such was I in that dense multitude,
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* Turning to them this way and that my face,
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* And, promising, I freed myself therefrom.
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* There was the Aretine, who from the arms
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* Untamed of Ghin di Tacco had his death,
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* And he who fleeing from pursuit was drowned.
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* There was imploring with his hands outstretched
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* Frederick Novello, and that one of Pisa
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* Who made the good Marzucco seem so strong.
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* I saw Count Orso; and the soul divided
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* By hatred and by envy from its body,
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* As it declared, and not for crime committed,
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* Pierre de la Brosse I say; and here provide
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* While still on earth the Lady of Brabant,
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* So that for this she be of no worse flock!
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* As soon as I was free from all those shades
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* Who only prayed that some one else may pray,
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* So as to hasten their becoming holy,
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* Began I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“It appears that thou deniest,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*O light of mine, expressly in some text,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That orison can bend decree of Heaven;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And ne’ertheless these people pray for this.\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Might then their expectation bootless be?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Or is to me thy saying not quite clear?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* And he to me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“My writing is explicit,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And not fallacious is the hope of these,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*If with sane intellect ’tis well regarded;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*For top of judgment doth not vail itself,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Because the fire of love fulfils at once\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*What he must satisfy who here installs him.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And there, where I affirmed that proposition,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Defect was not amended by a prayer,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Because the prayer from God was separate.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Verily, in so deep a questioning\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Do not decide, unless she tell it thee,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Who light ’twixt truth and intellect shall be.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*I know not if thou understand; I speak\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Of Beatrice; her shalt thou see above,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Smiling and happy, on this mountain’s top.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* And I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Good Leader, let us make more haste,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*For I no longer tire me as before;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And see, e’en now the hill a shadow casts.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“We will go forward with this day”\zsay-e\* he answered,
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“As far as now is possible for us;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But otherwise the fact is than thou thinkest.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Ere thou art up there, thou shalt see return\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Him, who now hides himself behind the hill,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*So that thou dost not interrupt his rays.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But yonder there behold! a soul that stationed\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*All, all alone is looking hitherward;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*It will point out to us the quickest way.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* We came up unto it; O Lombard soul,
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* How lofty and disdainful thou didst bear thee,
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* And grand and slow in moving of thine eyes!
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* Nothing whatever did it say to us,
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* But let us go our way, eying us only
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* After the manner of a couchant lion;
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* Still near to it Virgilius drew, entreating
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* That it would point us out the best ascent;
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* And it replied not unto his demand,
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* But of our native land and of our life
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* It questioned us; and the sweet Guide began:
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Mantua,”\zsay-e\*—and the shade, all in itself recluse,
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* Rose tow’rds him from the place where first it was,
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* Saying: \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*“O Mantuan, I am Sordello\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*Of thine own land!”\zsay-e\* and one embraced the other.
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* Ah! servile Italy, grief’s hostelry!
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* A ship without a pilot in great tempest!
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* No Lady thou of Provinces, but brothel!
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* That noble soul was so impatient, only
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* At the sweet sound of his own native land,
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* To make its citizen glad welcome there;
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* And now within thee are not without war
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* Thy living ones, and one doth gnaw the other
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* Of those whom one wall and one fosse shut in!
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* Search, wretched one, all round about the shores
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* Thy seaboard, and then look within thy bosom,
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* If any part of thee enjoyeth peace!
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* What boots it, that for thee Justinian
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* The bridle mend, if empty be the saddle?
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* Withouten this the shame would be the less.
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* Ah! people, thou that oughtest to be devout,
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* And to let Caesar sit upon the saddle,
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* If well thou hearest what God teacheth thee,
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* Behold how fell this wild beast has become,
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* Being no longer by the spur corrected,
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* Since thou hast laid thy hand upon the bridle.
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* O German Albert! who abandonest
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* Her that has grown recalcitrant and savage,
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* And oughtest to bestride her saddle-bow,
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* May a just judgment from the stars down fall
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* Upon thy blood, and be it new and open,
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* That thy successor may have fear thereof;
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* Because thy father and thyself have suffered,
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* By greed of those transalpine lands distrained,
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* The garden of the empire to be waste.
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* Come and behold Montecchi and Cappelletti,
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* Monaldi and Filippeschi, careless man!
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* Those sad already, and these doubt-depressed!
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* Come, cruel one! come and behold the oppression
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* Of thy nobility, and cure their wounds,
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* And thou shalt see how safe is Santafiore!
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* Come and behold thy Rome, that is lamenting,
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* Widowed, alone, and day and night exclaims,
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Rome"\*“My Caesar, why hast thou forsaken me?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* Come and behold how loving are the people;
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* And if for us no pity moveth thee,
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* Come and be made ashamed of thy renown!
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* And if it lawful be, O Jove Supreme!
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* Who upon earth for us wast crucified,
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* Are thy just eyes averted otherwhere?
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* Or preparation is ’t, that, in the abyss
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* Of thine own counsel, for some good thou makest
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* From our perception utterly cut off?
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* For all the towns of Italy are full
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* Of tyrants, and becometh a Marcellus
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* Each peasant churl who plays the partisan!
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* My Florence! well mayst thou contented be
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* With this digression, which concerns thee not,
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* Thanks to thy people who such forethought take!
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* Many at heart have justice, but shoot slowly,
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* That unadvised they come not to the bow,
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* But on their very lips thy people have it!
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* Many refuse to bear the common burden;
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* But thy solicitous people answereth
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* Without being asked, and crieth: \zsay-s |who="The Florentines"\*“I submit.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* Now be thou joyful, for thou hast good reason;
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* Thou affluent, thou in peace, thou full of wisdom!
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* If I speak true, the event conceals it not.
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* Athens and Lacedaemon, they who made
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* The ancient laws, and were so civilized,
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* Made towards living well a little sign
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* Compared with thee, who makest such fine-spun
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* Provisions, that to middle of November
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* Reaches not what thou in October spinnest.
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* How oft, within the time of thy remembrance,
\q1 \v 146 \vp 146\vp* Laws, money, offices, and usages
\q1 \v 147 \vp 147\vp* Hast thou remodelled, and renewed thy members?
\b
\q1 \v 148 \vp 148\vp* And if thou mind thee well, and see the light,
\q1 \v 149 \vp 149\vp* Thou shalt behold thyself like a sick woman,
\q1 \v 150 \vp 150\vp* Who cannot find repose upon her down,
\b
\q1 \v 151 \vp 151\vp* But by her tossing wardeth off her pain.
\c 7
\cp 7
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* After the gracious and glad salutations
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Had three and four times been reiterated,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* Sordello backward drew and said, \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*“Who are you?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Or ever to this mountain were directed\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The souls deserving to ascend to God,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*My bones were buried by Octavian.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*I am Virgilius; and for no crime else\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Did I lose heaven, than for not having faith;”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* In this wise then my Leader made reply.
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* As one who suddenly before him sees
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* Something whereat he marvels, who believes
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* And yet does not, saying, \zsay-s |who="One who marvels"\*“It is! it is not!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* So he appeared; and then bowed down his brow,
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* And with humility returned towards him,
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* And, where inferiors embrace, embraced him.
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*“O glory of the Latians, thou,”\zsay-e\* he said,
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*“Through whom our language showed what it could do\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*O pride eternal of the place I came from,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*What merit or what grace to me reveals thee?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*If I to hear thy words be worthy, tell me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*If thou dost come from Hell, and from what cloister.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Through all the circles of the doleful realm,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* Responded he, \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“have I come hitherward;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Heaven’s power impelled me, and with that I come.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*I by not doing, not by doing, lost\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The sight of that high sun which thou desirest,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And which too late by me was recognized.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*A place there is below not sad with torments,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But darkness only, where the lamentations\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Have not the sound of wailing, but are sighs.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*There dwell I with the little innocents\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Snatched by the teeth of Death, or ever they\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Were from our human sinfulness exempt.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*There dwell I among those who the three saintly\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Virtues did not put on, and without vice\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The others knew and followed all of them.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But if thou know and can, some indication\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Give us by which we may the sooner come\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Where Purgatory has its right beginning.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* He answered: \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*“No fixed place has been assigned us;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*’Tis lawful for me to go up and round;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*So far as I can go, as guide I join thee.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*But see already how the day declines,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*And to go up by night we are not able;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*Therefore ’tis well to think of some fair sojourn.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*Souls are there on the right hand here withdrawn;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*If thou permit me I will lead thee to them,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*And thou shalt know them not without delight.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“How is this?”\zsay-e\* was the answer; \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“should one wish\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*To mount by night would he prevented be\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*By others? or mayhap would not have power?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* And on the ground the good Sordello drew
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* His finger, saying, \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*“See, this line alone\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*Thou couldst not pass after the sun is gone;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*Not that aught else would hindrance give, however,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*To going up, save the nocturnal darkness;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*This with the want of power the will perplexes.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*We might indeed therewith return below,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*And, wandering, walk the hill-side round about,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*While the horizon holds the day imprisoned.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* Thereon my Lord, as if in wonder, said:
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Do thou conduct us thither, where thou sayest\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That we can take delight in tarrying.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* Little had we withdrawn us from that place,
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* When I perceived the mount was hollowed out
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* In fashion as the valleys here are hollowed.
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*“Thitherward,”\zsay-e\* said that shade, \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*“will we repair,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*Where of itself the hill-side makes a lap,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*And there for the new day will we await.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* ’Twixt hill and plain there was a winding path
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* Which led us to the margin of that dell,
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* Where dies the border more than half away.
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* Gold and fine silver, and scarlet and pearl-white,
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* The Indian wood resplendent and serene,
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* Fresh emerald the moment it is broken,
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* By herbage and by flowers within that hollow
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* Planted, each one in colour would be vanquished,
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* As by its greater vanquished is the less.
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* Nor in that place had nature painted only,
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* But of the sweetness of a thousand odours
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* Made there a mingled fragrance and unknown.
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*“Salve Regina,”\zsay-e\* on the green and flowers
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* There seated, singing, spirits I beheld,
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* Which were not visible outside the valley.
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*“Before the scanty sun now seeks his nest,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* Began the Mantuan who had led us thither,
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*“Among them do not wish me to conduct you.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*Better from off this ledge the acts and faces\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*Of all of them will you discriminate,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*Than in the plain below received among them.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*He who sits highest, and the semblance bears\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*Of having what he should have done neglected,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*And to the others’ song moves not his lips,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*Rudolph the Emperor was, who had the power\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*To heal the wounds that Italy have slain,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*So that through others slowly she revives.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*The other, who in look doth comfort him,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*Governed the region where the water springs,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*The Moldau bears the Elbe, and Elbe the sea.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*His name was Ottocar; and in swaddling-clothes\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*Far better he than bearded Winceslaus\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*His son, who feeds in luxury and ease.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*And the small-nosed, who close in council seems\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*With him that has an aspect so benign,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*Died fleeing and disflowering the lily;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*Look there, how he is beating at his breast!\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*Behold the other one, who for his cheek\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*Sighing has made of his own palm a bed;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*Father and father-in-law of France’s Pest\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*Are they, and know his vicious life and lewd,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*And hence proceeds the grief that so doth pierce them.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*He who appears so stalwart, and chimes in,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*Singing, with that one of the manly nose,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*The cord of every valour wore begirt;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*And if as King had after him remained\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*The stripling who in rear of him is sitting,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*Well had the valour passed from vase to vase,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*Which cannot of the other heirs be said.\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*Frederick and Jacomo possess the realms,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*But none the better heritage possesses.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*Not oftentimes upriseth through the branches\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*The probity of man; and this He wills\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*Who gives it, so that we may ask of Him.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*Eke to the large-nosed reach my words, no less\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*Than to the other, Pier, who with him sings;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*Whence Provence and Apulia grieve already\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*The plant is as inferior to its seed,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*As more than Beatrice and Margaret\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*Costanza boasteth of her husband still.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*Behold the monarch of the simple life,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*Harry of England, sitting there alone;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*He in his branches has a better issue.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*He who the lowest on the ground among them\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*Sits looking upward, is the Marquis William,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*For whose sake Alessandria and her war\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*Make Monferrat and Canavese weep.”\zsay-e\*
\c 8
\cp 8
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* ’Twas now the hour that turneth back desire
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* In those who sail the sea, and melts the heart,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* The day they’ve said to their sweet friends farewell,
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* And the new pilgrim penetrates with love,
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* If he doth hear from far away a bell
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* That seemeth to deplore the dying day,
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* When I began to make of no avail
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* My hearing, and to watch one of the souls
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* Uprisen, that begged attention with its hand.
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* It joined and lifted upward both its palms,
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* Fixing its eyes upon the orient,
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* As if it said to God, \zsay-s |who="A soul"\*“Naught else I care for.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* \zsay-s |who="A soul"\*“Te lucis ante”\zsay-e\* so devoutly issued
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* Forth from its mouth, and with such dulcet notes,
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* It made me issue forth from my own mind.
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* And then the others, sweetly and devoutly,
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* Accompanied it through all the hymn entire,
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* Having their eyes on the supernal wheels.
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* Here, Reader, fix thine eyes well on the truth,
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* For now indeed so subtile is the veil,
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* Surely to penetrate within is easy.
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* I saw that army of the gentle-born
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* Thereafterward in silence upward gaze,
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* As if in expectation, pale and humble;
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* And from on high come forth and down descend,
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* I saw two Angels with two flaming swords,
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* Truncated and deprived of their points.
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* Green as the little leaflets just now born
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* Their garments were, which, by their verdant pinions
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* Beaten and blown abroad, they trailed behind.
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* One just above us came to take his station,
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* And one descended to the opposite bank,
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* So that the people were contained between them.
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* Clearly in them discerned I the blond head;
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* But in their faces was the eye bewildered,
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* As faculty confounded by excess.
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*“From Mary’s bosom both of them have come,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* Sordello said, \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*“as guardians of the valley\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*Against the serpent, that will come anon.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* Whereupon I, who knew not by what road,
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* Turned round about, and closely drew myself,
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* Utterly frozen, to the faithful shoulders.
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* And once again Sordello: \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*“Now descend we\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*’Mid the grand shades, and we will speak to them;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*Right pleasant will it be for them to see you.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* Only three steps I think that I descended,
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* And was below, and saw one who was looking
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* Only at me, as if he fain would know me.
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* Already now the air was growing dark,
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* But not so that between his eyes and mine
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* It did not show what it before locked up.
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* Tow’rds me he moved, and I tow’rds him did move;
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* Noble Judge Nino! how it me delighted,
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* When I beheld thee not among the damned!
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* No greeting fair was left unsaid between us;
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* Then asked he: \zsay-s |who="Nino Visconti"\*“How long is it since thou camest\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nino Visconti"\*O’er the far waters to the mountain’s foot?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Oh!”\zsay-e\* said I to him, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“through the dismal places\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*I came this morn; and am in the first life,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Albeit the other, going thus, I gain.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* And on the instant my reply was heard,
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* He and Sordello both shrank back from me,
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* Like people who are suddenly bewildered.
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* One to Virgilius, and the other turned
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* To one who sat there, crying, \zsay-s |who="Nino Visconti"\*“Up, Currado!\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nino Visconti"\*Come and behold what God in grace has willed!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* Then, turned to me: \zsay-s |who="Nino Visconti"\*“By that especial grace\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nino Visconti"\*Thou owest unto Him, who so conceals\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nino Visconti"\*His own first wherefore, that it has no ford,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nino Visconti"\*When thou shalt be beyond the waters wide,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nino Visconti"\*Tell my Giovanna that she pray for me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nino Visconti"\*Where answer to the innocent is made.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nino Visconti"\*I do not think her mother loves me more,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nino Visconti"\*Since she has laid aside her wimple white,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nino Visconti"\*Which she, unhappy, needs must wish again.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nino Visconti"\*Through her full easily is comprehended\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nino Visconti"\*How long in woman lasts the fire of love,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nino Visconti"\*If eye or touch do not relight it often.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nino Visconti"\*So fair a hatchment will not make for her\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nino Visconti"\*The Viper marshalling the Milanese\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nino Visconti"\*A-field, as would have made Gallura’s Cock.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* In this wise spake he, with the stamp impressed
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* Upon his aspect of that righteous zeal
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* Which measurably burneth in the heart.
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* My greedy eyes still wandered up to heaven,
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* Still to that point where slowest are the stars,
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* Even as a wheel the nearest to its axle.
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* And my Conductor: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Son, what dost thou gaze at\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Up there?”\zsay-e\*  And I to him: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“At those three torches\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*With which this hither pole is all on fire.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* And he to me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“The four resplendent stars\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thou sawest this morning are down yonder low,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And these have mounted up to where those were.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* As he was speaking, to himself Sordello
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* Drew him, and said, \zsay-s |who="Sordello"\*“Lo there our Adversary!”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* And pointed with his finger to look thither.
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* Upon the side on which the little valley
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* No barrier hath, a serpent was; perchance
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* The same which gave to Eve the bitter food.
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* ’Twixt grass and flowers came on the evil streak,
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* Turning at times its head about, and licking
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* Its back like to a beast that smoothes itself.
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* I did not see, and therefore cannot say
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* How the celestial falcons ’gan to move,
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* But well I saw that they were both in motion.
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* Hearing the air cleft by their verdant wings,
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* The serpent fled, and round the Angels wheeled,
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* Up to their stations flying back alike.
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* The shade that to the Judge had near approached
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* When he had called, throughout that whole assault
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* Had not a moment loosed its gaze on me.
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Currado Malaspina"\*“So may the light that leadeth thee on high\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Currado Malaspina"\*Find in thine own free-will as much of wax\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Currado Malaspina"\*As needful is up to the highest azure,”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* Began it, \zsay-s |who="Currado Malaspina"\*“if some true intelligence\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Currado Malaspina"\*Of Valdimagra or its neighbourhood\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Currado Malaspina"\*Thou knowest, tell it me, who once was great there.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Currado Malaspina"\*Currado Malaspina was I called;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Currado Malaspina"\*I’m not the elder, but from him descended;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Currado Malaspina"\*To mine I bore the love which here refineth.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“O,”\zsay-e\* said I unto him, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“through your domains\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*I never passed, but where is there a dwelling\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Throughout all Europe, where they are not known?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That fame, which doeth honour to your house,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Proclaims its Signors and proclaims its land,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*So that he knows of them who ne’er was there.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And, as I hope for heaven, I swear to you\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Your honoured family in naught abates\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*The glory of the purse and of the sword.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*It is so privileged by use and nature,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That though a guilty head misguide the world,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Sole it goes right, and scorns the evil way.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* And he: \zsay-s |who="Currado Malaspina"\*“Now go; for the sun shall not lie\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Currado Malaspina"\*Seven times upon the pillow which the Ram\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Currado Malaspina"\*With all his four feet covers and bestrides,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Currado Malaspina"\*Before that such a courteous opinion\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Currado Malaspina"\*Shall in the middle of thy head be nailed\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Currado Malaspina"\*With greater nails than of another’s speech,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* \zsay-s |who="Currado Malaspina"\*Unless the course of justice standeth still.”\zsay-e\*
\c 9
\cp 9
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* The concubine of old Tithonus now
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Gleamed white upon the eastern balcony,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* Forth from the arms of her sweet paramour;
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* With gems her forehead all relucent was,
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* Set in the shape of that cold animal
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* Which with its tail doth smite amain the nations,
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* And of the steps, with which she mounts, the Night
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Had taken two in that place where we were,
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* And now the third was bending down its wings;
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* When I, who something had of Adam in me,
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* Vanquished by sleep, upon the grass reclined,
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* There were all five of us already sat.
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* Just at the hour when her sad lay begins
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* The little swallow, near unto the morning,
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* Perchance in memory of her former woes,
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* And when the mind of man, a wanderer
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* More from the flesh, and less by thought imprisoned,
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* Almost prophetic in its visions is,
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* In dreams it seemed to me I saw suspended
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* An eagle in the sky, with plumes of gold,
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* With wings wide open, and intent to stoop,
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* And this, it seemed to me, was where had been
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* By Ganymede his kith and kin abandoned,
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* When to the high consistory he was rapt.
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* I thought within myself, perchance he strikes
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* From habit only here, and from elsewhere
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* Disdains to bear up any in his feet.
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* Then wheeling somewhat more, it seemed to me,
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* Terrible as the lightning he descended,
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* And snatched me upward even to the fire.
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* Therein it seemed that he and I were burning,
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* And the imagined fire did scorch me so,
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* That of necessity my sleep was broken.
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* Not otherwise Achilles started up,
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* Around him turning his awakened eyes,
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* And knowing not the place in which he was,
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* What time from Chiron stealthily his mother
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* Carried him sleeping in her arms to Scyros,
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* Wherefrom the Greeks withdrew him afterwards,
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* Than I upstarted, when from off my face
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* Sleep fled away; and pallid I became,
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* As doth the man who freezes with affright.
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* Only my Comforter was at my side,
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* And now the sun was more than two hours high,
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* And turned towards the sea-shore was my face.
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Be not intimidated,”\zsay-e\* said my Lord,
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Be reassured, for all is well with us;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Do not restrain, but put forth all thy strength.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thou hast at length arrived at Purgatory;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*See there the cliff that closes it around;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*See there the entrance, where it seems disjoined.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Whilom at dawn, which doth precede the day,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*When inwardly thy spirit was asleep\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Upon the flowers that deck the land below,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*There came a Lady and said: ‘I am Lucia;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Let me take this one up, who is asleep;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*So will I make his journey easier for him.’\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Sordello and the other noble shapes\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Remained; she took thee, and, as day grew bright,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Upward she came, and I upon her footsteps.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*She laid thee here; and first her beauteous eyes\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That open entrance pointed out to me;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Then she and sleep together went away.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* In guise of one whose doubts are reassured,
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* And who to confidence his fear doth change,
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* After the truth has been discovered to him,
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* So did I change; and when without disquiet
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* My Leader saw me, up along the cliff
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* He moved, and I behind him, tow’rd the height.
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* Reader, thou seest well how I exalt
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* My theme, and therefore if with greater art
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* I fortify it, marvel not thereat.
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* Nearer approached we, and were in such place,
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* That there, where first appeared to me a rift
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* Like to a crevice that disparts a wall,
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* I saw a portal, and three stairs beneath,
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* Diverse in colour, to go up to it,
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* And a gate-keeper, who yet spake no word.
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* And as I opened more and more mine eyes,
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* I saw him seated on the highest stair,
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* Such in the face that I endured it not.
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* And in his hand he had a naked sword,
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* Which so reflected back the sunbeams tow’rds us,
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* That oft in vain I lifted up mine eyes.
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*“Tell it from where you are, what is’t you wish?”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* Began he to exclaim; \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*“where is the escort?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*Take heed your coming hither harm you not!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“A Lady of Heaven, with these things conversant,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* My Master answered him, \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“but even now\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Said to us, ‘Thither go; there is the portal.’”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*“And may she speed your footsteps in all good,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* Again began the courteous janitor;
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*“Come forward then unto these stairs of ours.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* Thither did we approach; and the first stair
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* Was marble white, so polished and so smooth,
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* I mirrored myself therein as I appear.
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* The second, tinct of deeper hue than perse,
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* Was of a calcined and uneven stone,
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* Cracked all asunder lengthwise and across.
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* The third, that uppermost rests massively,
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* Porphyry seemed to me, as flaming red
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* As blood that from a vein is spirting forth.
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* Both of his feet was holding upon this
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* The Angel of God, upon the threshold seated,
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* Which seemed to me a stone of diamond.
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* Along the three stairs upward with good will
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* Did my Conductor draw me, saying: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Ask\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Humbly that he the fastening may undo.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* Devoutly at the holy feet I cast me,
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* For mercy’s sake besought that he would open,
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* But first upon my breast three times I smote.
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* Seven P’s upon my forehead he described
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* With the sword’s point, and, \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*“Take heed that thou wash\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*These wounds, when thou shalt be within,”\zsay-e\* he said.
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* Ashes, or earth that dry is excavated,
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* Of the same colour were with his attire,
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* And from beneath it he drew forth two keys.
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* One was of gold, and the other was of silver;
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* First with the white, and after with the yellow,
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* Plied he the door, so that I was content.
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*“Whenever faileth either of these keys\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*So that it turn not rightly in the lock,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* He said to us, \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*“this entrance doth not open.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*More precious one is, but the other needs\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*More art and intellect ere it unlock,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*For it is that which doth the knot unloose.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*From Peter I have them; and he bade me err\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*Rather in opening than in keeping shut,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*If people but fall down before my feet.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* Then pushed the portals of the sacred door,
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* Exclaiming: \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*“Enter; but I give you warning\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*That forth returns whoever looks behind.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* And when upon their hinges were turned round
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* The swivels of that consecrated gate,
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* Which are of metal, massive and sonorous,
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* Roared not so loud, nor so discordant seemed
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* Tarpeia, when was ta’en from it the good
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* Metellus, wherefore meagre it remained.
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* At the first thunder-peal I turned attentive,
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* And \zsay-s |who="Voices"\*“Te Deum laudamus”\zsay-e\* seemed to hear
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* In voices mingled with sweet melody.
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* Exactly such an image rendered me
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* That which I heard, as we are wont to catch,
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* When people singing with the organ stand;
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* For now we hear, and now hear not, the words.
\c 10
\cp 10
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* When we had crossed the threshold of the door
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Which the perverted love of souls disuses,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* Because it makes the crooked way seem straight,
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* Re-echoing I heard it closed again;
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* And if I had turned back mine eyes upon it,
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* What for my failing had been fit excuse?
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* We mounted upward through a rifted rock,
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Which undulated to this side and that,
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* Even as a wave receding and advancing.
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Here it behoves us use a little art,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* Began my Leader, \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“to adapt ourselves\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Now here, now there, to the receding side.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* And this our footsteps so infrequent made,
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* That sooner had the moon’s decreasing disk
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* Regained its bed to sink again to rest,
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* Than we were forth from out that needle’s eye;
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* But when we free and in the open were,
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* There where the mountain backward piles itself,
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* I wearied out, and both of us uncertain
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* About our way, we stopped upon a plain
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* More desolate than roads across the deserts.
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* From where its margin borders on the void,
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* To foot of the high bank that ever rises,
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* A human body three times told would measure;
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* And far as eye of mine could wing its flight,
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* Now on the left, and on the right flank now,
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* The same this cornice did appear to me.
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* Thereon our feet had not been moved as yet,
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* When I perceived the embankment round about,
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* Which all right of ascent had interdicted,
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* To be of marble white, and so adorned
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* With sculptures, that not only Polycletus,
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* But Nature’s self, had there been put to shame.
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* The Angel, who came down to earth with tidings
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* Of peace, that had been wept for many a year,
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* And opened Heaven from its long interdict,
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* In front of us appeared so truthfully
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* There sculptured in a gracious attitude,
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* He did not seem an image that is silent.
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* One would have sworn that he was saying, \zsay-s |who="Gabriel"\*“Ave;”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* For she was there in effigy portrayed
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* Who turned the key to ope the exalted love,
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* And in her mien this language had impressed,
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Mary"\*“Ecce ancilla Dei,”\zsay-e\* as distinctly
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* As any figure stamps itself in wax.
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Keep not thy mind upon one place alone,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* The gentle Master said, who had me standing
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* Upon that side where people have their hearts;
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* Whereat I moved mine eyes, and I beheld
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* In rear of Mary, and upon that side
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* Where he was standing who conducted me,
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* Another story on the rock imposed;
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* Wherefore I passed Virgilius and drew near,
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* So that before mine eyes it might be set.
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* There sculptured in the self-same marble were
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* The cart and oxen, drawing the holy ark,
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* Wherefore one dreads an office not appointed.
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* People appeared in front, and all of them
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* In seven choirs divided, of two senses
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* Made one say \zsay-s |who="Hearing"\*“No,”\zsay-e\* the other, \zsay-s |who="Sight"\*“Yes, they sing.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* Likewise unto the smoke of the frankincense,
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* Which there was imaged forth, the eyes and nose
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* Were in the yes and no discordant made.
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* Preceded there the vessel benedight,
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* Dancing with girded loins, the humble Psalmist,
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* And more and less than King was he in this.
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* Opposite, represented at the window
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* Of a great palace, Michal looked upon him,
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* Even as a woman scornful and afflicted.
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* I moved my feet from where I had been standing,
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* To examine near at hand another story,
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* Which after Michal glimmered white upon me.
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* There the high glory of the Roman Prince
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* Was chronicled, whose great beneficence
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* Moved Gregory to his great victory;
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* ’Tis of the Emperor Trajan I am speaking;
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* And a poor widow at his bridle stood,
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* In attitude of weeping and of grief.
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* Around about him seemed it thronged and full
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* Of cavaliers, and the eagles in the gold
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* Above them visibly in the wind were moving.
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* The wretched woman in the midst of these
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* Seemed to be saying: \zsay-s |who="The widow"\*“Give me vengeance, Lord,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="The widow"\*For my dead son, for whom my heart is breaking.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* And he to answer her: \zsay-s |who="Trajan"\*“Now wait until\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Trajan"\*I shall return.”\zsay-e\*  And she: \zsay-s |who="The widow"\*“My Lord,”\zsay-e\* like one
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* In whom grief is impatient, \zsay-s |who="The widow"\*“shouldst thou not\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="The widow"\*Return?”\zsay-e\*  And he: \zsay-s |who="Trajan"\*“Who shall be where I am\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Trajan"\*Will give it thee.”\zsay-e\*  And she: \zsay-s |who="The widow"\*“Good deed of others\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="The widow"\*What boots it thee, if thou neglect thine own?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* Whence he: \zsay-s |who="Trajan"\*“Now comfort thee, for it behoves me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Trajan"\*That I discharge my duty ere I move;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Trajan"\*Justice so wills, and pity doth retain me.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* He who on no new thing has ever looked
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* Was the creator of this visible language,
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* Novel to us, for here it is not found.
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* While I delighted me in contemplating
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* The images of such humility,
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* And dear to look on for their Maker’s sake,
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Behold, upon this side, but rare they make\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Their steps,”\zsay-e\* the Poet murmured, \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“many people;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*These will direct us to the lofty stairs.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* Mine eyes, that in beholding were intent
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* To see new things, of which they curious are,
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* In turning round towards him were not slow.
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* But still I wish not, Reader, thou shouldst swerve
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* From thy good purposes, because thou hearest
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* How God ordaineth that the debt be paid;
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* Attend not to the fashion of the torment,
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* Think of what follows; think that at the worst
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* It cannot reach beyond the mighty sentence.
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Master,”\zsay-e\* began I, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“that which I behold\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Moving towards us seems to me not persons,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And what I know not, so in sight I waver.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* And he to me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“The grievous quality\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Of this their torment bows them so to earth,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That my own eyes at first contended with it;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But look there fixedly, and disentangle\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*By sight what cometh underneath those stones;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Already canst thou see how each is stricken.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* O ye proud Christians! wretched, weary ones!
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* Who, in the vision of the mind infirm
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* Confidence have in your backsliding steps,
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* Do ye not comprehend that we are worms,
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* Born to bring forth the angelic butterfly
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* That flieth unto judgment without screen?
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* Why floats aloft your spirit high in air?
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* Like are ye unto insects undeveloped,
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* Even as the worm in whom formation fails!
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* As to sustain a ceiling or a roof,
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* In place of corbel, oftentimes a figure
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* Is seen to join its knees unto its breast,
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* Which makes of the unreal real anguish
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* Arise in him who sees it, fashioned thus
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* Beheld I those, when I had ta’en good heed.
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* True is it, they were more or less bent down,
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* According as they more or less were laden;
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* And he who had most patience in his looks
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* Weeping did seem to say, \zsay-s |who="A soul"\*“I can no more!”\zsay-e\*
\c 11
\cp 11
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*“Our Father, thou who dwellest in the heavens,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*Not circumscribed, but from the greater love\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*Thou bearest to the first effects on high,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*Praised be thy name and thine omnipotence\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*By every creature, as befitting is\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*To render thanks to thy sweet effluence.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*Come unto us the peace of thy dominion,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*For unto it we cannot of ourselves,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*If it come not, with all our intellect.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*Even as thine own Angels of their will\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*Make sacrifice to thee, Hosanna singing,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*So may all men make sacrifice of theirs.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*Give unto us this day our daily manna,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*Withouten which in this rough wilderness\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*Backward goes he who toils most to advance.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*And even as we the trespass we have suffered\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*Pardon in one another, pardon thou\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*Benignly, and regard not our desert.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*Our virtue, which is easily o’ercome,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*Put not to proof with the old Adversary,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*But thou from him who spurs it so, deliver.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*This last petition verily, dear Lord,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*Not for ourselves is made, who need it not,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*But for their sake who have remained behind us.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* Thus for themselves and us good furtherance
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* Those shades imploring, went beneath a weight
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* Like unto that of which we sometimes dream,
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* Unequally in anguish round and round
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* And weary all, upon that foremost cornice,
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* Purging away the smoke-stains of the world.
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* If there good words are always said for us,
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* What may not here be said and done for them,
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* By those who have a good root to their will?
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* Well may we help them wash away the marks
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* That hence they carried, so that clean and light
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* They may ascend unto the starry wheels!
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Ah! so may pity and justice you disburden\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Soon, that ye may have power to move the wing,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That shall uplift you after your desire,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Show us on which hand tow’rd the stairs the way\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Is shortest, and if more than one the passes,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Point us out that which least abruptly falls;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*For he who cometh with me, through the burden\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Of Adam’s flesh wherewith he is invested,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Against his will is chary of his climbing.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* The words of theirs which they returned to those
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* That he whom I was following had spoken,
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* It was not manifest from whom they came,
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* But it was said: \zsay-s |who="Omberto Aldobrandeschi"\*“To the right hand come with us\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Omberto Aldobrandeschi"\*Along the bank, and ye shall find a pass\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Omberto Aldobrandeschi"\*Possible for living person to ascend.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Omberto Aldobrandeschi"\*And were I not impeded by the stone,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Omberto Aldobrandeschi"\*Which this proud neck of mine doth subjugate,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Omberto Aldobrandeschi"\*Whence I am forced to hold my visage down,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Omberto Aldobrandeschi"\*Him, who still lives and does not name himself,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Omberto Aldobrandeschi"\*Would I regard, to see if I may know him\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Omberto Aldobrandeschi"\*And make him piteous unto this burden.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Omberto Aldobrandeschi"\*A Latian was I, and born of a great Tuscan;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Omberto Aldobrandeschi"\*Guglielmo Aldobrandeschi was my father;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Omberto Aldobrandeschi"\*I know not if his name were ever with you.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Omberto Aldobrandeschi"\*The ancient blood and deeds of gallantry\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Omberto Aldobrandeschi"\*Of my progenitors so arrogant made me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Omberto Aldobrandeschi"\*That, thinking not upon the common mother,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Omberto Aldobrandeschi"\*All men I held in scorn to such extent\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Omberto Aldobrandeschi"\*I died therefor, as know the Sienese,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Omberto Aldobrandeschi"\*And every child in Campagnatico.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Omberto Aldobrandeschi"\*I am Omberto; and not to me alone\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Omberto Aldobrandeschi"\*Has pride done harm, but all my kith and kin\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Omberto Aldobrandeschi"\*Has with it dragged into adversity.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Omberto Aldobrandeschi"\*And here must I this burden bear for it\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Omberto Aldobrandeschi"\*Till God be satisfied, since I did not\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Omberto Aldobrandeschi"\*Among the living, here among the dead.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* Listening I downward bent my countenance;
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* And one of them, not this one who was speaking,
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* Twisted himself beneath the weight that cramps him,
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* And looked at me, and knew me, and called out,
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* Keeping his eyes laboriously fixed
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* On me, who all bowed down was going with them.
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“O,”\zsay-e\* asked I him, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“art thou not Oderisi,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Agobbio’s honour, and honour of that art\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Which is in Paris called illuminating?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*“Brother,”\zsay-e\* said he, \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*“more laughing are the leaves\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*Touched by the brush of Franco Bolognese;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*All his the honour now, and mine in part.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*In sooth I had not been so courteous\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*While I was living, for the great desire\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*Of excellence, on which my heart was bent.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*Here of such pride is paid the forfeiture;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*And yet I should not be here, were it not\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*That, having power to sin, I turned to God.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*O thou vain glory of the human powers,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*How little green upon thy summit lingers,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*If’t be not followed by an age of grossness!\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*In painting Cimabue thought that he\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*Should hold the field, now Giotto has the cry,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*So that the other’s fame is growing dim.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*So has one Guido from the other taken\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*The glory of our tongue, and he perchance\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*Is born, who from the nest shall chase them both.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*Naught is this mundane rumour but a breath\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*Of wind, that comes now this way and now that,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*And changes name, because it changes side.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*What fame shalt thou have more, if old peel off\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*From thee thy flesh, than if thou hadst been dead\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*Before thou left the ‘pappo’ and the ‘dindi,’\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*Ere pass a thousand years? which is a shorter\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*Space to the eterne, than twinkling of an eye\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*Unto the circle that in heaven wheels slowest.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*With him, who takes so little of the road\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*In front of me, all Tuscany resounded;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*And now he scarce is lisped of in Siena,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*Where he was lord, what time was overthrown\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*The Florentine delirium, that superb\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*Was at that day as now ’tis prostitute.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*Your reputation is the colour of grass\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*Which comes and goes, and that discolours it\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*By which it issues green from out the earth.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* And I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Thy true speech fills my heart with good\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Humility, and great tumour thou assuagest;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*But who is he, of whom just now thou spakest?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*“That,”\zsay-e\* he replied, \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*“is Provenzan Salvani,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*And he is here because he had presumed\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*To bring Siena all into his hands.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*He has gone thus, and goeth without rest\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*E’er since he died; such money renders back\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*In payment he who is on earth too daring.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* And I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“If every spirit who awaits\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*The verge of life before that he repent,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Remains below there and ascends not hither,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*(Unless good orison shall him bestead,)\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Until as much time as he lived be passed,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*How was the coming granted him in largess?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*“When he in greatest splendour lived,”\zsay-e\* said he,
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*“Freely upon the Campo of Siena,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*All shame being laid aside, he placed himself;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*And there to draw his friend from the duress\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*Which in the prison-house of Charles he suffered,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*He brought himself to tremble in each vein.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*I say no more, and know that I speak darkly;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*Yet little time shall pass before thy neighbours\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*Will so demean themselves that thou canst gloss it.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* \zsay-s |who="Oderisi"\*This action has released him from those confines.”\zsay-e\*
\c 12
\cp 12
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* Abreast, like oxen going in a yoke,
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* I with that heavy-laden soul went on,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* As long as the sweet pedagogue permitted;
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* But when he said, \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Leave him, and onward pass,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*For here ’tis good that with the sail and oars,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*As much as may be, each push on his barque;”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* Upright, as walking wills it, I redressed
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* My person, notwithstanding that my thoughts
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* Remained within me downcast and abashed.
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* I had moved on, and followed willingly
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* The footsteps of my Master, and we both
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* Already showed how light of foot we were,
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* When unto me he said: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Cast down thine eyes;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*’Twere well for thee, to alleviate the way,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*To look upon the bed beneath thy feet.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* As, that some memory may exist of them,
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* Above the buried dead their tombs in earth
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* Bear sculptured on them what they were before;
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* Whence often there we weep for them afresh,
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* From pricking of remembrance, which alone
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* To the compassionate doth set its spur;
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* So saw I there, but of a better semblance
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* In point of artifice, with figures covered
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* Whate’er as pathway from the mount projects.
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* I saw that one who was created noble
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* More than all other creatures, down from heaven
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* Flaming with lightnings fall upon one side.
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* I saw Briareus smitten by the dart
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* Celestial, lying on the other side,
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* Heavy upon the earth by mortal frost.
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* I saw Thymbraeus, Pallas saw, and Mars,
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* Still clad in armour round about their father,
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* Gaze at the scattered members of the giants.
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* I saw, at foot of his great labour, Nimrod,
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* As if bewildered, looking at the people
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* Who had been proud with him in Sennaar.
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* O Niobe! with what afflicted eyes
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* Thee I beheld upon the pathway traced,
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* Between thy seven and seven children slain!
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* O Saul! how fallen upon thy proper sword
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* Didst thou appear there lifeless in Gilboa,
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* That felt thereafter neither rain nor dew!
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* O mad Arachne! so I thee beheld
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* E’en then half spider, sad upon the shreds
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* Of fabric wrought in evil hour for thee!
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* O Rehoboam! no more seems to threaten
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* Thine image there; but full of consternation
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* A chariot bears it off, when none pursues!
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* Displayed moreo’er the adamantine pavement
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* How unto his own mother made Alcmaeon
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* Costly appear the luckless ornament;
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* Displayed how his own sons did throw themselves
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* Upon Sennacherib within the temple,
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* And how, he being dead, they left him there;
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* Displayed the ruin and the cruel carnage
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* That Tomyris wrought, when she to Cyrus said,
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Tomyris"\*“Blood didst thou thirst for, and with blood I glut thee!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* Displayed how routed fled the Assyrians
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* After that Holofernes had been slain,
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* And likewise the remainder of that slaughter.
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* I saw there Troy in ashes and in caverns;
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* O Ilion! thee, how abject and debased,
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* Displayed the image that is there discerned!
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* Whoe’er of pencil master was or stile,
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* That could portray the shades and traits which there
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* Would cause each subtile genius to admire?
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* Dead seemed the dead, the living seemed alive;
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* Better than I saw not who saw the truth,
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* All that I trod upon while bowed I went.
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* Now wax ye proud, and on with looks uplifted,
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* Ye sons of Eve, and bow not down your faces
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* So that ye may behold your evil ways!
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* More of the mount by us was now encompassed,
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* And far more spent the circuit of the sun,
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* Than had the mind preoccupied imagined,
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* When he, who ever watchful in advance
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* Was going on, began: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Lift up thy head,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*’Tis no more time to go thus meditating.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Lo there an Angel who is making haste\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*To come towards us; lo, returning is\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*From service of the day the sixth handmaiden.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*With reverence thine acts and looks adorn,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*So that he may delight to speed us upward;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Think that this day will never dawn again.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* I was familiar with his admonition
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* Ever to lose no time; so on this theme
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* He could not unto me speak covertly.
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* Towards us came the being beautiful
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* Vested in white, and in his countenance
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* Such as appears the tremulous morning star.
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* His arms he opened, and opened then his wings;
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*“Come,”\zsay-e\* said he, \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*“near at hand here are the steps,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*And easy from henceforth is the ascent.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* At this announcement few are they who come!
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* O human creatures, born to soar aloft,
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* Why fall ye thus before a little wind?
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* He led us on to where the rock was cleft;
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* There smote upon my forehead with his wings,
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* Then a safe passage promised unto me.
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* As on the right hand, to ascend the mount
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* Where seated is the church that lordeth it
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* O’er the well-guided, above Rubaconte,
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* The bold abruptness of the ascent is broken
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* By stairways that were made there in the age
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* When still were safe the ledger and the stave,
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* E’en thus attempered is the bank which falls
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* Sheer downward from the second circle there;
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* But on this side and that the high rock graze.
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* As we were turning thitherward our persons,
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Voices"\*“Beati pauperes spiritu,”\zsay-e\* voices
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* Sang in such wise that speech could tell it not.
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* Ah me! how different are these entrances
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* From the Infernal! for with anthems here
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* One enters, and below with wild laments.
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* We now were hunting up the sacred stairs,
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* And it appeared to me by far more easy
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* Than on the plain it had appeared before.
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* Whence I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“My Master, say, what heavy thing\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Has been uplifted from me, so that hardly\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Aught of fatigue is felt by me in walking?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* He answered: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“When the P’s which have remained\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Still on thy face almost obliterate\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Shall wholly, as the first is, be erased,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thy feet will be so vanquished by good will,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That not alone they shall not feel fatigue,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But urging up will be to them delight.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* Then did I even as they do who are going
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* With something on the head to them unknown,
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* Unless the signs of others make them doubt,
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* Wherefore the hand to ascertain is helpful,
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* And seeks and finds, and doth fulfill the office
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* Which cannot be accomplished by the sight;
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* And with the fingers of the right hand spread
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* I found but six the letters, that had carved
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* Upon my temples he who bore the keys;
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* Upon beholding which my Leader smiled.
\c 13
\cp 13
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* We were upon the summit of the stairs,
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Where for the second time is cut away
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* The mountain, which ascending shriveth all.
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* There in like manner doth a cornice bind
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* The hill all round about, as does the first,
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* Save that its arc more suddenly is curved.
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* Shade is there none, nor sculpture that appears;
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* So seems the bank, and so the road seems smooth,
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* With but the livid colour of the stone.
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“If to inquire we wait for people here,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* The Poet said, \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“I fear that peradventure\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Too much delay will our election have.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* Then steadfast on the sun his eyes he fixed,
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* Made his right side the centre of his motion,
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* And turned the left part of himself about.
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“O thou sweet light! with trust in whom I enter\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Upon this novel journey, do thou lead us,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* Said he, \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“as one within here should be led.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thou warmest the world, thou shinest over it;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*If other reason prompt not otherwise,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thy rays should evermore our leaders be!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* As much as here is counted for a mile,
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* So much already there had we advanced
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* In little time, by dint of ready will;
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* And tow’rds us there were heard to fly, albeit
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* They were not visible, spirits uttering
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* Unto Love’s table courteous invitations,
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* The first voice that passed onward in its flight,
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* \zsay-s |who="A voice"\*“Vinum non habent,”\zsay-e\* said in accents loud,
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* And went reiterating it behind us.
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* And ere it wholly grew inaudible
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* Because of distance, passed another, crying,
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="A voice"\*“I am Orestes!”\zsay-e\* and it also stayed not.
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“O,”\zsay-e\* said I, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Father, these, what voices are they?”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* And even as I asked, behold the third,
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* Saying: \zsay-s |who="A voice"\*“Love those from whom ye have had evil!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* And the good Master said: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“This circle scourges\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The sin of envy, and on that account\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Are drawn from love the lashes of the scourge.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The bridle of another sound shall be;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*I think that thou wilt hear it, as I judge,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Before thou comest to the Pass of Pardon.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But fix thine eyes athwart the air right steadfast,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And people thou wilt see before us sitting,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And each one close against the cliff is seated.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* Then wider than at first mine eyes I opened;
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* I looked before me, and saw shades with mantles
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* Not from the colour of the stone diverse.
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* And when we were a little farther onward,
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* I heard a cry of, \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*“Mary, pray for us!”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* A cry of, \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*“Michael, Peter, and all Saints!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* I do not think there walketh still on earth
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* A man so hard, that he would not be pierced
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* With pity at what afterward I saw.
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* For when I had approached so near to them
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* That manifest to me their acts became,
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* Drained was I at the eyes by heavy grief.
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* Covered with sackcloth vile they seemed to me,
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* And one sustained the other with his shoulder,
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* And all of them were by the bank sustained.
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* Thus do the blind, in want of livelihood,
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* Stand at the doors of churches asking alms,
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* And one upon another leans his head,
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* So that in others pity soon may rise,
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* Not only at the accent of their words,
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* But at their aspect, which no less implores.
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* And as unto the blind the sun comes not,
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* So to the shades, of whom just now I spake,
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* Heaven’s light will not be bounteous of itself;
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* For all their lids an iron wire transpierces,
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* And sews them up, as to a sparhawk wild
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* Is done, because it will not quiet stay.
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* To me it seemed, in passing, to do outrage,
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* Seeing the others without being seen;
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* Wherefore I turned me to my counsel sage.
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* Well knew he what the mute one wished to say,
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* And therefore waited not for my demand,
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* But said: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Speak, and be brief, and to the point.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* I had Virgilius upon that side
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* Of the embankment from which one may fall,
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* Since by no border ’tis engarlanded;
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* Upon the other side of me I had
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* The shades devout, who through the horrible seam
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* Pressed out the tears so that they bathed their cheeks.
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* To them I turned me, and, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“O people, certain,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* Began I, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“of beholding the high light,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Which your desire has solely in its care,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*So may grace speedily dissolve the scum\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Upon your consciences, that limpidly\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Through them descend the river of the mind,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Tell me, for dear ’twill be to me and gracious,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*If any soul among you here is Latian,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And ’twill perchance be good for him I learn it.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*“O brother mine, each one is citizen\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*Of one true city; but thy meaning is,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*Who may have lived in Italy a pilgrim.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* By way of answer this I seemed to hear
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* A little farther on than where I stood,
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* Whereat I made myself still nearer heard.
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* Among the rest I saw a shade that waited
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* In aspect, and should any one ask how,
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* Its chin it lifted upward like a blind man.
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Spirit,”\zsay-e\* I said, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“who stoopest to ascend,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*If thou art he who did reply to me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Make thyself known to me by place or name.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*“Sienese was I,”\zsay-e\* it replied, \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*“and with\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*The others here recleanse my guilty life,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*Weeping to Him to lend himself to us.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*Sapient I was not, although I Sapia\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*Was called, and I was at another’s harm\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*More happy far than at my own good fortune.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*And that thou mayst not think that I deceive thee,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*Hear if I was as foolish as I tell thee.\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*The arc already of my years descending,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*My fellow-citizens near unto Colle\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*Were joined in battle with their adversaries,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*And I was praying God for what he willed.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*Routed were they, and turned into the bitter\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*Passes of flight; and I, the chase beholding,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*A joy received unequalled by all others;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*So that I lifted upward my bold face\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*Crying to God, ‘Henceforth I fear thee not,’\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*As did the blackbird at the little sunshine.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*Peace I desired with God at the extreme\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*Of my existence, and as yet would not\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*My debt have been by penitence discharged,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*Had it not been that in remembrance held me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*Pier Pettignano in his holy prayers,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*Who out of charity was grieved for me.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*But who art thou, that into our conditions\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*Questioning goest, and hast thine eyes unbound\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*As I believe, and breathing dost discourse?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Mine eyes,”\zsay-e\* I said, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“will yet be here ta’en from me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*But for short space; for small is the offence\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Committed by their being turned with envy.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Far greater is the fear, wherein suspended\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*My soul is, of the torment underneath,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*For even now the load down there weighs on me.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* And she to me: \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*“Who led thee, then, among us\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*Up here, if to return below thou thinkest?”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* And I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“He who is with me, and speaks not;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And living am I; therefore ask of me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Spirit elect, if thou wouldst have me move\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*O’er yonder yet my mortal feet for thee.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*“O, this is such a novel thing to hear,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 146 \vp 146\vp* She answered, \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*“that great sign it is God loves thee;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 147 \vp 147\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*Therefore with prayer of thine sometimes assist me.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 148 \vp 148\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*And I implore, by what thou most desirest,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 149 \vp 149\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*If e’er thou treadest the soil of Tuscany,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 150 \vp 150\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*Well with my kindred reinstate my fame.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 151 \vp 151\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*Them wilt thou see among that people vain\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 152 \vp 152\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*Who hope in Talamone, and will lose there\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 153 \vp 153\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*More hope than in discovering the Diana;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 154 \vp 154\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sapia"\*But there still more the admirals will lose.”\zsay-e\*
\c 14
\cp 14
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*“Who is this one that goes about our mountain,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*Or ever Death has given him power of flight,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*And opes his eyes and shuts them at his will?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* \zsay-s |who="Rinieri da Calboli"\*“I know not who, but know he’s not alone;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* \zsay-s |who="Rinieri da Calboli"\*Ask him thyself, for thou art nearer to him,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* \zsay-s |who="Rinieri da Calboli"\*And gently, so that he may speak, accost him.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* Thus did two spirits, leaning tow’rds each other,
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Discourse about me there on the right hand;
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* Then held supine their faces to address me.
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* And said the one: \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*“O soul, that, fastened still\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*Within the body, tow’rds the heaven art going,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*For charity console us, and declare\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*Whence comest and who art thou; for thou mak’st us\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*As much to marvel at this grace of thine\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*As must a thing that never yet has been.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* And I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Through midst of Tuscany there wanders\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*A streamlet that is born in Falterona,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And not a hundred miles of course suffice it;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*From thereupon do I this body bring.\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*To tell you who I am were speech in vain,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Because my name as yet makes no great noise.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*“If well thy meaning I can penetrate\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*With intellect of mine,”\zsay-e\* then answered me
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* He who first spake, \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*“thou speakest of the Arno.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* And said the other to him: \zsay-s |who="Rinieri da Calboli"\*“Why concealed\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* \zsay-s |who="Rinieri da Calboli"\*This one the appellation of that river,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* \zsay-s |who="Rinieri da Calboli"\*Even as a man doth of things horrible?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* And thus the shade that questioned was of this
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* Himself acquitted: \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*“I know not; but truly\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*’Tis fit the name of such a valley perish;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*For from its fountain-head (where is so pregnant\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*The Alpine mountain whence is cleft Peloro\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*That in few places it that mark surpasses)\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*To where it yields itself in restoration\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*Of what the heaven doth of the sea dry up,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*Whence have the rivers that which goes with them,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*Virtue is like an enemy avoided\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*By all, as is a serpent, through misfortune\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*Of place, or through bad habit that impels them;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*On which account have so transformed their nature\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*The dwellers in that miserable valley,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*It seems that Circe had them in her pasture.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*’Mid ugly swine, of acorns worthier\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*Than other food for human use created,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*It first directeth its impoverished way.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*Curs findeth it thereafter, coming downward,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*More snarling than their puissance demands,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*And turns from them disdainfully its muzzle.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*It goes on falling, and the more it grows,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*The more it finds the dogs becoming wolves,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*This maledict and misadventurous ditch.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*Descended then through many a hollow gulf,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*It finds the foxes so replete with fraud,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*They fear no cunning that may master them.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*Nor will I cease because another hears me;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*And well ’twill be for him, if still he mind him\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*Of what a truthful spirit to me unravels.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*Thy grandson I behold, who doth become\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*A hunter of those wolves upon the bank\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*Of the wild stream, and terrifies them all.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*He sells their flesh, it being yet alive;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*Thereafter slaughters them like ancient beeves;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*Many of life, himself of praise, deprives.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*Blood-stained he issues from the dismal forest;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*He leaves it such, a thousand years from now\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*In its primeval state ’tis not re-wooded.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* As at the announcement of impending ills
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* The face of him who listens is disturbed,
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* From whate’er side the peril seize upon him;
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* So I beheld that other soul, which stood
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* Turned round to listen, grow disturbed and sad,
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* When it had gathered to itself the word.
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* The speech of one and aspect of the other
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* Had me desirous made to know their names,
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* And question mixed with prayers I made thereof,
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* Whereat the spirit which first spake to me
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* Began again: \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*“Thou wishest I should bring me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*To do for thee what thou’lt not do for me;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*But since God willeth that in thee shine forth\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*Such grace of his, I’ll not be chary with thee;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*Know, then, that I Guido del Duca am.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*My blood was so with envy set on fire,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*That if I had beheld a man make merry,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*Thou wouldst have seen me sprinkled o’er with pallor.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*From my own sowing such the straw I reap!\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*O human race! why dost thou set thy heart\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*Where interdict of partnership must be?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*This is Renier; this is the boast and honour\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*Of the house of Calboli, where no one since\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*Has made himself the heir of his desert.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*And not alone his blood is made devoid,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*’Twixt Po and mount, and sea-shore and the Reno,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*Of good required for truth and for diversion;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*For all within these boundaries is full\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*Of venomous roots, so that too tardily\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*By cultivation now would they diminish.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*Where is good Lizio, and Arrigo Manardi,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*Pier Traversaro, and Guido di Carpigna,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*O Romagnuoli into bastards turned?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*When in Bologna will a Fabbro rise?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*When in Faenza a Bernardin di Fosco,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*The noble scion of ignoble seed?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*Be not astonished, Tuscan, if I weep,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*When I remember, with Guido da Prata,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*Ugolin d’ Azzo, who was living with us,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*Frederick Tignoso and his company,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*The house of Traversara, and th’ Anastagi,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*And one race and the other is extinct;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*The dames and cavaliers, the toils and ease\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*That filled our souls with love and courtesy,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*There where the hearts have so malicious grown!\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*O Brettinoro! why dost thou not flee,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*Seeing that all thy family is gone,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*And many people, not to be corrupted?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*Bagnacaval does well in not begetting\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*And ill does Castrocaro, and Conio worse,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*In taking trouble to beget such Counts.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*Will do well the Pagani, when their Devil\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*Shall have departed; but not therefore pure\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*Will testimony of them e’er remain.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*O Ugolin de’ Fantoli, secure\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*Thy name is, since no longer is awaited\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*One who, degenerating, can obscure it!\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*But go now, Tuscan, for it now delights me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*To weep far better than it does to speak,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido del Duca"\*So much has our discourse my mind distressed.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* We were aware that those beloved souls
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* Heard us depart; therefore, by keeping silent,
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* They made us of our pathway confident.
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* When we became alone by going onward,
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* Thunder, when it doth cleave the air, appeared
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* A voice, that counter to us came, exclaiming:
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="A voice"\*“Shall slay me whosoever findeth me!”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* And fled as the reverberation dies
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* If suddenly the cloud asunder bursts.
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* As soon as hearing had a truce from this,
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* Behold another, with so great a crash,
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* That it resembled thunderings following fast:
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* \zsay-s |who="A voice"\*“I am Aglaurus, who became a stone!”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* And then, to press myself close to the Poet,
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* I backward, and not forward, took a step.
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* Already on all sides the air was quiet;
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* And said he to me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“That was the hard curb\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That ought to hold a man within his bounds;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But you take in the bait so that the hook\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 146 \vp 146\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Of the old Adversary draws you to him,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 147 \vp 147\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And hence availeth little curb or call.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 148 \vp 148\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The heavens are calling you, and wheel around you,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 149 \vp 149\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Displaying to you their eternal beauties,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 150 \vp 150\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And still your eye is looking on the ground;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 151 \vp 151\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Whence He, who all discerns, chastises you.”\zsay-e\*
\c 15
\cp 15
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* As much as ’twixt the close of the third hour
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* And dawn of day appeareth of that sphere
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* Which aye in fashion of a child is playing,
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* So much it now appeared, towards the night,
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* Was of his course remaining to the sun;
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* There it was evening, and ’twas midnight here;
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* And the rays smote the middle of our faces,
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Because by us the mount was so encircled,
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* That straight towards the west we now were going
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* When I perceived my forehead overpowered
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* Beneath the splendour far more than at first,
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* And stupor were to me the things unknown,
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* Whereat towards the summit of my brow
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* I raised my hands, and made myself the visor
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* Which the excessive glare diminishes.
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* As when from off the water, or a mirror,
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* The sunbeam leaps unto the opposite side,
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* Ascending upward in the selfsame measure
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* That it descends, and deviates as far
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* From falling of a stone in line direct,
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* (As demonstrate experiment and art,)
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* So it appeared to me that by a light
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* Refracted there before me I was smitten;
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* On which account my sight was swift to flee.
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“What is that, Father sweet, from which I cannot\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*So fully screen my sight that it avail me,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* Said I, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“and seems towards us to be moving?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Marvel thou not, if dazzle thee as yet\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The family of heaven,”\zsay-e\* he answered me;
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“An angel ’tis, who comes to invite us upward.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Soon will it be, that to behold these things\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Shall not be grievous, but delightful to thee\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*As much as nature fashioned thee to feel.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* When we had reached the Angel benedight,
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* With joyful voice he said: \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*“Here enter in\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*To stairway far less steep than are the others.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* We mounting were, already thence departed,
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* And \zsay-s |who="Voices"\*“Beati misericordes”\zsay-e\* was
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* Behind us sung, \zsay-s |who="Voices"\*“Rejoice, thou that o’ercomest!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* My Master and myself, we two alone
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* Were going upward, and I thought, in going,
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* Some profit to acquire from words of his;
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* And I to him directed me, thus asking:
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“What did the spirit of Romagna mean,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Mentioning interdict and partnership?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* Whence he to me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Of his own greatest failing\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*He knows the harm; and therefore wonder not\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*If he reprove us, that we less may rue it.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Because are thither pointed your desires\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Where by companionship each share is lessened,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Envy doth ply the bellows to your sighs.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But if the love of the supernal sphere\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Should upwardly direct your aspiration,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*There would not be that fear within your breast;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*For there, as much the more as one says ‘Our,’\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*So much the more of good each one possesses,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And more of charity in that cloister burns.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“I am more hungering to be satisfied,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* I said, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“than if I had before been silent,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And more of doubt within my mind I gather.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*How can it be, that boon distributed\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*The more possessors can more wealthy make\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Therein, than if by few it be possessed?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* And he to me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Because thou fixest still\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thy mind entirely upon earthly things,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thou pluckest darkness from the very light.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That goodness infinite and ineffable\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Which is above there, runneth unto love,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*As to a lucid body comes the sunbeam.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*So much it gives itself as it finds ardour,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*So that as far as charity extends,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*O’er it increases the eternal valour.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And the more people thitherward aspire,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*More are there to love well, and more they love there,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And, as a mirror, one reflects the other.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And if my reasoning appease thee not,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thou shalt see Beatrice; and she will fully\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Take from thee this and every other longing.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Endeavour, then, that soon may be extinct,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*As are the two already, the five wounds\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That close themselves again by being painful.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* Even as I wished to say, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Thou dost appease me,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* I saw that I had reached another circle,
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* So that my eager eyes made me keep silence.
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* There it appeared to me that in a vision
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* Ecstatic on a sudden I was rapt,
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* And in a temple many persons saw;
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* And at the door a woman, with the sweet
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* Behaviour of a mother, saying: \zsay-s |who="Mary"\*“Son,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Mary"\*Why in this manner hast thou dealt with us?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Mary"\*Lo, sorrowing, thy father and myself\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Mary"\*Were seeking for thee;”\zsay-e\*—and as here she ceased,
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* That which appeared at first had disappeared.
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* Then I beheld another with those waters
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* Adown her cheeks which grief distils whenever
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* From great disdain of others it is born,
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* And saying: \zsay-s |who="The wife of Pisistratus"\*“If of that city thou art lord,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="The wife of Pisistratus"\*For whose name was such strife among the gods,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="The wife of Pisistratus"\*And whence doth every science scintillate,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="The wife of Pisistratus"\*Avenge thyself on those audacious arms\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="The wife of Pisistratus"\*That clasped our daughter, O Pisistratus;”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* And the lord seemed to me benign and mild
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* To answer her with aspect temperate:
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pisistratus"\*“What shall we do to those who wish us ill,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pisistratus"\*If he who loves us be by us condemned?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* Then saw I people hot in fire of wrath,
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* With stones a young man slaying, clamorously
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* Still crying to each other, \zsay-s |who="The mob"\*“Kill him! kill him!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* And him I saw bow down, because of death
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* That weighed already on him, to the earth,
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* But of his eyes made ever gates to heaven,
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* Imploring the high Lord, in so great strife,
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* That he would pardon those his persecutors,
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* With such an aspect as unlocks compassion.
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* Soon as my soul had outwardly returned
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* To things external to it which are true,
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* Did I my not false errors recognize.
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* My Leader, who could see me bear myself
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* Like to a man that rouses him from sleep,
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* Exclaimed: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“What ails thee, that thou canst not stand?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But hast been coming more than half a league\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Veiling thine eyes, and with thy legs entangled,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*In guise of one whom wine or sleep subdues?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“O my sweet Father, if thou listen to me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*I’ll tell thee,”\zsay-e\* said I, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“what appeared to me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*When thus from me my legs were ta’en away.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* And he: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“If thou shouldst have a hundred masks\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Upon thy face, from me would not be shut\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thy cogitations, howsoever small.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*What thou hast seen was that thou mayst not fail\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*To ope thy heart unto the waters of peace,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Which from the eternal fountain are diffused.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*I did not ask, ‘What ails thee?’ as he does\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Who only looketh with the eyes that see not\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*When of the soul bereft the body lies,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But asked it to give vigour to thy feet;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thus must we needs urge on the sluggards, slow\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*To use their wakefulness when it returns.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* We passed along, athwart the twilight peering
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* Forward as far as ever eye could stretch
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* Against the sunbeams serotine and lucent;
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* And lo! by slow degrees a smoke approached
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* In our direction, sombre as the night,
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* Nor was there place to hide one’s self therefrom.
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* This of our eyes and the pure air bereft us.
\c 16
\cp 16
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* Darkness of hell, and of a night deprived
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Of every planet under a poor sky,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* As much as may be tenebrous with cloud,
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* Ne’er made unto my sight so thick a veil,
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* As did that smoke which there enveloped us,
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* Nor to the feeling of so rough a texture;
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* For not an eye it suffered to stay open;
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Whereat mine escort, faithful and sagacious,
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* Drew near to me and offered me his shoulder.
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* E’en as a blind man goes behind his guide,
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* Lest he should wander, or should strike against
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* Aught that may harm or peradventure kill him,
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* So went I through the bitter and foul air,
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* Listening unto my Leader, who said only,
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Look that from me thou be not separated.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* Voices I heard, and every one appeared
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* To supplicate for peace and misericord
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* The Lamb of God who takes away our sins.
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* Still \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*“Agnus Dei”\zsay-e\* their exordium was;
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* One word there was in all, and metre one,
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* So that all harmony appeared among them.
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Master,”\zsay-e\* I said, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“are spirits those I hear?”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* And he to me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Thou apprehendest truly,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And they the knot of anger go unloosing.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*“Now who art thou, that cleavest through our smoke\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*And art discoursing of us even as though\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*Thou didst by calends still divide the time?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* After this manner by a voice was spoken;
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* Whereon my Master said: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Do thou reply,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And ask if on this side the way go upward.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* And I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“O creature that dost cleanse thyself\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*To return beautiful to Him who made thee,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Thou shalt hear marvels if thou follow me.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*“Thee will I follow far as is allowed me,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* He answered; \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*“and if smoke prevent our seeing,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*Hearing shall keep us joined instead thereof.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* Thereon began I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“With that swathing band\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Which death unwindeth am I going upward,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And hither came I through the infernal anguish.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And if God in his grace has me infolded,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*So that he wills that I behold his court\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*By method wholly out of modern usage,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Conceal not from me who ere death thou wast,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*But tell it me, and tell me if I go\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Right for the pass, and be thy words our escort.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*“Lombard was I, and I was Marco called;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*The world I knew, and loved that excellence,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*At which has each one now unbent his bow.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*For mounting upward, thou art going right.”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* Thus he made answer, and subjoined: \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*“I pray thee\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*To pray for me when thou shalt be above.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* And I to him: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“My faith I pledge to thee\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*To do what thou dost ask me; but am bursting\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Inly with doubt, unless I rid me of it.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*First it was simple, and is now made double\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*By thy opinion, which makes certain to me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Here and elsewhere, that which I couple with it.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*The world forsooth is utterly deserted\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*By every virtue, as thou tellest me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And with iniquity is big and covered;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*But I beseech thee point me out the cause,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That I may see it, and to others show it;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*For one in the heavens, and here below one puts it.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* A sigh profound, that grief forced into Ai!
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* He first sent forth, and then began he: \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*“Brother,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*The world is blind, and sooth thou comest from it!\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*Ye who are living every cause refer\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*Still upward to the heavens, as if all things\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*They of necessity moved with themselves.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*If this were so, in you would be destroyed\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*Free will, nor any justice would there be\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*In having joy for good, or grief for evil.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*The heavens your movements do initiate,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*I say not all; but granting that I say it,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*Light has been given you for good and evil,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*And free volition; which, if some fatigue\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*In the first battles with the heavens it suffers,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*Afterwards conquers all, if well ’tis nurtured.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*To greater force and to a better nature,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*Though free, ye subject are, and that creates\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*The mind in you the heavens have not in charge.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*Hence, if the present world doth go astray,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*In you the cause is, be it sought in you;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*And I therein will now be thy true spy.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*Forth from the hand of Him, who fondles it\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*Before it is, like to a little girl\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*Weeping and laughing in her childish sport,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*Issues the simple soul, that nothing knows,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*Save that, proceeding from a joyous Maker,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*Gladly it turns to that which gives it pleasure.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*Of trivial good at first it tastes the savour;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*Is cheated by it, and runs after it,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*If guide or rein turn not aside its love.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*Hence it behoved laws for a rein to place,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*Behoved a king to have, who at the least\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*Of the true city should discern the tower.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*The laws exist, but who sets hand to them?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*No one; because the shepherd who precedes\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*Can ruminate, but cleaveth not the hoof;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*Wherefore the people that perceives its guide\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*Strike only at the good for which it hankers,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*Feeds upon that, and farther seeketh not.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*Clearly canst thou perceive that evil guidance\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*The cause is that has made the world depraved,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*And not that nature is corrupt in you.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*Rome, that reformed the world, accustomed was\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*Two suns to have, which one road and the other,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*Of God and of the world, made manifest.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*One has the other quenched, and to the crosier\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*The sword is joined, and ill beseemeth it\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*That by main force one with the other go,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*Because, being joined, one feareth not the other;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*If thou believe not, think upon the grain,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*For by its seed each herb is recognized.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*In the land laved by Po and Adige,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*Valour and courtesy used to be found,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*Before that Frederick had his controversy;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*Now in security can pass that way\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*Whoever will abstain, through sense of shame,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*From speaking with the good, or drawing near them.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*True, three old men are left, in whom upbraids\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*The ancient age the new, and late they deem it\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*That God restore them to the better life:\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*Currado da Palazzo, and good Gherardo,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*And Guido da Castel, who better named is,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*In fashion of the French, the simple Lombard:\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*Say thou henceforward that the Church of Rome,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*Confounding in itself two governments,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*Falls in the mire, and soils itself and burden.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“O Marco mine,”\zsay-e\* I said, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“thou reasonest well;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And now discern I why the sons of Levi\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Have been excluded from the heritage.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*But what Gherardo is it, who, as sample\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Of a lost race, thou sayest has remained\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*In reprobation of the barbarous age?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*“Either thy speech deceives me, or it tempts me,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* He answered me; \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*“for speaking Tuscan to me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*It seems of good Gherardo naught thou knowest.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*By other surname do I know him not,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*Unless I take it from his daughter Gaia.\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*May God be with you, for I come no farther.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*Behold the dawn, that through the smoke rays out,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*Already whitening; and I must depart—\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* \zsay-s |who="Marco Lombardo"\*Yonder the Angel is—ere he appear.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* Thus did he speak, and would no farther hear me.
\c 17
\cp 17
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* Remember, Reader, if e’er in the Alps
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* A mist o’ertook thee, through which thou couldst see
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* Not otherwise than through its membrane mole,
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* How, when the vapours humid and condensed
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* Begin to dissipate themselves, the sphere
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* Of the sun feebly enters in among them,
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* And thy imagination will be swift
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* In coming to perceive how I re-saw
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* The sun at first, that was already setting.
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* Thus, to the faithful footsteps of my Master
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* Mating mine own, I issued from that cloud
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* To rays already dead on the low shores.
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* O thou, Imagination, that dost steal us
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* So from without sometimes, that man perceives not,
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* Although around may sound a thousand trumpets,
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* Who moveth thee, if sense impel thee not?
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* Moves thee a light, which in the heaven takes form,
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* By self, or by a will that downward guides it.
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* Of her impiety, who changed her form
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* Into the bird that most delights in singing,
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* In my imagining appeared the trace;
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* And hereupon my mind was so withdrawn
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* Within itself, that from without there came
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* Nothing that then might be received by it.
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* Then reigned within my lofty fantasy
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* One crucified, disdainful and ferocious
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* In countenance, and even thus was dying.
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* Around him were the great Ahasuerus,
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* Esther his wife, and the just Mordecai,
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* Who was in word and action so entire.
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* And even as this image burst asunder
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* Of its own self, in fashion of a bubble
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* In which the water it was made of fails,
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* There rose up in my vision a young maiden
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* Bitterly weeping, and she said: \zsay-s |who="Lavinia"\*“O queen,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Lavinia"\*Why hast thou wished in anger to be naught?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="Lavinia"\*Thou’st slain thyself, Lavinia not to lose;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Lavinia"\*Now hast thou lost me; I am she who mourns,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Lavinia"\*Mother, at thine ere at another’s ruin.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* As sleep is broken, when upon a sudden
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* New light strikes in upon the eyelids closed,
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* And broken quivers ere it dieth wholly,
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* So this imagining of mine fell down
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* As soon as the effulgence smote my face,
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* Greater by far than what is in our wont.
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* I turned me round to see where I might be,
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* When said a voice, \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*“Here is the passage up;”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* Which from all other purposes removed me,
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* And made my wish so full of eagerness
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* To look and see who was it that was speaking,
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* It never rests till meeting face to face;
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* But as before the sun, which quells the sight,
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* And in its own excess its figure veils,
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* Even so my power was insufficient here.
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“This is a spirit divine, who in the way\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Of going up directs us without asking,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And who with his own light himself conceals.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*He does with us as man doth with himself;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*For he who sees the need, and waits the asking,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Malignly leans already tow’rds denial.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Accord we now our feet to such inviting,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Let us make haste to mount ere it grow dark;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*For then we could not till the day return.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* Thus my Conductor said; and I and he
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* Together turned our footsteps to a stairway;
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* And I, as soon as the first step I reached,
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* Near me perceived a motion as of wings,
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* And fanning in the face, and saying, \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*“‘Beati\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*Pacifici,’ who are without ill anger.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* Already over us were so uplifted
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* The latest sunbeams, which the night pursues,
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* That upon many sides the stars appeared.
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“O manhood mine, why dost thou vanish so?”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* I said within myself; for I perceived
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* The vigour of my legs was put in truce.
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* We at the point were where no more ascends
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* The stairway upward, and were motionless,
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* Even as a ship, which at the shore arrives;
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* And I gave heed a little, if I might hear
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* Aught whatsoever in the circle new;
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* Then to my Master turned me round and said:
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Say, my sweet Father, what delinquency\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Is purged here in the circle where we are?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Although our feet may pause, pause not thy speech.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* And he to me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“The love of good, remiss\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*In what it should have done, is here restored;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Here plied again the ill-belated oar;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But still more openly to understand,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Turn unto me thy mind, and thou shalt gather\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Some profitable fruit from our delay.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Neither Creator nor a creature ever,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Son,”\zsay-e\* he began, \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“was destitute of love\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Natural or spiritual; and thou knowest it.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The natural was ever without error;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But err the other may by evil object,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Or by too much, or by too little vigour.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*While in the first it well directed is,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And in the second moderates itself,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*It cannot be the cause of sinful pleasure;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But when to ill it turns, and, with more care\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Or lesser than it ought, runs after good,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*’Gainst the Creator works his own creation.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Hence thou mayst comprehend that love must be\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The seed within yourselves of every virtue,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And every act that merits punishment.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Now inasmuch as never from the welfare\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Of its own subject can love turn its sight,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*From their own hatred all things are secure;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And since we cannot think of any being\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Standing alone, nor from the First divided,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Of hating Him is all desire cut off.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Hence if, discriminating, I judge well,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The evil that one loves is of one’s neighbour,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And this is born in three modes in your clay.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*There are, who, by abasement of their neighbour,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Hope to excel, and therefore only long\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That from his greatness he may be cast down;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*There are, who power, grace, honour, and renown\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Fear they may lose because another rises,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thence are so sad that the reverse they love;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And there are those whom injury seems to chafe,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*So that it makes them greedy for revenge,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And such must needs shape out another’s harm.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*This threefold love is wept for down below;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Now of the other will I have thee hear,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That runneth after good with measure faulty.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Each one confusedly a good conceives\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Wherein the mind may rest, and longeth for it;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Therefore to overtake it each one strives.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*If languid love to look on this attract you,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Or in attaining unto it, this cornice,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*After just penitence, torments you for it.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*There’s other good that does not make man happy;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*’Tis not felicity, ’tis not the good\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Essence, of every good the fruit and root.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The love that yields itself too much to this\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Above us is lamented in three circles;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But how tripartite it may be described,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*I say not, that thou seek it for thyself.”\zsay-e\*
\c 18
\cp 18
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* An end had put unto his reasoning
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* The lofty Teacher, and attent was looking
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* Into my face, if I appeared content;
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* And I, whom a new thirst still goaded on,
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* Without was mute, and said within: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Perchance\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*The too much questioning I make annoys him.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* But that true Father, who had comprehended
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* The timid wish, that opened not itself,
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* By speaking gave me hardihood to speak.
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* Whence I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“My sight is, Master, vivified\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*So in thy light, that clearly I discern\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Whate’er thy speech importeth or describes.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Therefore I thee entreat, sweet Father dear,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*To teach me love, to which thou dost refer\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Every good action and its contrary.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Direct,”\zsay-e\* he said, \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“towards me the keen eyes\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Of intellect, and clear will be to thee\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The error of the blind, who would be leaders.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The soul, which is created apt to love,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Is mobile unto everything that pleases,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Soon as by pleasure she is waked to action.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Your apprehension from some real thing\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*An image draws, and in yourselves displays it\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*So that it makes the soul turn unto it.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And if, when turned, towards it she incline,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Love is that inclination; it is nature,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Which is by pleasure bound in you anew\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Then even as the fire doth upward move\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*By its own form, which to ascend is born,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Where longest in its matter it endures,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*So comes the captive soul into desire,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Which is a motion spiritual, and ne’er rests\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Until she doth enjoy the thing beloved.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Now may apparent be to thee how hidden\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The truth is from those people, who aver\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*All love is in itself a laudable thing;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Because its matter may perchance appear\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Aye to be good; but yet not each impression\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Is good, albeit good may be the wax.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Thy words, and my sequacious intellect,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* I answered him, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“have love revealed to me;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*But that has made me more impregned with doubt;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*For if love from without be offered us,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And with another foot the soul go not,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*If right or wrong she go, ’tis not her merit.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* And he to me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“What reason seeth here,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Myself can tell thee; beyond that await\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*For Beatrice, since ’tis a work of faith.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Every substantial form, that segregate\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*From matter is, and with it is united,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Specific power has in itself collected,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Which without act is not perceptible,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Nor shows itself except by its effect,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*As life does in a plant by the green leaves.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But still, whence cometh the intelligence\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Of the first notions, man is ignorant,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And the affection for the first allurements,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Which are in you as instinct in the bee\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*To make its honey; and this first desire\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Merit of praise or blame containeth not.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Now, that to this all others may be gathered,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Innate within you is the power that counsels,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And it should keep the threshold of assent.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*This is the principle, from which is taken\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Occasion of desert in you, according\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*As good and guilty loves it takes and winnows.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Those who, in reasoning, to the bottom went,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Were of this innate liberty aware,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Therefore bequeathed they Ethics to the world.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Supposing, then, that from necessity\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Springs every love that is within you kindled,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Within yourselves the power is to restrain it.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The noble virtue Beatrice understands\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*By the free will; and therefore see that thou\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Bear it in mind, if she should speak of it.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* The moon, belated almost unto midnight,
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* Now made the stars appear to us more rare,
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* Formed like a bucket, that is all ablaze,
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* And counter to the heavens ran through those paths
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* Which the sun sets aflame, when he of Rome
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* Sees it ’twixt Sardes and Corsicans go down;
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* And that patrician shade, for whom is named
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* Pietola more than any Mantuan town,
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* Had laid aside the burden of my lading;
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* Whence I, who reason manifest and plain
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* In answer to my questions had received,
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* Stood like a man in drowsy reverie.
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* But taken from me was this drowsiness
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* Suddenly by a people, that behind
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* Our backs already had come round to us.
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* And as, of old, Ismenus and Asopus
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* Beside them saw at night the rush and throng,
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* If but the Thebans were in need of Bacchus,
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* So they along that circle curve their step,
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* From what I saw of those approaching us,
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* Who by good-will and righteous love are ridden.
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* Full soon they were upon us, because running
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* Moved onward all that mighty multitude,
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* And two in the advance cried out, lamenting,
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Two souls"\*“Mary in haste unto the mountain ran,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Two souls"\*And Caesar, that he might subdue Ilerda,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Two souls"\*Thrust at Marseilles, and then ran into Spain.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*“Quick! quick! so that the time may not be lost\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*By little love!”\zsay-e\* forthwith the others cried,
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*“For ardour in well-doing freshens grace!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“O folk, in whom an eager fervour now\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Supplies perhaps delay and negligence,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Put by you in well-doing, through lukewarmness,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*This one who lives, and truly I lie not,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Would fain go up, if but the sun relight us;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*So tell us where the passage nearest is.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* These were the words of him who was my Guide;
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* And some one of those spirits said: \zsay-s |who="The Abbot of San Zeno"\*“Come on\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Abbot of San Zeno"\*Behind us, and the opening shalt thou find;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Abbot of San Zeno"\*So full of longing are we to move onward,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Abbot of San Zeno"\*That stay we cannot; therefore pardon us,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Abbot of San Zeno"\*If thou for churlishness our justice take.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Abbot of San Zeno"\*I was San Zeno’s Abbot at Verona,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Abbot of San Zeno"\*Under the empire of good Barbarossa,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Abbot of San Zeno"\*Of whom still sorrowing Milan holds discourse;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Abbot of San Zeno"\*And he has one foot in the grave already,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Abbot of San Zeno"\*Who shall erelong lament that monastery,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Abbot of San Zeno"\*And sorry be of having there had power,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Abbot of San Zeno"\*Because his son, in his whole body sick,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Abbot of San Zeno"\*And worse in mind, and who was evil-born,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Abbot of San Zeno"\*He put into the place of its true pastor.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* If more he said, or silent was, I know not,
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* He had already passed so far beyond us;
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* But this I heard, and to retain it pleased me.
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* And he who was in every need my succour
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* Said: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Turn thee hitherward; see two of them\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Come fastening upon slothfulness their teeth.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* In rear of all they shouted: \zsay-s |who="Two souls"\*“Sooner were\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Two souls"\*The people dead to whom the sea was opened,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Two souls"\*Than their inheritors the Jordan saw;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Two souls"\*And those who the fatigue did not endure\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Two souls"\*Unto the issue, with Anchises’ son,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Two souls"\*Themselves to life withouten glory offered.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* Then when from us so separated were
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* Those shades, that they no longer could be seen,
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* Within me a new thought did entrance find,
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* Whence others many and diverse were born;
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* And so I lapsed from one into another,
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* That in a reverie mine eyes I closed,
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* And meditation into dream transmuted.
\c 19
\cp 19
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* It was the hour when the diurnal heat
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* No more can warm the coldness of the moon,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* Vanquished by earth, or peradventure Saturn,
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* When geomancers their Fortuna Major
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* See in the orient before the dawn
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* Rise by a path that long remains not dim,
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* There came to me in dreams a stammering woman,
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Squint in her eyes, and in her feet distorted,
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* With hands dissevered and of sallow hue.
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* I looked at her; and as the sun restores
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* The frigid members which the night benumbs,
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* Even thus my gaze did render voluble
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* Her tongue, and made her all erect thereafter
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* In little while, and the lost countenance
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* As love desires it so in her did colour.
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* When in this wise she had her speech unloosed,
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* She ’gan to sing so, that with difficulty
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* Could I have turned my thoughts away from her.
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Siren"\*“I am,”\zsay-e\* she sang, \zsay-s |who="The Siren"\*“I am the Siren sweet\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Siren"\*Who mariners amid the main unman,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Siren"\*So full am I of pleasantness to hear.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Siren"\*I drew Ulysses from his wandering way\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Siren"\*Unto my song, and he who dwells with me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Siren"\*Seldom departs so wholly I content him.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* Her mouth was not yet closed again, before
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* Appeared a Lady saintly and alert
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* Close at my side to put her to confusion.
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* \zsay-s |who="A holy lady"\*“Virgilius, O Virgilius! who is this?”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* Sternly she said; and he was drawing near
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* With eyes still fixed upon that modest one.
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* She seized the other and in front laid open,
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* Rending her garments, and her belly showed me;
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* This waked me with the stench that issued from it.
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* I turned mine eyes, and good Virgilius said:
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“At least thrice have I called thee; rise and come;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Find we the opening by which thou mayst enter.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* I rose; and full already of high day
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* Were all the circles of the Sacred Mountain,
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* And with the new sun at our back we went.
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* Following behind him, I my forehead bore
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* Like unto one who has it laden with thought,
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* Who makes himself the half arch of a bridge,
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* When I heard say, \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*“Come, here the passage is,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* Spoken in a manner gentle and benign,
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* Such as we hear not in this mortal region.
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* With open wings, which of a swan appeared,
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* Upward he turned us who thus spake to us,
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* Between the two walls of the solid granite.
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* He moved his pinions afterwards and fanned us,
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* Affirming those ‘qui lugent’ to be blessed,
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* For they shall have their souls with comfort filled.
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“What aileth thee, that aye to earth thou gazest?”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* To me my Guide began to say, we both
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* Somewhat beyond the Angel having mounted.
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* And I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“With such misgiving makes me go\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*A vision new, which bends me to itself,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*So that I cannot from the thought withdraw me.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Didst thou behold,”\zsay-e\* he said, \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“that old enchantress,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Who sole above us henceforth is lamented?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Didst thou behold how man is freed from her?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Suffice it thee, and smite earth with thy heels,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thine eyes lift upward to the lure, that whirls\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The Eternal King with revolutions vast.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* Even as the hawk, that first his feet surveys,
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* Then turns him to the call and stretches forward,
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* Through the desire of food that draws him thither,
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* Such I became, and such, as far as cleaves
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* The rock to give a way to him who mounts,
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* Went on to where the circling doth begin.
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* On the fifth circle when I had come forth,
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* People I saw upon it who were weeping,
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* Stretched prone upon the ground, all downward turned.
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*“Adhaesit pavimento anima mea,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* I heard them say with sighings so profound,
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* That hardly could the words be understood.
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“O ye elect of God, whose sufferings\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Justice and Hope both render less severe,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Direct ye us towards the high ascents.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*“If ye are come secure from this prostration,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*And wish to find the way most speedily,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*Let your right hands be evermore outside.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* Thus did the Poet ask, and thus was answered
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* By them somewhat in front of us; whence I
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* In what was spoken divined the rest concealed,
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* And unto my Lord’s eyes mine eyes I turned;
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* Whence he assented with a cheerful sign
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* To what the sight of my desire implored.
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* When of myself I could dispose at will,
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* Above that creature did I draw myself,
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* Whose words before had caused me to take note,
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* Saying: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“O Spirit, in whom weeping ripens\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That without which to God we cannot turn,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Suspend awhile for me thy greater care.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Who wast thou, and why are your backs turned upwards,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Tell me, and if thou wouldst that I procure thee\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Anything there whence living I departed.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* And he to me: \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*“Wherefore our backs the heaven\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*Turns to itself, know shalt thou; but beforehand\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*‘Scias quod ego fui successor Petri.’\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*Between Siestri and Chiaveri descends\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*A river beautiful, and of its name\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*The title of my blood its summit makes.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*A month and little more essayed I how\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*Weighs the great cloak on him from mire who keeps it,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*For all the other burdens seem a feather.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*Tardy, ah woe is me! was my conversion;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*But when the Roman Shepherd I was made,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*Then I discovered life to be a lie.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*I saw that there the heart was not at rest,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*Nor farther in that life could one ascend;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*Whereby the love of this was kindled in me.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*Until that time a wretched soul and parted\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*From God was I, and wholly avaricious;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*Now, as thou seest, I here am punished for it.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*What avarice does is here made manifest\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*In the purgation of these souls converted,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*And no more bitter pain the Mountain has.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*Even as our eye did not uplift itself\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*Aloft, being fastened upon earthly things,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*So justice here has merged it in the earth.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*As avarice had extinguished our affection\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*For every good, whereby was action lost,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*So justice here doth hold us in restraint,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*Bound and imprisoned by the feet and hands;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*And so long as it pleases the just Lord\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*Shall we remain immovable and prostrate.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* I on my knees had fallen, and wished to speak;
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* But even as I began, and he was ’ware,
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* Only by listening, of my reverence,
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*“What cause,”\zsay-e\* he said, \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*“has downward bent thee thus?”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* And I to him: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“For your own dignity,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Standing, my conscience stung me with remorse.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*“Straighten thy legs, and upward raise thee, brother,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* He answered: \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*“Err not, fellow-servant am I\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*With thee and with the others to one power.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*If e’er that holy, evangelic sound,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*Which sayeth ‘neque nubent,’ thou hast heard,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*Well canst thou see why in this wise I speak.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*Now go; no longer will I have thee linger,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*Because thy stay doth incommode my weeping,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*With which I ripen that which thou hast said.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*On earth I have a grandchild named Alagia,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*Good in herself, unless indeed our house\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*Malevolent may make her by example,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* \zsay-s |who="Adrian V"\*And she alone remains to me on earth.”\zsay-e\*
\c 20
\cp 20
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* Ill strives the will against a better will;
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Therefore, to pleasure him, against my pleasure
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* I drew the sponge not saturate from the water.
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* Onward I moved, and onward moved my Leader,
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* Through vacant places, skirting still the rock,
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* As on a wall close to the battlements;
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* For they that through their eyes pour drop by drop
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* The malady which all the world pervades,
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* On the other side too near the verge approach.
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* Accursed mayst thou be, thou old she-wolf,
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* That more than all the other beasts hast prey,
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* Because of hunger infinitely hollow!
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* O heaven, in whose gyrations some appear
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* To think conditions here below are changed,
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* When will he come through whom she shall depart?
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* Onward we went with footsteps slow and scarce,
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* And I attentive to the shades I heard
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* Piteously weeping and bemoaning them;
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* And I by peradventure heard \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*“Sweet Mary!”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* Uttered in front of us amid the weeping
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* Even as a woman does who is in child-birth;
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* And in continuance: \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*“How poor thou wast\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*Is manifested by that hostelry\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*Where thou didst lay thy sacred burden down.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* Thereafterward I heard: \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*“O good Fabricius,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*Virtue with poverty didst thou prefer\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*To the possession of great wealth with vice.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* So pleasurable were these words to me
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* That I drew farther onward to have knowledge
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* Touching that spirit whence they seemed to come.
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* He furthermore was speaking of the largess
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* Which Nicholas unto the maidens gave,
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* In order to conduct their youth to honour.
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“O soul that dost so excellently speak,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Tell me who wast thou,”\zsay-e\* said I, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“and why only\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Thou dost renew these praises well deserved?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Not without recompense shall be thy word,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*If I return to finish the short journey\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Of that life which is flying to its end.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* And he: \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*“I’ll tell thee, not for any comfort\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*I may expect from earth, but that so much\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*Grace shines in thee or ever thou art dead.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*I was the root of that malignant plant\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*Which overshadows all the Christian world,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*So that good fruit is seldom gathered from it;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*But if Douay and Ghent, and Lille and Bruges\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*Had Power, soon vengeance would be taken on it;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*And this I pray of Him who judges all.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*Hugh Capet was I called upon the earth;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*From me were born the Louises and Philips,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*By whom in later days has France been governed.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*I was the son of a Parisian butcher,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*What time the ancient kings had perished all,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*Excepting one, contrite in cloth of gray.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*I found me grasping in my hands the rein\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*Of the realm’s government, and so great power\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*Of new acquest, and so with friends abounding,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*That to the widowed diadem promoted\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*The head of mine own offspring was, from whom\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*The consecrated bones of these began.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*So long as the great dowry of Provence\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*Out of my blood took not the sense of shame,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*’Twas little worth, but still it did no harm.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*Then it began with falsehood and with force\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*Its rapine; and thereafter, for amends,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*Took Ponthieu, Normandy, and Gascony.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*Charles came to Italy, and for amends\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*A victim made of Conradin, and then\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*Thrust Thomas back to heaven, for amends.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*A time I see, not very distant now,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*Which draweth forth another Charles from France,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*The better to make known both him and his.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*Unarmed he goes, and only with the lance\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*That Judas jousted with; and that he thrusts\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*So that he makes the paunch of Florence burst.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*He thence not land, but sin and infamy,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*Shall gain, so much more grievous to himself\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*As the more light such damage he accounts.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*The other, now gone forth, ta’en in his ship,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*See I his daughter sell, and chaffer for her\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*As corsairs do with other female slaves.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*What more, O Avarice, canst thou do to us,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*Since thou my blood so to thyself hast drawn,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*It careth not for its own proper flesh?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*That less may seem the future ill and past,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*I see the flower-de-luce Alagna enter,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*And Christ in his own Vicar captive made.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*I see him yet another time derided;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*I see renewed the vinegar and gall,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*And between living thieves I see him slain.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*I see the modern Pilate so relentless,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*This does not sate him, but without decretal\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*He to the temple bears his sordid sails!\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*When, O my Lord! shall I be joyful made\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*By looking on the vengeance which, concealed,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*Makes sweet thine anger in thy secrecy?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*What I was saying of that only bride\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*Of the Holy Ghost, and which occasioned thee\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*To turn towards me for some commentary,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*So long has been ordained to all our prayers\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*As the day lasts; but when the night comes on,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*Contrary sound we take instead thereof.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*At that time we repeat Pygmalion,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*Of whom a traitor, thief, and parricide\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*Made his insatiable desire of gold;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*And the misery of avaricious Midas,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*That followed his inordinate demand,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*At which forevermore one needs but laugh.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*The foolish Achan each one then records,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*And how he stole the spoils; so that the wrath\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*Of Joshua still appears to sting him here.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*Then we accuse Sapphira with her husband,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*We laud the hoof-beats Heliodorus had,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*And the whole mount in infamy encircles\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*Polymnestor who murdered Polydorus.\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*Here finally is cried: ‘O Crassus, tell us,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*For thou dost know, what is the taste of gold?’\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*Sometimes we speak, one loud, another low,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*According to desire of speech, that spurs us\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*To greater now and now to lesser pace.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*But in the good that here by day is talked of,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*Erewhile alone I was not; yet near by\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Hugh Capet"\*No other person lifted up his voice.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* From him already we departed were,
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* And made endeavour to o’ercome the road
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* As much as was permitted to our power,
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* When I perceived, like something that is falling,
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* The mountain tremble, whence a chill seized on me,
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* As seizes him who to his death is going.
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* Certes so violently shook not Delos,
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* Before Latona made her nest therein
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* To give birth to the two eyes of the heaven.
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* Then upon all sides there began a cry,
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* Such that the Master drew himself towards me,
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* Saying, \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Fear not, while I am guiding thee.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*“Gloria in excelsis Deo,”\zsay-e\* all
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* Were saying, from what near I comprehended,
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* Where it was possible to hear the cry.
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* We paused immovable and in suspense,
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* Even as the shepherds who first heard that song,
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* Until the trembling ceased, and it was finished.
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* Then we resumed again our holy path,
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* Watching the shades that lay upon the ground,
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* Already turned to their accustomed plaint.
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* No ignorance ever with so great a strife
\q1 \v 146 \vp 146\vp* Had rendered me importunate to know,
\q1 \v 147 \vp 147\vp* If erreth not in this my memory,
\b
\q1 \v 148 \vp 148\vp* As meditating then I seemed to have;
\q1 \v 149 \vp 149\vp* Nor out of haste to question did I dare,
\q1 \v 150 \vp 150\vp* Nor of myself I there could aught perceive;
\b
\q1 \v 151 \vp 151\vp* So I went onward timorous and thoughtful.
\c 21
\cp 21
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* The natural thirst, that ne’er is satisfied
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Excepting with the water for whose grace
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* The woman of Samaria besought,
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* Put me in travail, and haste goaded me
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* Along the encumbered path behind my Leader
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* And I was pitying that righteous vengeance;
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* And lo! in the same manner as Luke writeth
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* That Christ appeared to two upon the way
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* From the sepulchral cave already risen,
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* A shade appeared to us, and came behind us,
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* Down gazing on the prostrate multitude,
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* Nor were we ware of it, until it spake,
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* Saying, \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*“My brothers, may God give you peace!”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* We turned us suddenly, and Virgilius rendered
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* To him the countersign thereto conforming.
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* Thereon began he: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“In the blessed council,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thee may the court veracious place in peace,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That me doth banish in eternal exile!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*“How,”\zsay-e\* said he, and the while we went with speed,
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*“If ye are shades whom God deigns not on high,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Who up his stairs so far has guided you?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* And said my Teacher: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“If thou note the marks\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Which this one bears, and which the Angel traces\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Well shalt thou see he with the good must reign.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But because she who spinneth day and night\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*For him had not yet drawn the distaff off,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Which Clotho lays for each one and compacts,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*His soul, which is thy sister and my own,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*In coming upwards could not come alone,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*By reason that it sees not in our fashion.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Whence I was drawn from out the ample throat\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Of Hell to be his guide, and I shall guide him\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*As far on as my school has power to lead.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But tell us, if thou knowest, why such a shudder\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Erewhile the mountain gave, and why together\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*All seemed to cry, as far as its moist feet?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* In asking he so hit the very eye
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* Of my desire, that merely with the hope
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* My thirst became the less unsatisfied.
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*“Naught is there,”\zsay-e\* he began, \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*“that without order\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*May the religion of the mountain feel,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Nor aught that may be foreign to its custom.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Free is it here from every permutation;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*What from itself heaven in itself receiveth\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Can be of this the cause, and naught beside;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Because that neither rain, nor hail, nor snow,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Nor dew, nor hoar-frost any higher falls\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Than the short, little stairway of three steps.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Dense clouds do not appear, nor rarefied,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Nor coruscation, nor the daughter of Thaumas,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*That often upon earth her region shifts;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*No arid vapour any farther rises\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Than to the top of the three steps I spake of,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Whereon the Vicar of Peter has his feet.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Lower down perchance it trembles less or more,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*But, for the wind that in the earth is hidden\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*I know not how, up here it never trembled.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*It trembles here, whenever any soul\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Feels itself pure, so that it soars, or moves\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*To mount aloft, and such a cry attends it.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Of purity the will alone gives proof,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Which, being wholly free to change its convent,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Takes by surprise the soul, and helps it fly.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*First it wills well; but the desire permits not,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Which divine justice with the self-same will\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*There was to sin, upon the torment sets.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*And I, who have been lying in this pain\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Five hundred years and more, but just now felt\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*A free volition for a better seat.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Therefore thou heardst the earthquake, and the pious\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Spirits along the mountain rendering praise\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Unto the Lord, that soon he speed them upwards.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* So said he to him; and since we enjoy
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* As much in drinking as the thirst is great,
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* I could not say how much it did me good.
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* And the wise Leader: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Now I see the net\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That snares you here, and how ye are set free,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Why the earth quakes, and wherefore ye rejoice.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Now who thou wast be pleased that I may know;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And why so many centuries thou hast here\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Been lying, let me gather from thy words.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*“In days when the good Titus, with the aid\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Of the supremest King, avenged the wounds\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Whence issued forth the blood by Judas sold,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Under the name that most endures and honours,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Was I on earth,”\zsay-e\* that spirit made reply,
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*“Greatly renowned, but not with faith as yet.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*My vocal spirit was so sweet, that Rome\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Me, a Thoulousian, drew unto herself,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Where I deserved to deck my brows with myrtle.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Statius the people name me still on earth;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*I sang of Thebes, and then of great Achilles;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*But on the way fell with my second burden.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*The seeds unto my ardour were the sparks\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Of that celestial flame which heated me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Whereby more than a thousand have been fired;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Of the Aeneid speak I, which to me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*A mother was, and was my nurse in song;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Without this weighed I not a drachma’s weight.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*And to have lived upon the earth what time\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Virgilius lived, I would accept one sun\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*More than I must ere issuing from my ban.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* These words towards me made Virgilius turn
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* With looks that in their silence said, \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Be silent!”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* But yet the power that wills cannot do all things;
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* For tears and laughter are such pursuivants
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* Unto the passion from which each springs forth,
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* In the most truthful least the will they follow.
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* I only smiled, as one who gives the wink;
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* Whereat the shade was silent, and it gazed
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* Into mine eyes, where most expression dwells;
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* And, \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*“As thou well mayst consummate a labour\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*So great,”\zsay-e\* it said, \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*“why did thy face just now\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Display to me the lightning of a smile?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* Now am I caught on this side and on that;
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* One keeps me silent, one to speak conjures me,
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* Wherefore I sigh, and I am understood.
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Speak,”\zsay-e\* said my Master, \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“and be not afraid\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Of speaking, but speak out, and say to him\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*What he demands with such solicitude.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* Whence I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Thou peradventure marvellest,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*O antique spirit, at the smile I gave;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*But I will have more wonder seize upon thee.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*This one, who guides on high these eyes of mine,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Is that Virgilius, from whom thou didst learn\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*To sing aloud of men and of the Gods.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*If other cause thou to my smile imputedst,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Abandon it as false, and trust it was\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Those words which thou hast spoken concerning him.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* Already he was stooping to embrace
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* My Teacher’s feet; but he said to him: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Brother,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Do not; for shade thou art, and shade beholdest.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* And he uprising: \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*“Now canst thou the sum\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Of love which warms me to thee comprehend,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*When this our vanity I disremember,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Treating a shadow as substantial thing.”\zsay-e\*
\c 22
\cp 22
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* Already was the Angel left behind us,
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* The Angel who to the sixth round had turned us,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* Having erased one mark from off my face;
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* And those who have in justice their desire
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* Had said to us, \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*“Beati,”\zsay-e\* in their voices,
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* With \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*“sitio,”\zsay-e\* and without more ended it.
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* And I, more light than through the other passes,
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Went onward so, that without any labour
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* I followed upward the swift-footed spirits;
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* When thus Virgilius began: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“The love\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Kindled by virtue aye another kindles,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Provided outwardly its flame appear.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Hence from the hour that Juvenal descended\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Among us into the infernal Limbo,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Who made apparent to me thy affection,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*My kindliness towards thee was as great\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*As ever bound one to an unseen person,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*So that these stairs will now seem short to me.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But tell me, and forgive me as a friend,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*If too great confidence let loose the rein,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And as a friend now hold discourse with me;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*How was it possible within thy breast\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*For avarice to find place, ’mid so much wisdom\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*As thou wast filled with by thy diligence?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* These words excited Statius at first
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* Somewhat to laughter; afterward he answered:
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*“Each word of thine is love’s dear sign to me.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Verily oftentimes do things appear\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Which give fallacious matter to our doubts,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Instead of the true causes which are hidden!\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Thy question shows me thy belief to be\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*That I was niggard in the other life,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*It may be from the circle where I was;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Therefore know thou, that avarice was removed\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Too far from me; and this extravagance\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Thousands of lunar periods have punished.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*And were it not that I my thoughts uplifted,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*When I the passage heard where thou exclaimest,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*As if indignant, unto human nature,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*‘To what impellest thou not, O cursed hunger\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Of gold, the appetite of mortal men?’\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Revolving I should feel the dismal joustings.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Then I perceived the hands could spread too wide\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Their wings in spending, and repented me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*As well of that as of my other sins;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*How many with shorn hair shall rise again\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Because of ignorance, which from this sin\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Cuts off repentance living and in death!\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*And know that the transgression which rebuts\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*By direct opposition any sin\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Together with it here its verdure dries.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Therefore if I have been among that folk\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Which mourns its avarice, to purify me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*For its opposite has this befallen me.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Now when thou sangest the relentless weapons\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Of the twofold affliction of Jocasta,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* The singer of the Songs Bucolic said,
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“From that which Clio there with thee preludes,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*It does not seem that yet had made thee faithful\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That faith without which no good works suffice.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*If this be so, what candles or what sun\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Scattered thy darkness so that thou didst trim\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thy sails behind the Fisherman thereafter?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* And he to him: \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*“Thou first directedst me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Towards Parnassus, in its grots to drink,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*And first concerning God didst me enlighten.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Thou didst as he who walketh in the night,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Who bears his light behind, which helps him not,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*But wary makes the persons after him,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*When thou didst say: ‘The age renews itself,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Justice returns, and man’s primeval time,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*And a new progeny descends from heaven.’\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Through thee I Poet was, through thee a Christian;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*But that thou better see what I design,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*To colour it will I extend my hand.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Already was the world in every part\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Pregnant with the true creed, disseminated\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*By messengers of the eternal kingdom;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*And thy assertion, spoken of above,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*With the new preachers was in unison;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Whence I to visit them the custom took.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Then they became so holy in my sight,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*That, when Domitian persecuted them,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Not without tears of mine were their laments;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*And all the while that I on earth remained,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Them I befriended, and their upright customs\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Made me disparage all the other sects.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*And ere I led the Greeks unto the rivers\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Of Thebes, in poetry, I was baptized,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*But out of fear was covertly a Christian,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*For a long time professing paganism;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*And this lukewarmness caused me the fourth circle\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*To circuit round more than four centuries.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Thou, therefore, who hast raised the covering\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*That hid from me whatever good I speak of,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*While in ascending we have time to spare,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Tell me, in what place is our friend Terentius,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Caecilius, Plautus, Varro, if thou knowest;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Tell me if they are damned, and in what alley.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“These, Persius and myself, and others many,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* Replied my Leader, \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“with that Grecian are\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Whom more than all the rest the Muses suckled,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*In the first circle of the prison blind;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Ofttimes we of the mountain hold discourse\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Which has our nurses ever with itself.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Euripides is with us, Antiphon,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Simonides, Agatho, and many other\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Greeks who of old their brows with laurel decked.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*There some of thine own people may be seen,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Antigone, Deiphile and Argia,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And there Ismene mournful as of old.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*There she is seen who pointed out Langia;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*There is Tiresias’ daughter, and there Thetis,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And there Deidamia with her sisters.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* Silent already were the poets both,
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* Attent once more in looking round about,
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* From the ascent and from the walls released;
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* And four handmaidens of the day already
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* Were left behind, and at the pole the fifth
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* Was pointing upward still its burning horn,
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* What time my Guide: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“I think that tow’rds the edge\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Our dexter shoulders it behoves us turn,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Circling the mount as we are wont to do.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* Thus in that region custom was our ensign;
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* And we resumed our way with less suspicion
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* For the assenting of that worthy soul
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* They in advance went on, and I alone
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* Behind them, and I listened to their speech,
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* Which gave me lessons in the art of song.
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* But soon their sweet discourses interrupted
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* A tree which midway in the road we found,
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* With apples sweet and grateful to the smell.
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* And even as a fir-tree tapers upward
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* From bough to bough, so downwardly did that;
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* I think in order that no one might climb it.
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* On that side where our pathway was enclosed
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* Fell from the lofty rock a limpid water,
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* And spread itself abroad upon the leaves.
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* The Poets twain unto the tree drew near,
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* And from among the foliage a voice
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* Cried: \zsay-s |who="A voice"\*“Of this food ye shall have scarcity.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* Then said: \zsay-s |who="A voice"\*“More thoughtful Mary was of making\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* \zsay-s |who="A voice"\*The marriage feast complete and honourable,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* \zsay-s |who="A voice"\*Than of her mouth which now for you responds;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* \zsay-s |who="A voice"\*And for their drink the ancient Roman women\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 146 \vp 146\vp* \zsay-s |who="A voice"\*With water were content; and Daniel\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 147 \vp 147\vp* \zsay-s |who="A voice"\*Disparaged food, and understanding won.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 148 \vp 148\vp* \zsay-s |who="A voice"\*The primal age was beautiful as gold;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 149 \vp 149\vp* \zsay-s |who="A voice"\*Acorns it made with hunger savorous,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 150 \vp 150\vp* \zsay-s |who="A voice"\*And nectar every rivulet with thirst.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 151 \vp 151\vp* \zsay-s |who="A voice"\*Honey and locusts were the aliments\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 152 \vp 152\vp* \zsay-s |who="A voice"\*That fed the Baptist in the wilderness;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 153 \vp 153\vp* \zsay-s |who="A voice"\*Whence he is glorious, and so magnified\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 154 \vp 154\vp* \zsay-s |who="A voice"\*As by the Evangel is revealed to you.”\zsay-e\*
\c 23
\cp 23
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* The while among the verdant leaves mine eyes
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* I riveted, as he is wont to do
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* Who wastes his life pursuing little birds,
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* My more than Father said unto me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Son,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Come now; because the time that is ordained us\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*More usefully should be apportioned out.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* I turned my face and no less soon my steps
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Unto the Sages, who were speaking so
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* They made the going of no cost to me;
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* And lo! were heard a song and a lament,
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*“Labia mea, Domine,”\zsay-e\* in fashion
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* Such that delight and dolence it brought forth.
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“O my sweet Father, what is this I hear?”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* Began I; and he answered: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Shades that go\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Perhaps the knot unloosing of their debt.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* In the same way that thoughtful pilgrims do,
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* Who, unknown people on the road o’ertaking,
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* Turn themselves round to them, and do not stop,
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* Even thus, behind us with a swifter motion
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* Coming and passing onward, gazed upon us
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* A crowd of spirits silent and devout.
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* Each in his eyes was dark and cavernous,
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* Pallid in face, and so emaciate
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* That from the bones the skin did shape itself.
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* I do not think that so to merest rind
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* Could Erisichthon have been withered up
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* By famine, when most fear he had of it.
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* Thinking within myself I said: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Behold,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*This is the folk who lost Jerusalem,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*When Mary made a prey of her own son.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* Their sockets were like rings without the gems;
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* Whoever in the face of men reads ‘omo’
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* Might well in these have recognised the ‘m.’
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* Who would believe the odour of an apple,
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* Begetting longing, could consume them so,
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* And that of water, without knowing how?
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* I still was wondering what so famished them,
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* For the occasion not yet manifest
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* Of their emaciation and sad squalor;
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* And lo! from out the hollow of his head
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* His eyes a shade turned on me, and looked keenly;
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* Then cried aloud: \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*“What grace to me is this?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* Never should I have known him by his look;
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* But in his voice was evident to me
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* That which his aspect had suppressed within it.
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* This spark within me wholly re-enkindled
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* My recognition of his altered face,
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* And I recalled the features of Forese.
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*“Ah, do not look at this dry leprosy,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* Entreated he, \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*“which doth my skin discolour,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*Nor at default of flesh that I may have;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*But tell me truth of thee, and who are those\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*Two souls, that yonder make for thee an escort;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*Do not delay in speaking unto me.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“That face of thine, which dead I once bewept,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Gives me for weeping now no lesser grief,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* I answered him, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“beholding it so changed!\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*But tell me, for God’s sake, what thus denudes you?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Make me not speak while I am marvelling,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*For ill speaks he who’s full of other longings.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* And he to me: \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*“From the eternal council\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*Falls power into the water and the tree\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*Behind us left, whereby I grow so thin.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*All of this people who lamenting sing,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*For following beyond measure appetite\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*In hunger and thirst are here re-sanctified.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*Desire to eat and drink enkindles in us\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*The scent that issues from the apple-tree,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*And from the spray that sprinkles o’er the verdure;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*And not a single time alone, this ground\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*Encompassing, is refreshed our pain,—\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*I say our pain, and ought to say our solace,—\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*For the same wish doth lead us to the tree\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*Which led the Christ rejoicing to say ‘Eli,’\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*When with his veins he liberated us.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* And I to him: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Forese, from that day\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*When for a better life thou changedst worlds,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Up to this time five years have not rolled round.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*If sooner were the power exhausted in thee\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Of sinning more, than thee the hour surprised\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Of that good sorrow which to God reweds us,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*How hast thou come up hitherward already?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*I thought to find thee down there underneath,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Where time for time doth restitution make.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* And he to me: \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*“Thus speedily has led me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*To drink of the sweet wormwood of these torments,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*My Nella with her overflowing tears;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*She with her prayers devout and with her sighs\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*Has drawn me from the coast where one awaits,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*And from the other circles set me free.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*So much more dear and pleasing is to God\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*My little widow, whom so much I loved,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*As in good works she is the more alone;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*For the Barbagia of Sardinia\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*By far more modest in its women is\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*Than the Barbagia I have left her in.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*O brother sweet, what wilt thou have me say?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*A future time is in my sight already,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*To which this hour will not be very old,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*When from the pulpit shall be interdicted\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*To the unblushing womankind of Florence\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*To go about displaying breast and paps.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*What savages were e’er, what Saracens,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*Who stood in need, to make them covered go,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*Of spiritual or other discipline?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*But if the shameless women were assured\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*Of what swift Heaven prepares for them, already\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*Wide open would they have their mouths to howl;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*For if my foresight here deceive me not,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*They shall be sad ere he has bearded cheeks\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*Who now is hushed to sleep with lullaby.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*O brother, now no longer hide thee from me;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*See that not only I, but all these people\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*Are gazing there, where thou dost veil the sun.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* Whence I to him: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“If thou bring back to mind\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*What thou with me hast been and I with thee,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*The present memory will be grievous still.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Out of that life he turned me back who goes\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*In front of me, two days agone when round\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*The sister of him yonder showed herself,”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* And to the sun I pointed.  \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Through the deep\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Night of the truly dead has this one led me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*With this true flesh, that follows after him.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Thence his encouragements have led me up,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Ascending and still circling round the mount\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That you doth straighten, whom the world made crooked.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*He says that he will bear me company,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Till I shall be where Beatrice will be;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*There it behoves me to remain without him.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*This is Virgilius, who thus says to me,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* And him I pointed at; \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“the other is\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That shade for whom just now shook every slope\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Your realm, that from itself discharges him.”\zsay-e\*
\c 24
\cp 24
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* Nor speech the going, nor the going that
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Slackened; but talking we went bravely on,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* Even as a vessel urged by a good wind.
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* And shadows, that appeared things doubly dead,
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* From out the sepulchres of their eyes betrayed
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* Wonder at me, aware that I was living.
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* And I, continuing my colloquy,
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Said: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Peradventure he goes up more slowly\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Than he would do, for other people’s sake.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*But tell me, if thou knowest, where is Piccarda;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Tell me if any one of note I see\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Among this folk that gazes at me so.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*“My sister, who, ’twixt beautiful and good,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*I know not which was more, triumphs rejoicing\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*Already in her crown on high Olympus.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* So said he first, and then: \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*“’Tis not forbidden\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*To name each other here, so milked away\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*Is our resemblance by our dieting.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*This,”\zsay-e\* pointing with his finger, \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*“is Buonagiunta,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*Buonagiunta, of Lucca; and that face\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*Beyond him there, more peaked than the others,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*Has held the holy Church within his arms;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*From Tours was he, and purges by his fasting\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*Bolsena’s eels and the Vernaccia wine.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* He named me many others one by one;
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* And all contented seemed at being named,
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* So that for this I saw not one dark look.
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* I saw for hunger bite the empty air
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* Ubaldin dalla Pila, and Boniface,
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* Who with his crook had pastured many people.
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* I saw Messer Marchese, who had leisure
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* Once at Forli for drinking with less dryness,
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* And he was one who ne’er felt satisfied.
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* But as he does who scans, and then doth prize
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* One more than others, did I him of Lucca,
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* Who seemed to take most cognizance of me.
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* He murmured, and I know not what Gentucca
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* From that place heard I, where he felt the wound
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* Of justice, that doth macerate them so.
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“O soul,”\zsay-e\* I said, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“that seemest so desirous\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*To speak with me, do so that I may hear thee,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And with thy speech appease thyself and me.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonagiunta"\*“A maid is born, and wears not yet the veil,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* Began he, \zsay-s |who="Bonagiunta"\*“who to thee shall pleasant make\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonagiunta"\*My city, howsoever men may blame it.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonagiunta"\*Thou shalt go on thy way with this prevision;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonagiunta"\*If by my murmuring thou hast been deceived,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonagiunta"\*True things hereafter will declare it to thee.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonagiunta"\*But say if him I here behold, who forth\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonagiunta"\*Evoked the new-invented rhymes, beginning,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonagiunta"\*‘Ladies, that have intelligence of love?’”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* And I to him: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“One am I, who, whenever\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Love doth inspire me, note, and in that measure\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Which he within me dictates, singing go.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonagiunta"\*“O brother, now I see,”\zsay-e\* he said, \zsay-s |who="Bonagiunta"\*“the knot\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonagiunta"\*Which me, the Notary, and Guittone held\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonagiunta"\*Short of the sweet new style that now I hear.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonagiunta"\*I do perceive full clearly how your pens\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonagiunta"\*Go closely following after him who dictates,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonagiunta"\*Which with our own forsooth came not to pass;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonagiunta"\*And he who sets himself to go beyond,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bonagiunta"\*No difference sees from one style to another;”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* And as if satisfied, he held his peace.
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* Even as the birds, that winter tow’rds the Nile,
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* Sometimes into a phalanx form themselves,
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* Then fly in greater haste, and go in file;
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* In such wise all the people who were there,
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* Turning their faces, hurried on their steps,
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* Both by their leanness and their wishes light.
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* And as a man, who weary is with trotting,
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* Lets his companions onward go, and walks,
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* Until he vents the panting of his chest;
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* So did Forese let the holy flock
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* Pass by, and came with me behind it, saying,
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*“When will it be that I again shall see thee?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“How long,”\zsay-e\* I answered, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“I may live, I know not;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Yet my return will not so speedy be,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*But I shall sooner in desire arrive;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Because the place where I was set to live\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*From day to day of good is more depleted,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And unto dismal ruin seems ordained.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*“Now go,”\zsay-e\* he said, \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*“for him most guilty of it\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*At a beast’s tail behold I dragged along\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*Towards the valley where is no repentance.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*Faster at every step the beast is going,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*Increasing evermore until it smites him,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*And leaves the body vilely mutilated.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*Not long those wheels shall turn,”\zsay-e\* and he uplifted
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* His eyes to heaven, \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*“ere shall be clear to thee\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*That which my speech no farther can declare.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*Now stay behind; because the time so precious\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*Is in this kingdom, that I lose too much\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Forese Donati"\*By coming onward thus abreast with thee.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* As sometimes issues forth upon a gallop
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* A cavalier from out a troop that ride,
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* And seeks the honour of the first encounter,
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* So he with greater strides departed from us;
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* And on the road remained I with those two,
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* Who were such mighty marshals of the world.
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* And when before us he had gone so far
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* Mine eyes became to him such pursuivants
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* As was my understanding to his words,
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* Appeared to me with laden and living boughs
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* Another apple-tree, and not far distant,
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* From having but just then turned thitherward.
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* People I saw beneath it lift their hands,
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* And cry I know not what towards the leaves,
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* Like little children eager and deluded,
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* Who pray, and he they pray to doth not answer,
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* But, to make very keen their appetite,
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* Holds their desire aloft, and hides it not.
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* Then they departed as if undeceived;
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* And now we came unto the mighty tree
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* Which prayers and tears so manifold refuses.
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="A voice"\*“Pass farther onward without drawing near;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="A voice"\*The tree of which Eve ate is higher up,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="A voice"\*And out of that one has this tree been raised.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* Thus said I know not who among the branches;
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* Whereat Virgilius, Statius, and myself
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* Went crowding forward on the side that rises.
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="A voice"\*“Be mindful,”\zsay-e\* said he, \zsay-s |who="A voice"\*“of the accursed ones\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="A voice"\*Formed of the cloud-rack, who inebriate\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="A voice"\*Combated Theseus with their double breasts;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="A voice"\*And of the Jews who showed them soft in drinking,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="A voice"\*Whence Gideon would not have them for companions\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="A voice"\*When he tow’rds Midian the hills descended.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* Thus, closely pressed to one of the two borders,
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* On passed we, hearing sins of gluttony,
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* Followed forsooth by miserable gains;
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* Then set at large upon the lonely road,
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* A thousand steps and more we onward went,
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* In contemplation, each without a word.
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*“What go ye thinking thus, ye three alone?”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* Said suddenly a voice, whereat I started
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* As terrified and timid beasts are wont.
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* I raised my head to see who this might be,
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* And never in a furnace was there seen
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* Metals or glass so lucent and so red
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* As one I saw who said: \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*“If it may please you\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*To mount aloft, here it behoves you turn;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*This way goes he who goeth after peace.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* His aspect had bereft me of my sight,
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* So that I turned me back unto my Teachers,
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* Like one who goeth as his hearing guides him.
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* And as, the harbinger of early dawn,
\q1 \v 146 \vp 146\vp* The air of May doth move and breathe out fragrance,
\q1 \v 147 \vp 147\vp* Impregnate all with herbage and with flowers,
\b
\q1 \v 148 \vp 148\vp* So did I feel a breeze strike in the midst
\q1 \v 149 \vp 149\vp* My front, and felt the moving of the plumes
\q1 \v 150 \vp 150\vp* That breathed around an odour of ambrosia;
\b
\q1 \v 151 \vp 151\vp* And heard it said: \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*“Blessed are they whom grace\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 152 \vp 152\vp* \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*So much illumines, that the love of taste\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 153 \vp 153\vp* \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*Excites not in their breasts too great desire,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 154 \vp 154\vp* \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*Hungering at all times so far as is just.”\zsay-e\*
\c 25
\cp 25
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* Now was it the ascent no hindrance brooked,
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Because the sun had his meridian circle
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* To Taurus left, and night to Scorpio;
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* Wherefore as doth a man who tarries not,
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* But goes his way, whate’er to him appear,
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* If of necessity the sting transfix him,
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* In this wise did we enter through the gap,
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Taking the stairway, one before the other,
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* Which by its narrowness divides the climbers.
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* And as the little stork that lifts its wing
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* With a desire to fly, and does not venture
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* To leave the nest, and lets it downward droop,
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* Even such was I, with the desire of asking
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* Kindled and quenched, unto the motion coming
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* He makes who doth address himself to speak.
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* Not for our pace, though rapid it might be,
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* My father sweet forbore, but said: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Let fly\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The bow of speech thou to the barb hast drawn.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* With confidence I opened then my mouth,
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* And I began: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“How can one meagre grow\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*There where the need of nutriment applies not?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“If thou wouldst call to mind how Meleager\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Was wasted by the wasting of a brand,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*This would not,”\zsay-e\* said he, \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“be to thee so sour;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And wouldst thou think how at each tremulous motion\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Trembles within a mirror your own image;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That which seems hard would mellow seem to thee.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But that thou mayst content thee in thy wish\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Lo Statius here; and him I call and pray\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*He now will be the healer of thy wounds.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*“If I unfold to him the eternal vengeance,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* Responded Statius, \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*“where thou present art,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Be my excuse that I can naught deny thee.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* Then he began: \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*“Son, if these words of mine\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Thy mind doth contemplate and doth receive,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*They’ll be thy light unto the How thou sayest.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*The perfect blood, which never is drunk up\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Into the thirsty veins, and which remaineth\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Like food that from the table thou removest,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Takes in the heart for all the human members\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Virtue informative, as being that\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Which to be changed to them goes through the veins\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Again digest, descends it where ’tis better\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Silent to be than say; and then drops thence\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Upon another’s blood in natural vase.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*There one together with the other mingles,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*One to be passive meant, the other active\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*By reason of the perfect place it springs from;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*And being conjoined, begins to operate,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Coagulating first, then vivifying\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*What for its matter it had made consistent.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*The active virtue, being made a soul\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*As of a plant, (in so far different,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*This on the way is, that arrived already,)\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Then works so much, that now it moves and feels\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Like a sea-fungus, and then undertakes\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*To organize the powers whose seed it is.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Now, Son, dilates and now distends itself\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*The virtue from the generator’s heart,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Where nature is intent on all the members.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*But how from animal it man becomes\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Thou dost not see as yet; this is a point\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Which made a wiser man than thou once err\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*So far, that in his doctrine separate\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*He made the soul from possible intellect,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*For he no organ saw by this assumed.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Open thy breast unto the truth that’s coming,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*And know that, just as soon as in the foetus\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*The articulation of the brain is perfect,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*The primal Motor turns to it well pleased\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*At so great art of nature, and inspires\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*A spirit new with virtue all replete,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Which what it finds there active doth attract\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Into its substance, and becomes one soul,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Which lives, and feels, and on itself revolves.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*And that thou less may wonder at my word,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Behold the sun’s heat, which becometh wine,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Joined to the juice that from the vine distils.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Whenever Lachesis has no more thread,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*It separates from the flesh, and virtually\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Bears with itself the human and divine;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*The other faculties are voiceless all;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*The memory, the intelligence, and the will\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*In action far more vigorous than before.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Without a pause it falleth of itself\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*In marvellous way on one shore or the other;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*There of its roads it first is cognizant.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Soon as the place there circumscribeth it,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*The virtue informative rays round about,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*As, and as much as, in the living members.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*And even as the air, when full of rain,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*By alien rays that are therein reflected,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*With divers colours shows itself adorned,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*So there the neighbouring air doth shape itself\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Into that form which doth impress upon it\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Virtually the soul that has stood still.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*And then in manner of the little flame,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Which followeth the fire where’er it shifts,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*After the spirit followeth its new form.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Since afterwards it takes from this its semblance,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*It is called shade; and thence it organizes\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Thereafter every sense, even to the sight.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Thence is it that we speak, and thence we laugh;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*Thence is it that we form the tears and sighs,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*That on the mountain thou mayhap hast heard.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*According as impress us our desires\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*And other affections, so the shade is shaped,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Statius"\*And this is cause of what thou wonderest at.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* And now unto the last of all the circles
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* Had we arrived, and to the right hand turned,
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* And were attentive to another care.
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* There the embankment shoots forth flames of fire,
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* And upward doth the cornice breathe a blast
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* That drives them back, and from itself sequesters.
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* Hence we must needs go on the open side,
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* And one by one; and I did fear the fire
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* On this side, and on that the falling down.
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* My Leader said: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Along this place one ought\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*To keep upon the eyes a tightened rein,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Seeing that one so easily might err.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*“Summae Deus clementiae,”\zsay-e\* in the bosom
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* Of the great burning chanted then I heard,
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* Which made me no less eager to turn round;
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* And spirits saw I walking through the flame;
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* Wherefore I looked, to my own steps and theirs
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* Apportioning my sight from time to time.
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* After the close which to that hymn is made,
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* Aloud they shouted, \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*“Virum non cognosco;”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* Then recommenced the hymn with voices low.
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* This also ended, cried they: \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*“To the wood\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*Diana ran, and drove forth Helice\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*Therefrom, who had of Venus felt the poison.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* Then to their song returned they; then the wives
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* They shouted, and the husbands who were chaste.
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* As virtue and the marriage vow imposes.
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* And I believe that them this mode suffices,
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* For all the time the fire is burning them;
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* With such care is it needful, and such food,
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* That the last wound of all should be closed up.
\c 26
\cp 26
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* While on the brink thus one before the other
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* We went upon our way, oft the good Master
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* Said: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Take thou heed! suffice it that I warn thee.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* On the right shoulder smote me now the sun,
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* That, raying out, already the whole west
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* Changed from its azure aspect into white.
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* And with my shadow did I make the flame
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Appear more red; and even to such a sign
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* Shades saw I many, as they went, give heed.
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* This was the cause that gave them a beginning
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* To speak of me; and to themselves began they
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* To say: \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*“That seems not a factitious body!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* Then towards me, as far as they could come,
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* Came certain of them, always with regard
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* Not to step forth where they would not be burned.
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*“O thou who goest, not from being slower\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*But reverent perhaps, behind the others,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*Answer me, who in thirst and fire am burning.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*Nor to me only is thine answer needful;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*For all of these have greater thirst for it\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*Than for cold water Ethiop or Indian.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*Tell us how is it that thou makest thyself\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*A wall unto the sun, as if thou hadst not\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*Entered as yet into the net of death.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* Thus one of them addressed me, and I straight
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* Should have revealed myself, were I not bent
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* On other novelty that then appeared.
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* For through the middle of the burning road
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* There came a people face to face with these,
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* Which held me in suspense with gazing at them.
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* There see I hastening upon either side
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* Each of the shades, and kissing one another
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* Without a pause, content with brief salute.
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* Thus in the middle of their brown battalions
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* Muzzle to muzzle one ant meets another
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* Perchance to spy their journey or their fortune.
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* No sooner is the friendly greeting ended,
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* Or ever the first footstep passes onward,
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* Each one endeavours to outcry the other;
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* The new-come people: \zsay-s |who="The new-come souls"\*“Sodom and Gomorrah!”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* The rest: \zsay-s |who="The other souls"\*“Into the cow Pasiphae enters,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="The other souls"\*So that the bull unto her lust may run!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* Then as the cranes, that to Riphaean mountains
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* Might fly in part, and part towards the sands,
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* These of the frost, those of the sun avoidant,
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* One folk is going, and the other coming,
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* And weeping they return to their first songs,
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* And to the cry that most befitteth them;
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* And close to me approached, even as before,
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* The very same who had entreated me,
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* Attent to listen in their countenance.
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* I, who their inclination twice had seen,
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* Began: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“O souls secure in the possession,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Whene’er it may be, of a state of peace,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Neither unripe nor ripened have remained\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*My members upon earth, but here are with me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*With their own blood and their articulations.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*I go up here to be no longer blind;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*A Lady is above, who wins this grace,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Whereby the mortal through your world I bring.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*But as your greatest longing satisfied\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*May soon become, so that the Heaven may house you\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Which full of love is, and most amply spreads,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Tell me, that I again in books may write it,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Who are you, and what is that multitude\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Which goes upon its way behind your backs?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* Not otherwise with wonder is bewildered
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* The mountaineer, and staring round is dumb,
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* When rough and rustic to the town he goes,
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* Than every shade became in its appearance;
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* But when they of their stupor were disburdened,
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* Which in high hearts is quickly quieted,
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*“Blessed be thou, who of our border-lands,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* He recommenced who first had questioned us,
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*“Experience freightest for a better life.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*The folk that comes not with us have offended\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*In that for which once Caesar, triumphing,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*Heard himself called in contumely, ‘Queen.’\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*Therefore they separate, exclaiming, ‘Sodom!’\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*Themselves reproving, even as thou hast heard,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*And add unto their burning by their shame.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*Our own transgression was hermaphrodite;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*But because we observed not human law,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*Following like unto beasts our appetite,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*In our opprobrium by us is read,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*When we part company, the name of her\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*Who bestialized herself in bestial wood.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*Now knowest thou our acts, and what our crime was;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*Wouldst thou perchance by name know who we are,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*There is not time to tell, nor could I do it.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*Thy wish to know me shall in sooth be granted;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*I’m Guido Guinicelli, and now purge me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*Having repented ere the hour extreme.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* The same that in the sadness of Lycurgus
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* Two sons became, their mother re-beholding,
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* Such I became, but rise not to such height,
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* The moment I heard name himself the father
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* Of me and of my betters, who had ever
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* Practised the sweet and gracious rhymes of love;
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* And without speech and hearing thoughtfully
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* For a long time I went, beholding him,
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* Nor for the fire did I approach him nearer.
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* When I was fed with looking, utterly
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* Myself I offered ready for his service,
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* With affirmation that compels belief.
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* And he to me: \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*“Thou leavest footprints such\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*In me, from what I hear, and so distinct,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*Lethe cannot efface them, nor make dim.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*But if thy words just now the truth have sworn,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*Tell me what is the cause why thou displayest\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*In word and look that dear thou holdest me?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* And I to him: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Those dulcet lays of yours\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Which, long as shall endure our modern fashion,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Shall make for ever dear their very ink!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*“O brother,”\zsay-e\* said he, \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*“he whom I point out,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* And here he pointed at a spirit in front,
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*“Was of the mother tongue a better smith.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*Verses of love and proses of romance,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*He mastered all; and let the idiots talk,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*Who think the Lemosin surpasses him.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*To clamour more than truth they turn their faces,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*And in this way establish their opinion,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*Ere art or reason has by them been heard.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*Thus many ancients with Guittone did,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*From cry to cry still giving him applause,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*Until the truth has conquered with most persons.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*Now, if thou hast such ample privilege\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*’Tis granted thee to go unto the cloister\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*Wherein is Christ the abbot of the college,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*To him repeat for me a Paternoster,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*So far as needful to us of this world,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido Guinizelli"\*Where power of sinning is no longer ours.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* Then, to give place perchance to one behind,
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* Whom he had near, he vanished in the fire
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* As fish in water going to the bottom.
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* I moved a little tow’rds him pointed out,
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* And said that to his name my own desire
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* An honourable place was making ready.
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* He of his own free will began to say:
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* ‘Tan m’ abellis vostre cortes deman,
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* Que jeu nom’ puesc ni vueill a vos cobrire;
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* Jeu sui Arnaut, que plor e vai chantan;
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* Consiros vei la passada folor,
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* E vei jauzen lo jorn qu’ esper denan.
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* Ara vus prec per aquella valor,
\q1 \v 146 \vp 146\vp* Que vus condus al som de la scalina,
\q1 \v 147 \vp 147\vp* Sovenga vus a temprar ma dolor.’
\b
\q1 \v 148 \vp 148\vp* Then hid him in the fire that purifies them.
\c 27
\cp 27
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* As when he vibrates forth his earliest rays,
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* In regions where his Maker shed his blood,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* (The Ebro falling under lofty Libra,
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* And waters in the Ganges burnt with noon,)
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* So stood the Sun; hence was the day departing,
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* When the glad Angel of God appeared to us.
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* Outside the flame he stood upon the verge,
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* And chanted forth, \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*“Beati mundo corde,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* In voice by far more living than our own.
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* Then: \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*“No one farther goes, souls sanctified,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*If first the fire bite not; within it enter,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*And be not deaf unto the song beyond.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* When we were close beside him thus he said;
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* Wherefore e’en such became I, when I heard him,
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* As he is who is put into the grave.
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* Upon my clasped hands I straightened me,
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* Scanning the fire, and vividly recalling
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* The human bodies I had once seen burned.
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* Towards me turned themselves my good Conductors,
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* And unto me Virgilius said: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“My son,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Here may indeed be torment, but not death.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Remember thee, remember! and if I\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*On Geryon have safely guided thee,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*What shall I do now I am nearer God?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Believe for certain, shouldst thou stand a full\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Millennium in the bosom of this flame,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*It could not make thee bald a single hair.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And if perchance thou think that I deceive thee,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Draw near to it, and put it to the proof\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*With thine own hands upon thy garment’s hem.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Now lay aside, now lay aside all fear,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Turn hitherward, and onward come securely;”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* And I still motionless, and ’gainst my conscience!
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* Seeing me stand still motionless and stubborn,
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* Somewhat disturbed he said: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Now look thou, Son,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*’Twixt Beatrice and thee there is this wall.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* As at the name of Thisbe oped his lids
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* The dying Pyramus, and gazed upon her,
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* What time the mulberry became vermilion,
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* Even thus, my obduracy being softened,
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* I turned to my wise Guide, hearing the name
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* That in my memory evermore is welling.
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* Whereat he wagged his head, and said: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“How now?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Shall we stay on this side?”\zsay-e\* then smiled as one
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* Does at a child who’s vanquished by an apple.
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* Then into the fire in front of me he entered,
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* Beseeching Statius to come after me,
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* Who a long way before divided us.
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* When I was in it, into molten glass
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* I would have cast me to refresh myself,
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* So without measure was the burning there!
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* And my sweet Father, to encourage me,
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* Discoursing still of Beatrice went on,
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* Saying: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Her eyes I seem to see already!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* A voice, that on the other side was singing,
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* Directed us, and we, attent alone
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* On that, came forth where the ascent began.
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="A voice"\*“Venite, benedicti Patris mei,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* Sounded within a splendour, which was there
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* Such it o’ercame me, and I could not look.
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="A voice"\*“The sun departs,”\zsay-e\* it added, \zsay-s |who="A voice"\*“and night cometh;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="A voice"\*Tarry ye not, but onward urge your steps,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="A voice"\*So long as yet the west becomes not dark.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* Straight forward through the rock the path ascended
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* In such a way that I cut off the rays
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* Before me of the sun, that now was low.
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* And of few stairs we yet had made assay,
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* Ere by the vanished shadow the sun’s setting
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* Behind us we perceived, I and my Sages.
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* And ere in all its parts immeasurable
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* The horizon of one aspect had become,
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* And Night her boundless dispensation held,
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* Each of us of a stair had made his bed;
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* Because the nature of the mount took from us
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* The power of climbing, more than the delight.
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* Even as in ruminating passive grow
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* The goats, who have been swift and venturesome
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* Upon the mountain-tops ere they were fed,
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* Hushed in the shadow, while the sun is hot,
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* Watched by the herdsman, who upon his staff
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* Is leaning, and in leaning tendeth them;
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* And as the shepherd, lodging out of doors,
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* Passes the night beside his quiet flock,
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* Watching that no wild beast may scatter it,
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* Such at that hour were we, all three of us,
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* I like the goat, and like the herdsmen they,
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* Begirt on this side and on that by rocks.
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* Little could there be seen of things without;
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* But through that little I beheld the stars
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* More luminous and larger than their wont.
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* Thus ruminating, and beholding these,
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* Sleep seized upon me,—sleep, that oftentimes
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* Before a deed is done has tidings of it.
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* It was the hour, I think, when from the East
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* First on the mountain Citherea beamed,
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* Who with the fire of love seems always burning;
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* Youthful and beautiful in dreams methought
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* I saw a lady walking in a meadow,
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* Gathering flowers; and singing she was saying:
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Leah"\*“Know whosoever may my name demand\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Leah"\*That I am Leah, and go moving round\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Leah"\*My beauteous hands to make myself a garland.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Leah"\*To please me at the mirror, here I deck me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Leah"\*But never does my sister Rachel leave\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Leah"\*Her looking-glass, and sitteth all day long.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Leah"\*To see her beauteous eyes as eager is she,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Leah"\*As I am to adorn me with my hands;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Leah"\*Her, seeing, and me, doing satisfies.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* And now before the antelucan splendours
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* That unto pilgrims the more grateful rise,
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* As, home-returning, less remote they lodge,
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* The darkness fled away on every side,
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* And slumber with it; whereupon I rose,
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* Seeing already the great Masters risen.
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“That apple sweet, which through so many branches\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The care of mortals goeth in pursuit of,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*To-day shall put in peace thy hungerings.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* Speaking to me, Virgilius of such words
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* As these made use; and never were there guerdons
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* That could in pleasantness compare with these.
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* Such longing upon longing came upon me
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* To be above, that at each step thereafter
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* For flight I felt in me the pinions growing.
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* When underneath us was the stairway all
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* Run o’er, and we were on the highest step,
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* Virgilius fastened upon me his eyes,
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* And said: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“The temporal fire and the eternal,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Son, thou hast seen, and to a place art come\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Where of myself no farther I discern.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*By intellect and art I here have brought thee;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Take thine own pleasure for thy guide henceforth;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Beyond the steep ways and the narrow art thou.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Behold the sun, that shines upon thy forehead;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Behold the grass, the flowerets, and the shrubs\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Which of itself alone this land produces.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Until rejoicing come the beauteous eyes\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Which weeping caused me to come unto thee,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thou canst sit down, and thou canst walk among them.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Expect no more or word or sign from me;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Free and upright and sound is thy free-will,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And error were it not to do its bidding;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thee o’er thyself I therefore crown and mitre!”\zsay-e\*
\c 28
\cp 28
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* Eager already to search in and round
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* The heavenly forest, dense and living-green,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* Which tempered to the eyes the new-born day,
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* Withouten more delay I left the bank,
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* Taking the level country slowly, slowly
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* Over the soil that everywhere breathes fragrance.
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* A softly-breathing air, that no mutation
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Had in itself, upon the forehead smote me
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* No heavier blow than of a gentle wind,
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* Whereat the branches, lightly tremulous,
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* Did all of them bow downward toward that side
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* Where its first shadow casts the Holy Mountain;
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* Yet not from their upright direction swayed,
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* So that the little birds upon their tops
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* Should leave the practice of each art of theirs;
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* But with full ravishment the hours of prime,
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* Singing, received they in the midst of leaves,
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* That ever bore a burden to their rhymes,
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* Such as from branch to branch goes gathering on
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* Through the pine forest on the shore of Chiassi,
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* When Eolus unlooses the Sirocco.
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* Already my slow steps had carried me
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* Into the ancient wood so far, that I
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* Could not perceive where I had entered it.
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* And lo! my further course a stream cut off,
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* Which tow’rd the left hand with its little waves
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* Bent down the grass that on its margin sprang.
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* All waters that on earth most limpid are
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* Would seem to have within themselves some mixture
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* Compared with that which nothing doth conceal,
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* Although it moves on with a brown, brown current
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* Under the shade perpetual, that never
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* Ray of the sun lets in, nor of the moon.
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* With feet I stayed, and with mine eyes I passed
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* Beyond the rivulet, to look upon
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* The great variety of the fresh may.
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* And there appeared to me (even as appears
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* Suddenly something that doth turn aside
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* Through very wonder every other thought)
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* A lady all alone, who went along
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* Singing and culling floweret after floweret,
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* With which her pathway was all painted over.
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Ah, beauteous lady, who in rays of love\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Dost warm thyself, if I may trust to looks,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Which the heart’s witnesses are wont to be,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*May the desire come unto thee to draw\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Near to this river’s bank,”\zsay-e\* I said to her,
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“So much that I might hear what thou art singing.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Thou makest me remember where and what\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Proserpina that moment was when lost\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Her mother her, and she herself the Spring.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* As turns herself, with feet together pressed
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* And to the ground, a lady who is dancing,
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* And hardly puts one foot before the other,
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* On the vermilion and the yellow flowerets
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* She turned towards me, not in other wise
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* Than maiden who her modest eyes casts down;
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* And my entreaties made to be content,
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* So near approaching, that the dulcet sound
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* Came unto me together with its meaning
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* As soon as she was where the grasses are.
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* Bathed by the waters of the beauteous river,
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* To lift her eyes she granted me the boon.
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* I do not think there shone so great a light
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* Under the lids of Venus, when transfixed
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* By her own son, beyond his usual custom!
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* Erect upon the other bank she smiled,
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* Bearing full many colours in her hands,
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* Which that high land produces without seed.
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* Apart three paces did the river make us;
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* But Hellespont, where Xerxes passed across,
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* (A curb still to all human arrogance,)
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* More hatred from Leander did not suffer
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* For rolling between Sestos and Abydos,
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* Than that from me, because it oped not then.
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*“Ye are new-comers; and because I smile,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* Began she, \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*“peradventure, in this place\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*Elect to human nature for its nest,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*Some apprehension keeps you marvelling;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*But the psalm ‘Delectasti’ giveth light\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*Which has the power to uncloud your intellect.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*And thou who foremost art, and didst entreat me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*Speak, if thou wouldst hear more; for I came ready\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*To all thy questionings, as far as needful.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“The water,”\zsay-e\* said I, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“and the forest’s sound,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Are combating within me my new faith\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*In something which I heard opposed to this.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* Whence she: \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*“I will relate how from its cause\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*Proceedeth that which maketh thee to wonder,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*And purge away the cloud that smites upon thee.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*The Good Supreme, sole in itself delighting,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*Created man good, and this goodly place\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*Gave him as hansel of eternal peace.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*By his default short while he sojourned here;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*By his default to weeping and to toil\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*He changed his innocent laughter and sweet play.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*That the disturbance which below is made\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*By exhalations of the land and water,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*(Which far as may be follow after heat,)\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*Might not upon mankind wage any war,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*This mount ascended tow’rds the heaven so high,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*And is exempt, from there where it is locked.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*Now since the universal atmosphere\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*Turns in a circuit with the primal motion\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*Unless the circle is broken on some side,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*Upon this height, that all is disengaged\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*In living ether, doth this motion strike\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*And make the forest sound, for it is dense;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*And so much power the stricken plant possesses\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*That with its virtue it impregns the air,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*And this, revolving, scatters it around;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*And yonder earth, according as ’tis worthy\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*In self or in its clime, conceives and bears\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*Of divers qualities the divers trees;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*It should not seem a marvel then on earth,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*This being heard, whenever any plant\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*Without seed manifest there taketh root.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*And thou must know, this holy table-land\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*In which thou art is full of every seed,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*And fruit has in it never gathered there.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*The water which thou seest springs not from vein\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*Restored by vapour that the cold condenses,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*Like to a stream that gains or loses breath;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*But issues from a fountain safe and certain,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*Which by the will of God as much regains\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*As it discharges, open on two sides.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*Upon this side with virtue it descends,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*Which takes away all memory of sin;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*On that, of every good deed done restores it.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*Here Lethe, as upon the other side\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*Eunoe, it is called; and worketh not\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*If first on either side it be not tasted.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*This every other savour doth transcend;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*And notwithstanding slaked so far may be\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*Thy thirst, that I reveal to thee no more,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*I’ll give thee a corollary still in grace,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*Nor think my speech will be to thee less dear\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*If it spread out beyond my promise to thee.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*Those who in ancient times have feigned in song\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*The Age of Gold and its felicity,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*Dreamed of this place perhaps upon Parnassus.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*Here was the human race in innocence;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*Here evermore was Spring, and every fruit;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*This is the nectar of which each one speaks.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* Then backward did I turn me wholly round
\q1 \v 146 \vp 146\vp* Unto my Poets, and saw that with a smile
\q1 \v 147 \vp 147\vp* They had been listening to these closing words;
\b
\q1 \v 148 \vp 148\vp* Then to the beautiful lady turned mine eyes.
\c 29
\cp 29
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* Singing like unto an enamoured lady
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* She, with the ending of her words, continued:
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*“Beati quorum tecta sunt peccata.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* And even as Nymphs, that wandered all alone
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* Among the sylvan shadows, sedulous
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* One to avoid and one to see the sun,
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* She then against the stream moved onward, going
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Along the bank, and I abreast of her,
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* Her little steps with little steps attending.
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* Between her steps and mine were not a hundred,
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* When equally the margins gave a turn,
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* In such a way, that to the East I faced.
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* Nor even thus our way continued far
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* Before the lady wholly turned herself
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* Unto me, saying, \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*“Brother, look and listen!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* And lo! a sudden lustre ran across
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* On every side athwart the spacious forest,
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* Such that it made me doubt if it were lightning.
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* But since the lightning ceases as it comes,
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* And that continuing brightened more and more,
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* Within my thought I said, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“What thing is this?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* And a delicious melody there ran
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* Along the luminous air, whence holy zeal
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* Made me rebuke the hardihood of Eve;
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* For there where earth and heaven obedient were,
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* The woman only, and but just created,
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* Could not endure to stay ’neath any veil;
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* Underneath which had she devoutly stayed,
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* I sooner should have tasted those delights
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* Ineffable, and for a longer time.
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* While ’mid such manifold first-fruits I walked
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* Of the eternal pleasure all enrapt,
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* And still solicitous of more delights,
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* In front of us like an enkindled fire
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* Became the air beneath the verdant boughs,
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* And the sweet sound as singing now was heard.
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* O Virgins sacrosanct! if ever hunger,
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* Vigils, or cold for you I have endured,
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* The occasion spurs me their reward to claim!
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* Now Helicon must needs pour forth for me,
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* And with her choir Urania must assist me,
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* To put in verse things difficult to think.
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* A little farther on, seven trees of gold
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* In semblance the long space still intervening
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* Between ourselves and them did counterfeit;
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* But when I had approached so near to them
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* The common object, which the sense deceives,
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* Lost not by distance any of its marks,
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* The faculty that lends discourse to reason
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* Did apprehend that they were candlesticks,
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* And in the voices of the song \zsay-s |who="Voices"\*“Hosanna!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* Above them flamed the harness beautiful,
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* Far brighter than the moon in the serene
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* Of midnight, at the middle of her month.
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* I turned me round, with admiration filled,
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* To good Virgilius, and he answered me
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* With visage no less full of wonderment.
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* Then back I turned my face to those high things,
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* Which moved themselves towards us so sedately,
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* They had been distanced by new-wedded brides.
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* The lady chid me: \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*“Why dost thou burn only\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*So with affection for the living lights,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*And dost not look at what comes after them?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* Then saw I people, as behind their leaders,
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* Coming behind them, garmented in white,
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* And such a whiteness never was on earth.
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* The water on my left flank was resplendent,
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* And back to me reflected my left side,
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* E’en as a mirror, if I looked therein.
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* When I upon my margin had such post
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* That nothing but the stream divided us,
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* Better to see I gave my steps repose;
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* And I beheld the flamelets onward go,
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* Leaving behind themselves the air depicted,
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* And they of trailing pennons had the semblance,
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* So that it overhead remained distinct
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* With sevenfold lists, all of them of the colours
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* Whence the sun’s bow is made, and Delia’s girdle.
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* These standards to the rearward longer were
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* Than was my sight; and, as it seemed to me,
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* Ten paces were the outermost apart.
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* Under so fair a heaven as I describe
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* The four and twenty Elders, two by two,
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* Came on incoronate with flower-de-luce.
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* They all of them were singing: \zsay-s |who="The elders"\*“Blessed thou\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="The elders"\*Among the daughters of Adam art, and blessed\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="The elders"\*For evermore shall be thy loveliness.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* After the flowers and other tender grasses
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* In front of me upon the other margin
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* Were disencumbered of that race elect,
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* Even as in heaven star followeth after star,
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* There came close after them four animals,
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* Incoronate each one with verdant leaf.
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* Plumed with six wings was every one of them,
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* The plumage full of eyes; the eyes of Argus
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* If they were living would be such as these.
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* Reader! to trace their forms no more I waste
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* My rhymes; for other spendings press me so,
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* That I in this cannot be prodigal.
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* But read Ezekiel, who depicteth them
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* As he beheld them from the region cold
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* Coming with cloud, with whirlwind, and with fire;
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* And such as thou shalt find them in his pages,
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* Such were they here; saving that in their plumage
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* John is with me, and differeth from him.
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* The interval between these four contained
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* A chariot triumphal on two wheels,
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* Which by a Griffin’s neck came drawn along;
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* And upward he extended both his wings
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* Between the middle list and three and three,
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* So that he injured none by cleaving it.
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* So high they rose that they were lost to sight;
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* His limbs were gold, so far as he was bird,
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* And white the others with vermilion mingled.
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* Not only Rome with no such splendid car
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* E’er gladdened Africanus, or Augustus,
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* But poor to it that of the Sun would be,—
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* That of the Sun, which swerving was burnt up
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* At the importunate orison of Earth,
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* When Jove was so mysteriously just.
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* Three maidens at the right wheel in a circle
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* Came onward dancing; one so very red
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* That in the fire she hardly had been noted.
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* The second was as if her flesh and bones
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* Had all been fashioned out of emerald;
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* The third appeared as snow but newly fallen.
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* And now they seemed conducted by the white,
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* Now by the red, and from the song of her
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* The others took their step, or slow or swift.
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* Upon the left hand four made holiday
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* Vested in purple, following the measure
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* Of one of them with three eyes in her head.
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* In rear of all the group here treated of
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* Two old men I beheld, unlike in habit,
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* But like in gait, each dignified and grave.
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* One showed himself as one of the disciples
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* Of that supreme Hippocrates, whom nature
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* Made for the animals she holds most dear;
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* Contrary care the other manifested,
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* With sword so shining and so sharp, it caused
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* Terror to me on this side of the river.
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* Thereafter four I saw of humble aspect,
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* And behind all an aged man alone
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* Walking in sleep with countenance acute.
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* And like the foremost company these seven
\q1 \v 146 \vp 146\vp* Were habited; yet of the flower-de-luce
\q1 \v 147 \vp 147\vp* No garland round about the head they wore,
\b
\q1 \v 148 \vp 148\vp* But of the rose, and other flowers vermilion;
\q1 \v 149 \vp 149\vp* At little distance would the sight have sworn
\q1 \v 150 \vp 150\vp* That all were in a flame above their brows.
\b
\q1 \v 151 \vp 151\vp* And when the car was opposite to me
\q1 \v 152 \vp 152\vp* Thunder was heard; and all that folk august
\q1 \v 153 \vp 153\vp* Seemed to have further progress interdicted,
\b
\q1 \v 154 \vp 154\vp* There with the vanward ensigns standing still.
\c 30
\cp 30
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* When the Septentrion of the highest heaven
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* (Which never either setting knew or rising,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* Nor veil of other cloud than that of sin,
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* And which made every one therein aware
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* Of his own duty, as the lower makes
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* Whoever turns the helm to come to port)
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* Motionless halted, the veracious people,
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* That came at first between it and the Griffin,
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* Turned themselves to the car, as to their peace.
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* And one of them, as if by Heaven commissioned,
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* Singing, \zsay-s |who="An elder"\*“Veni, sponsa, de Libano”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* Shouted three times, and all the others after.
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* Even as the Blessed at the final summons
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* Shall rise up quickened each one from his cavern,
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* Uplifting light the reinvested flesh,
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* So upon that celestial chariot
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* A hundred rose ‘ad vocem tanti senis,’
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* Ministers and messengers of life eternal.
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* They all were saying, \zsay-s |who="The angels"\*“Benedictus qui venis,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* And, scattering flowers above and round about,
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* \zsay-s |who="The angels"\*“Manibus o date lilia plenis.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* Ere now have I beheld, as day began,
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* The eastern hemisphere all tinged with rose,
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* And the other heaven with fair serene adorned;
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* And the sun’s face, uprising, overshadowed
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* So that by tempering influence of vapours
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* For a long interval the eye sustained it;
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* Thus in the bosom of a cloud of flowers
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* Which from those hands angelical ascended,
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* And downward fell again inside and out,
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* Over her snow-white veil with olive cinct
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* Appeared a lady under a green mantle,
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* Vested in colour of the living flame.
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* And my own spirit, that already now
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* So long a time had been, that in her presence
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* Trembling with awe it had not stood abashed,
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* Without more knowledge having by mine eyes,
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* Through occult virtue that from her proceeded
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* Of ancient love the mighty influence felt.
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* As soon as on my vision smote the power
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* Sublime, that had already pierced me through
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* Ere from my boyhood I had yet come forth,
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* To the left hand I turned with that reliance
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* With which the little child runs to his mother,
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* When he has fear, or when he is afflicted,
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* To say unto Virgilius: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Not a drachm\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Of blood remains in me, that does not tremble;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*I know the traces of the ancient flame.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* But us Virgilius of himself deprived
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* Had left, Virgilius, sweetest of all fathers,
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* Virgilius, to whom I for safety gave me:
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* Nor whatsoever lost the ancient mother
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* Availed my cheeks now purified from dew,
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* That weeping they should not again be darkened.
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“Dante, because Virgilius has departed\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Do not weep yet, do not weep yet awhile;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*For by another sword thou need’st must weep.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* E’en as an admiral, who on poop and prow
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* Comes to behold the people that are working
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* In other ships, and cheers them to well-doing,
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* Upon the left hand border of the car,
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* When at the sound I turned of my own name,
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* Which of necessity is here recorded,
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* I saw the Lady, who erewhile appeared
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* Veiled underneath the angelic festival,
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* Direct her eyes to me across the river.
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* Although the veil, that from her head descended,
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* Encircled with the foliage of Minerva,
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* Did not permit her to appear distinctly,
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* In attitude still royally majestic
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* Continued she, like unto one who speaks,
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* And keeps his warmest utterance in reserve:
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“Look at me well; in sooth I’m Beatrice!\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*How didst thou deign to come unto the Mountain?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Didst thou not know that man is happy here?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* Mine eyes fell downward into the clear fountain,
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* But, seeing myself therein, I sought the grass,
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* So great a shame did weigh my forehead down.
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* As to the son the mother seems superb,
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* So she appeared to me; for somewhat bitter
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* Tasteth the savour of severe compassion.
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* Silent became she, and the Angels sang
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* Suddenly, \zsay-s |who="The angels"\*“In te, Domine, speravi:”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* But beyond ‘pedes meos’ did not pass.
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* Even as the snow among the living rafters
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* Upon the back of Italy congeals,
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* Blown on and drifted by Sclavonian winds,
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* And then, dissolving, trickles through itself
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* Whene’er the land that loses shadow breathes,
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* So that it seems a fire that melts a taper;
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* E’en thus was I without a tear or sigh,
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* Before the song of those who sing for ever
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* After the music of the eternal spheres.
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* But when I heard in their sweet melodies
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* Compassion for me, more than had they said,
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="The angels"\*“O wherefore, lady, dost thou thus upbraid him?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* The ice, that was about my heart congealed,
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* To air and water changed, and in my anguish
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* Through mouth and eyes came gushing from my breast.
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* She, on the right-hand border of the car
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* Still firmly standing, to those holy beings
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* Thus her discourse directed afterwards:
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“Ye keep your watch in the eternal day,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*So that nor night nor sleep can steal from you\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*One step the ages make upon their path;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Therefore my answer is with greater care,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*That he may hear me who is weeping yonder,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*So that the sin and dole be of one measure.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Not only by the work of those great wheels,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*That destine every seed unto some end,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*According as the stars are in conjunction,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*But by the largess of celestial graces,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Which have such lofty vapours for their rain\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*That near to them our sight approaches not,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Such had this man become in his new life\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Potentially, that every righteous habit\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Would have made admirable proof in him;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*But so much more malignant and more savage\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Becomes the land untilled and with bad seed,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The more good earthly vigour it possesses.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Some time did I sustain him with my look;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Revealing unto him my youthful eyes,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*I led him with me turned in the right way.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*As soon as ever of my second age\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*I was upon the threshold and changed life,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Himself from me he took and gave to others.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*When from the flesh to spirit I ascended,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And beauty and virtue were in me increased,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*I was to him less dear and less delightful;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And into ways untrue he turned his steps,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Pursuing the false images of good,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*That never any promises fulfil;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Nor prayer for inspiration me availed,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*By means of which in dreams and otherwise\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*I called him back, so little did he heed them.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*So low he fell, that all appliances\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*For his salvation were already short,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Save showing him the people of perdition.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*For this I visited the gates of death,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And unto him, who so far up has led him,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*My intercessions were with weeping borne.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*God’s lofty fiat would be violated,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*If Lethe should be passed, and if such viands\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Should tasted be, withouten any scot\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Of penitence, that gushes forth in tears.”\zsay-e\*
\c 31
\cp 31
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“O thou who art beyond the sacred river,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Turning to me the point of her discourse,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* That edgewise even had seemed to me so keen,
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* She recommenced, continuing without pause,
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“Say, say if this be true; to such a charge,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Thy own confession needs must be conjoined.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* My faculties were in so great confusion,
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* That the voice moved, but sooner was extinct
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* Than by its organs it was set at large.
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* Awhile she waited; then she said: \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“What thinkest?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Answer me; for the mournful memories\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*In thee not yet are by the waters injured.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* Confusion and dismay together mingled
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* Forced such a Yes! from out my mouth, that sight
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* Was needful to the understanding of it.
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* Even as a cross-bow breaks, when ’tis discharged
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* Too tensely drawn the bowstring and the bow,
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* And with less force the arrow hits the mark,
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* So I gave way beneath that heavy burden,
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* Outpouring in a torrent tears and sighs,
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* And the voice flagged upon its passage forth.
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* Whence she to me: \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“In those desires of mine\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Which led thee to the loving of that good,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Beyond which there is nothing to aspire to,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*What trenches lying traverse or what chains\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Didst thou discover, that of passing onward\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Thou shouldst have thus despoiled thee of the hope?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And what allurements or what vantages\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Upon the forehead of the others showed,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*That thou shouldst turn thy footsteps unto them?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* After the heaving of a bitter sigh,
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* Hardly had I the voice to make response,
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* And with fatigue my lips did fashion it.
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* Weeping I said: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“The things that present were\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*With their false pleasure turned aside my steps,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Soon as your countenance concealed itself.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* And she: \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“Shouldst thou be silent, or deny\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*What thou confessest, not less manifest\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Would be thy fault, by such a Judge ’tis known.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*But when from one’s own cheeks comes bursting forth\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The accusal of the sin, in our tribunal\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Against the edge the wheel doth turn itself.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*But still, that thou mayst feel a greater shame\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*For thy transgression, and another time\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Hearing the Sirens thou mayst be more strong,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Cast down the seed of weeping and attend;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*So shalt thou hear, how in an opposite way\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*My buried flesh should have directed thee.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Never to thee presented art or nature\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Pleasure so great as the fair limbs wherein\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*I was enclosed, which scattered are in earth.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And if the highest pleasure thus did fail thee\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*By reason of my death, what mortal thing\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Should then have drawn thee into its desire?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Thou oughtest verily at the first shaft\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Of things fallacious to have risen up\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*To follow me, who was no longer such.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Thou oughtest not to have stooped thy pinions downward\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*To wait for further blows, or little girl,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Or other vanity of such brief use.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The callow birdlet waits for two or three,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*But to the eyes of those already fledged,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*In vain the net is spread or shaft is shot.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* Even as children silent in their shame
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* Stand listening with their eyes upon the ground,
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* And conscious of their fault, and penitent;
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* So was I standing; and she said: \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“If thou\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*In hearing sufferest pain, lift up thy beard\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And thou shalt feel a greater pain in seeing.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* With less resistance is a robust holm
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* Uprooted, either by a native wind
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* Or else by that from regions of Iarbas,
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* Than I upraised at her command my chin;
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* And when she by the beard the face demanded,
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* Well I perceived the venom of her meaning.
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* And as my countenance was lifted up,
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* Mine eye perceived those creatures beautiful
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* Had rested from the strewing of the flowers;
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* And, still but little reassured, mine eyes
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* Saw Beatrice turned round towards the monster,
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* That is one person only in two natures.
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* Beneath her veil, beyond the margent green,
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* She seemed to me far more her ancient self
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* To excel, than others here, when she was here.
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* So pricked me then the thorn of penitence,
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* That of all other things the one which turned me
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* Most to its love became the most my foe.
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* Such self-conviction stung me at the heart
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* O’erpowered I fell, and what I then became
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* She knoweth who had furnished me the cause.
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* Then, when the heart restored my outward sense,
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* The lady I had found alone, above me
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* I saw, and she was saying, \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*“Hold me, hold me.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* Up to my throat she in the stream had drawn me,
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* And, dragging me behind her, she was moving
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* Upon the water lightly as a shuttle.
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* When I was near unto the blessed shore,
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Voices"\*“Asperges me,”\zsay-e\* I heard so sweetly sung,
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* Remember it I cannot, much less write it.
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* The beautiful lady opened wide her arms,
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* Embraced my head, and plunged me underneath,
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* Where I was forced to swallow of the water.
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* Then forth she drew me, and all dripping brought
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* Into the dance of the four beautiful,
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* And each one with her arm did cover me.
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* ‘We here are Nymphs, and in the Heaven are stars;
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* Ere Beatrice descended to the world,
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* We as her handmaids were appointed her.
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* We’ll lead thee to her eyes; but for the pleasant
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* Light that within them is, shall sharpen thine
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* The three beyond, who more profoundly look.’
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* Thus singing they began; and afterwards
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* Unto the Griffin’s breast they led me with them,
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* Where Beatrice was standing, turned towards us.
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="The four nymphs"\*“See that thou dost not spare thine eyes,”\zsay-e\* they said;
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="The four nymphs"\*“Before the emeralds have we stationed thee,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="The four nymphs"\*Whence Love aforetime drew for thee his weapons.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* A thousand longings, hotter than the flame,
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* Fastened mine eyes upon those eyes relucent,
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* That still upon the Griffin steadfast stayed.
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* As in a glass the sun, not otherwise
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* Within them was the twofold monster shining,
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* Now with the one, now with the other nature.
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* Think, Reader, if within myself I marvelled,
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* When I beheld the thing itself stand still,
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* And in its image it transformed itself.
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* While with amazement filled and jubilant,
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* My soul was tasting of the food, that while
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* It satisfies us makes us hunger for it,
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* Themselves revealing of the highest rank
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* In bearing, did the other three advance,
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* Singing to their angelic saraband.
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="The three nymphs"\*“Turn, Beatrice, O turn thy holy eyes,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* Such was their song, \zsay-s |who="The three nymphs"\*“unto thy faithful one,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="The three nymphs"\*Who has to see thee ta’en so many steps.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="The three nymphs"\*In grace do us the grace that thou unveil\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="The three nymphs"\*Thy face to him, so that he may discern\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="The three nymphs"\*The second beauty which thou dost conceal.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* O splendour of the living light eternal!
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* Who underneath the shadow of Parnassus
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* Has grown so pale, or drunk so at its cistern,
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* He would not seem to have his mind encumbered
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* Striving to paint thee as thou didst appear,
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* Where the harmonious heaven o’ershadowed thee,
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* When in the open air thou didst unveil?
\c 32
\cp 32
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* So steadfast and attentive were mine eyes
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* In satisfying their decennial thirst,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* That all my other senses were extinct,
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* And upon this side and on that they had
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* Walls of indifference, so the holy smile
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* Drew them unto itself with the old net
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* When forcibly my sight was turned away
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Towards my left hand by those goddesses,
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* Because I heard from them a \zsay-s |who="The three nymphs"\*“Too intently!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* And that condition of the sight which is
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* In eyes but lately smitten by the sun
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* Bereft me of my vision some short while;
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* But to the less when sight re-shaped itself,
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* I say the less in reference to the greater
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* Splendour from which perforce I had withdrawn,
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* I saw upon its right wing wheeled about
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* The glorious host returning with the sun
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* And with the sevenfold flames upon their faces.
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* As underneath its shields, to save itself,
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* A squadron turns, and with its banner wheels,
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* Before the whole thereof can change its front,
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* That soldiery of the celestial kingdom
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* Which marched in the advance had wholly passed us
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* Before the chariot had turned its pole.
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* Then to the wheels the maidens turned themselves,
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* And the Griffin moved his burden benedight,
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* But so that not a feather of him fluttered.
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* The lady fair who drew me through the ford
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* Followed with Statius and myself the wheel
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* Which made its orbit with the lesser arc.
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* So passing through the lofty forest, vacant
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* By fault of her who in the serpent trusted,
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* Angelic music made our steps keep time.
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* Perchance as great a space had in three flights
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* An arrow loosened from the string o’erpassed,
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* As we had moved when Beatrice descended.
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* I heard them murmur altogether, \zsay-s |who="The procession"\*“Adam!”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* Then circled they about a tree despoiled
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* Of blooms and other leafage on each bough.
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* Its tresses, which so much the more dilate
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* As higher they ascend, had been by Indians
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* Among their forests marvelled at for height.
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="The procession"\*“Blessed art thou, O Griffin, who dost not\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="The procession"\*Pluck with thy beak these branches sweet to taste,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="The procession"\*Since appetite by this was turned to evil.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* After this fashion round the tree robust
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* The others shouted; and the twofold creature:
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Griffin"\*“Thus is preserved the seed of all the just.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* And turning to the pole which he had dragged,
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* He drew it close beneath the widowed bough,
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* And what was of it unto it left bound.
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* In the same manner as our trees (when downward
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* Falls the great light, with that together mingled
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* Which after the celestial Lasca shines)
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* Begin to swell, and then renew themselves,
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* Each one with its own colour, ere the Sun
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* Harness his steeds beneath another star:
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* Less than of rose and more than violet
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* A hue disclosing, was renewed the tree
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* That had erewhile its boughs so desolate.
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* I never heard, nor here below is sung,
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* The hymn which afterward that people sang,
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* Nor did I bear the melody throughout.
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* Had I the power to paint how fell asleep
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* Those eyes compassionless, of Syrinx hearing,
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* Those eyes to which more watching cost so dear,
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* Even as a painter who from model paints
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* I would portray how I was lulled asleep;
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* He may, who well can picture drowsihood.
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* Therefore I pass to what time I awoke,
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* And say a splendour rent from me the veil
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* Of slumber, and a calling: \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*“Rise, what dost thou?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* As to behold the apple-tree in blossom
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* Which makes the Angels greedy for its fruit,
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* And keeps perpetual bridals in the Heaven,
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* Peter and John and James conducted were,
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* And, overcome, recovered at the word
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* By which still greater slumbers have been broken,
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* And saw their school diminished by the loss
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* Not only of Elias, but of Moses,
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* And the apparel of their Master changed;
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* So I revived, and saw that piteous one
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* Above me standing, who had been conductress
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* Aforetime of my steps beside the river,
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* And all in doubt I said, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Where’s Beatrice?”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* And she: \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*“Behold her seated underneath\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*The leafage new, upon the root of it.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*Behold the company that circles her;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*The rest behind the Griffin are ascending\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*With more melodious song, and more profound.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* And if her speech were more diffuse I know not,
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* Because already in my sight was she
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* Who from the hearing of aught else had shut me.
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* Alone she sat upon the very earth,
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* Left there as guardian of the chariot
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* Which I had seen the biform monster fasten.
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* Encircling her, a cloister made themselves
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* The seven Nymphs, with those lights in their hands
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* Which are secure from Aquilon and Auster.
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“Short while shalt thou be here a forester,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And thou shalt be with me for evermore\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*A citizen of that Rome where Christ is Roman.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Therefore, for that world’s good which liveth ill,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Fix on the car thine eyes, and what thou seest,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Having returned to earth, take heed thou write.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* Thus Beatrice; and I, who at the feet
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* Of her commandments all devoted was,
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* My mind and eyes directed where she willed.
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* Never descended with so swift a motion
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* Fire from a heavy cloud, when it is raining
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* From out the region which is most remote,
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* As I beheld the bird of Jove descend
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* Down through the tree, rending away the bark,
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* As well as blossoms and the foliage new,
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* And he with all his might the chariot smote,
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* Whereat it reeled, like vessel in a tempest
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* Tossed by the waves, now starboard and now larboard.
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* Thereafter saw I leap into the body
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* Of the triumphal vehicle a Fox,
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* That seemed unfed with any wholesome food.
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* But for his hideous sins upbraiding him,
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* My Lady put him to as swift a flight
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* As such a fleshless skeleton could bear.
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* Then by the way that it before had come,
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* Into the chariot’s chest I saw the Eagle
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* Descend, and leave it feathered with his plumes.
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* And such as issues from a heart that mourns,
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* A voice from Heaven there issued, and it said:
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="A voice"\*“My little bark, how badly art thou freighted!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* Methought, then, that the earth did yawn between
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* Both wheels, and I saw rise from it a Dragon,
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* Who through the chariot upward fixed his tail,
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* And as a wasp that draweth back its sting,
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* Drawing unto himself his tail malign,
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* Drew out the floor, and went his way rejoicing.
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* That which remained behind, even as with grass
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* A fertile region, with the feathers, offered
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* Perhaps with pure intention and benign,
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* Reclothed itself, and with them were reclothed
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* The pole and both the wheels so speedily,
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* A sigh doth longer keep the lips apart.
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* Transfigured thus the holy edifice
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* Thrust forward heads upon the parts of it,
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* Three on the pole and one at either corner.
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* The first were horned like oxen; but the four
\q1 \v 146 \vp 146\vp* Had but a single horn upon the forehead;
\q1 \v 147 \vp 147\vp* A monster such had never yet been seen!
\b
\q1 \v 148 \vp 148\vp* Firm as a rock upon a mountain high,
\q1 \v 149 \vp 149\vp* Seated upon it, there appeared to me
\q1 \v 150 \vp 150\vp* A shameless whore, with eyes swift glancing round,
\b
\q1 \v 151 \vp 151\vp* And, as if not to have her taken from him,
\q1 \v 152 \vp 152\vp* Upright beside her I beheld a giant;
\q1 \v 153 \vp 153\vp* And ever and anon they kissed each other.
\b
\q1 \v 154 \vp 154\vp* But because she her wanton, roving eye
\q1 \v 155 \vp 155\vp* Turned upon me, her angry paramour
\q1 \v 156 \vp 156\vp* Did scourge her from her head unto her feet.
\b
\q1 \v 157 \vp 157\vp* Then full of jealousy, and fierce with wrath,
\q1 \v 158 \vp 158\vp* He loosed the monster, and across the forest
\q1 \v 159 \vp 159\vp* Dragged it so far, he made of that alone
\b
\q1 \v 160 \vp 160\vp* A shield unto the whore and the strange beast.
\c 33
\cp 33
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* \zsay-s |who="The seven nymphs"\*“Deus venerunt gentes,”\zsay-e\* alternating
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Now three, now four, melodious psalmody
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* The maidens in the midst of tears began;
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* And Beatrice, compassionate and sighing,
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* Listened to them with such a countenance,
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* That scarce more changed was Mary at the cross.
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* But when the other virgins place had given
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* For her to speak, uprisen to her feet
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* With colour as of fire, she made response:
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“‘Modicum, et non videbitis me;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Et iterum,’ my sisters predilect,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*‘Modicum, et vos videbitis me.’”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* Then all the seven in front of her she placed;
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* And after her, by beckoning only, moved
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* Me and the lady and the sage who stayed.
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* So she moved onward; and I do not think
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* That her tenth step was placed upon the ground,
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* When with her eyes upon mine eyes she smote,
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* And with a tranquil aspect, \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“Come more quickly,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* To me she said, \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“that, if I speak with thee,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*To listen to me thou mayst be well placed.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* As soon as I was with her as I should be,
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* She said to me: \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“Why, brother, dost thou not\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Venture to question now, in coming with me?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* As unto those who are too reverential,
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* Speaking in presence of superiors,
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* Who drag no living utterance to their teeth,
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* It me befell, that without perfect sound
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* Began I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“My necessity, Madonna,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*You know, and that which thereunto is good.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* And she to me: \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“Of fear and bashfulness\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Henceforward I will have thee strip thyself,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*So that thou speak no more as one who dreams.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Know that the vessel which the serpent broke\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Was, and is not; but let him who is guilty\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Think that God’s vengeance does not fear a sop.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Without an heir shall not for ever be\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The Eagle that left his plumes upon the car,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Whence it became a monster, then a prey;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*For verily I see, and hence narrate it,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The stars already near to bring the time,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*From every hindrance safe, and every bar,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Within which a Five-hundred, Ten, and Five,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*One sent from God, shall slay the thievish woman\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And that same giant who is sinning with her.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And peradventure my dark utterance,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Like Themis and the Sphinx, may less persuade thee,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Since, in their mode, it clouds the intellect;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*But soon the facts shall be the Naiades\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Who shall this difficult enigma solve,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Without destruction of the flocks and harvests.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Note thou; and even as by me are uttered\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*These words, so teach them unto those who live\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*That life which is a running unto death;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And bear in mind, whene’er thou writest them,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Not to conceal what thou hast seen the plant,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*That twice already has been pillaged here.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Whoever pillages or shatters it,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*With blasphemy of deed offendeth God,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Who made it holy for his use alone.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*For biting that, in pain and in desire\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Five thousand years and more the first-born soul\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Craved Him, who punished in himself the bite.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Thy genius slumbers, if it deem it not\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*For special reason so pre-eminent\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*In height, and so inverted in its summit.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And if thy vain imaginings had not been\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Water of Elsa round about thy mind,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And Pyramus to the mulberry, their pleasure,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Thou by so many circumstances only\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*The justice of the interdict of God\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Morally in the tree wouldst recognize.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*But since I see thee in thine intellect\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Converted into stone and stained with sin,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*So that the light of my discourse doth daze thee,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*I will too, if not written, at least painted,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Thou bear it back within thee, for the reason\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*That cinct with palm the pilgrim’s staff is borne.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* And I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“As by a signet is the wax\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Which does not change the figure stamped upon it,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*My brain is now imprinted by yourself.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*But wherefore so beyond my power of sight\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Soars your desirable discourse, that aye\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*The more I strive, so much the more I lose it?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“That thou mayst recognize,”\zsay-e\* she said, \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“the school\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Which thou hast followed, and mayst see how far\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Its doctrine follows after my discourse,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And mayst behold your path from the divine\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Distant as far as separated is\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*From earth the heaven that highest hastens on.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* Whence her I answered: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“I do not remember\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That ever I estranged myself from you,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Nor have I conscience of it that reproves me.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“And if thou art not able to remember,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* Smiling she answered, \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“recollect thee now\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*That thou this very day hast drunk of Lethe;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*And if from smoke a fire may be inferred,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Such an oblivion clearly demonstrates\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Some error in thy will elsewhere intent.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Truly from this time forward shall my words\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Be naked, so far as it is befitting\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*To lay them open unto thy rude gaze.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* And more coruscant and with slower steps
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* The sun was holding the meridian circle,
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* Which, with the point of view, shifts here and there
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* When halted (as he cometh to a halt,
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* Who goes before a squadron as its escort,
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* If something new he find upon his way)
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* The ladies seven at a dark shadow’s edge,
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* Such as, beneath green leaves and branches black,
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* The Alp upon its frigid border wears.
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* In front of them the Tigris and Euphrates
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* Methought I saw forth issue from one fountain,
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* And slowly part, like friends, from one another.
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“O light, O glory of the human race!\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*What stream is this which here unfolds itself\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*From out one source, and from itself withdraws?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* For such a prayer, ’twas said unto me, \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“Pray\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Matilda that she tell thee;”\zsay-e\* and here answered,
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* As one does who doth free himself from blame,
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* The beautiful lady: \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*“This and other things\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*Were told to him by me; and sure I am\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*The water of Lethe has not hid them from him.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* And Beatrice: \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*“Perhaps a greater care,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Which oftentimes our memory takes away,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Has made the vision of his mind obscure.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*But Eunoe behold, that yonder rises;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Lead him to it, and, as thou art accustomed,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Beatrice"\*Revive again the half-dead virtue in him.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* Like gentle soul, that maketh no excuse,
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* But makes its own will of another’s will
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* As soon as by a sign it is disclosed,
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* Even so, when she had taken hold of me,
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* The beautiful lady moved, and unto Statius
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* Said, in her womanly manner, \zsay-s |who="Matelda"\*“Come with him.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* If, Reader, I possessed a longer space
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* For writing it, I yet would sing in part
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* Of the sweet draught that ne’er would satiate me;
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* But inasmuch as full are all the leaves
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* Made ready for this second canticle,
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* The curb of art no farther lets me go.
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* From the most holy water I returned
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* Regenerate, in the manner of new trees
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* That are renewed with a new foliage,
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* Pure and disposed to mount unto the stars.
